\id 1KI Original Douay-Rheims (1582/1610)
\usfm 3.0
\ide UTF-8
\h 3 Kings
\toc1 The Third Book of Kings, according to the Hebrews the First of Malachim. מלכים
\toc2 3 Kings
\toc3 1KI
\mt1 The Third Book of Kings, according to the Hebrews the First of Malachim. מלכים
\is THE ARGUMENT OF THE THIRD BOOKE OF KINGES.
\ip VVITH commemoration of King Dauids old age, of his appointing a ſucceſſour, and of his death, in \f - \ft The contentes of this booke, diuided into three partes.\f* the firſt and part of the ſecond chapters, this booke conteyneth two other principal partes: the former is of King Salomon; of his entrance to the Kingdom; his deuotion; wiſdom; magnificence; richeſſe; great familie; building of the Temple, and other ſumptuous palaces; of his fal alſo into luxurie, and idolatrie; in the reſt of the ſecond chapter to the end of the eleuenth. The other part sheweth the diuiſion of the Kingdom; onlie two tribes remayning to Roboam, Salomons ſonne, with title of King of Iuda; and tenne paſſing to Ieroboam his ſeruant, called King of Iſrael. So folow the ſeueral reignes of Abias, Aſa, and Ioſaphat Kinges of Iuda: and of Nadab, Baaſa, Ela, Zambri, Amri, Achab with Iezabel, and Ochoſias Kinges of Iſrael: with the preaching, miracles, and other actes of Abias, Elias, Eliſeus, and other Prophetes, in the other eleuen chapters.
\c 1
\cd King Dauid waxing old, Abiſag a Sunamite is brought to him. 5. Adonias pretending to reigne, 11. Nathan and Bethſabee obtaine, 28. that Salomon is declared and annointed King. 41. Wherupon Adonias (his folowers parting to their houſes) 50. fleeth to the altar in the tabernacle, but vpon promiſe of ſafetie doth homage to Salomon.
\p
\v 1 AND \f 1 \fr 1.1 \ft The firſt part. King David's admonitions to his ſon: and his death.\f* king Dauid was old, and had manie daies of age: and when he was couered with clothes, he was not warmed.
\v 2 His ſeruantes therfore ſayd to him: Let vs ſeeke for our lord the king a yong woman a virgin, and let her ſtand before the king, and cherishe him, and ſleepe in his boſome, and warme our lord the king.
\v 3 They ſought therfore a beautiful yong woman in al the coſtes of Iſrael, and they found Abiſag a Sunamite, and brought her to the king.
\v 4 And the damſel was exceding beautiful, and she ſlept with the king, and ſerued him, but the king did not know her.
\v 5 And Adonias the ſonne of Haggith was eleuated, ſaying: I wil reigne. And he made him ſelf chariotes and horſemen, and fiftie men, that should runne before him.
\v 6 Neither did his father controwle him at any time, ſaying: Why didſt thou this? And he alſo was very beautiful, the ſecond borne after Abſalom.
\v 7 And he had talke with Ioab the ſonne of Saruia, & with \f a \fr 1.7 \ft For this conſpiracie Abiathar was depoſed. \+it ch. 2. v. 27\+it*.\f* Abiathar the prieſt, who furthered Adonias ſide.
\v 8 But Sadoc the prieſt, and Banaias the ſonne of Ioiada, and Nathan the prophet, and Semei, and Rei, and the ſtrength of Dauids armie was not with Adonias.
\v 9 Adonias therfore hauing immolated rammes and calues, and al fatte beaſtes beſide the Stone zoheleth, which was nigh to the Fountaine Rogel, called al his brethren the ſonnes of the king, and al the men of Iuda the ſeruantes of the king:
\v 10 But Nathan the prophet, and Banaias, and al the ſtrong ones, and Salomon his brother he called not.
\v 11 Nathan therfore ſayd to Bethſabee the mother of Salomon: Haſt thou not heard that Adonias the ſonne of Haggith hath reigned, and our lord Dauid is ignorant therof?
\v 12 Now rherfore come, take counſel of me, and ſaue thy life, and thy ſonne Salomons.
\v 13 Goe, and enter in to king Dauid, and ſay to him: Didſt not thou my lord king ſweare to me thy handmayd, ſaying: Salomon thy ſonne shal reigne after me, and he shal ſitte in my throne? Why then reigneth Adonias?
\v 14 And whiles thou art yet ſpeaking there with the king, I wil come after thee, and make vp thy wordes.
\v 15 Bethſabee therfore went in to the king in the chamber: and the king was exceding old, and Abiſag the Sunamite miniſtred to him.
\v 16 Bethſabee bowed her ſelf, and adored the king. To whom the king ſayd: What is thy wil? quoth he.
\v 17 Who anſwering ſayd: My lord, thou didſt ſweare to thy handmayd by our Lord thy God, that Salomon thy ſonne shal reigne after me, and he shal ſitte in my throne.
\v 18 And behold now Adonias reigneth, thou my lord the king not knowing therof.
\v 19 He hath killed oxen, & al fatte thinges, and manie rammes, and called al the kinges ſonnes, Abiathar alſo the prieſt, and Ioab the General of the warfarre: but Salomon thy ſeruant he called not.
\v 20 Notwithſtanding my lord king, the eyes of al Iſrael looke vpon thee, that thou wouldeſt ſhew them, who shal ſitte in thy throne my lord king after thee.
\v 21 And it shal be when my lord king ſleepeth with his fathers, I and my ſonne Salomon shal be \f b \fr 1.21 \ft Baſely eſteemed, or puniſhed as offenders vpon ſuſpition or ieloſie.\f* ſinners.
\v 22 As she was yet ſpeaking with the king, Nathan the prophet came.
\v 23 And they told the king, ſaying: Nathan the prophete is here. And when he was gone in to the king, and had adored bowing to the earth,
\v 24 Nathan ſayd: My lord king, didſt thou ſay: Let Adonias reigne after me, and let him ſitte vpon my throne?
\v 25 Becauſe he is gone downe to day, and hath immolated oxen, and fattelinges, and manie rammes, and called al the kinges ſonnes, and the captaynes of the armie, Abiathar alſo the prieſt: and they eating and drinking before him, and ſaying: God ſaue the king Adonias:
\v 26 me thy ſeruant, and Sadoc the prieſt, and Banaias the ſonne of Ioiada, and Salomon thy ſeruant he called not.
\v 27 Is this word proceded from my lord the king, and haſt thou not told me thy ſeruant who ſhould ſitte vpon the throne of my lord the king after him?
\v 28 And king Dauid anſwered, ſaying: Cal vnto me Bethſabee. Who when she was entered in to the king, and ſtood before him,
\v 29 the king ſware, and ſayd: Our Lord liueth, which hath deliuered my ſoule from al diſtreſſe,
\v 30 that as I ſware to thee by our Lord the God of Iſrael, ſaying: Salomon thy ſonne shal reigne after me, and he shal ſitte vpon my throne for me, ſo wil I doe this day.
\v 31 And Bethſabee bowing her contenance vnto the earth adored the king, ſaying: God ſaue my lord for euer.
\v 32 King Dauid alſo ſaid: Cal me Sadoc the prieſt, and Nathan the prophet, & Banaias the ſonne of Ioiada. Who when they were entred in before the king,
\v 33 he ſaid to them: Take with you the ſeruantes of your lord, and ſette Salomon my ſonne vpon my mule: and bring him into Gihon.
\v 34 And let Sadoc the prieſt anoint him there, and Nathan the prophet to be king ouer Iſrael: and you shal ſound the trumpet, and shal ſay: God ſaue king Salomon.
\v 35 And you shal goe vp after him, and he shal come, and shal ſitte vpon my throne, and he shal reigne for me: and I wil ordaine him that he be prince ouer Iſrael, and ouer Iuda.
\v 36 And Banaias the ſonne of Ioiada anſwered the king, ſaying: Amen: ſo ſpeake our Lord the God of my lord the king.
\v 37 As our Lord hath beene with my lord the king, ſo be he with Salomon, and make his throne higher then the throne of my lord king Dauid.
\v 38 Sadoc therfore the prieſt, and Nathan the prophet went downe, and Banaias the ſonne of Ioiada, and Cherethi, and Phelethi: and they ſet Salomon vpon the mule of king Dauid, and brought him into Gihon.
\v 39 And Sadoc the prieſt tooke a horne of oile out of the tabernacle, and annointed Salomon: and they ſounded the trumpet, and al the people ſaid: God ſaue king Salomon.
\v 40 And al the multitude went vp after him, and the people ſinging on shaulmes, and reioyſing with great gladnes, and the earth ſounded of their crie.
\v 41 And Adonias heard, and al that were inuited of him, and the feaſt was ended: yea and Ioab hearing the voice of the trumpet, ſaid: What meaneth the crie of the citie making a tumult?
\v 42 As he yet ſpake, came Ionathas the ſonne of Abiathar the prieſt: to whom Adonias ſaid: Come in, becauſe thou art a ſtout man, and bringeſt good newes.
\v 43 And Ionathas anſwered Adonias: Not ſo: for our lord king Dauid hath appointed Salomon king.
\v 44 and hath ſent with him Sadoc the prieſt, and Nathan the prophete, and Banaias the ſonne of Ioiada, and Cerethi, and Phelethi, and they haue ſet him vpon the kinges mule.
\v 45 And Sadoc the prieſt, and Nathan the prophete haue annointed him king in Gihon: & they are gone vp thence reioyſing, and the citie ſounded: this is the voice that you heard.
\v 46 Yea and Salomon ſitteth vpon the throne of the kingdom.
\v 47 And the kinges ſeruantes entring in, haue bleſſed our lord king Dauid, ſaying: God amplify the name of Salomon aboue thy name, and magnifie his throne aboue thy throne. And the king \f c \fr 1.47 \ft King Dauid did not adore his ſonne as a ſubiect adoreth his prince, but adored God, geuing thankes for this benefite of a ſucceſſor. as it foloweth in rhe next verſe.\f* adored in his bed:
\v 48 and he hath thus ſpoken: Bleſſed be our Lord the God of Iſrael, who hath geuen this day one ſitting in my throne, mine eies ſeing it.
\v 49 They therfore were terrified, and they al aroſe, that had beene inuited of Adonias, and euery man went his way.
\v 50 And Adonias fearing Salomon aroſe, and went, and held the horne of the altar.
\v 51 And they told Salomon, ſaying: Behold Adonias fearing king Salomon, hath taken hold of the horne of the altar, ſaying: Let king Salomon ſweare to me this day, that he wil not kil his ſeruant with the ſword.
\v 52 And Salomon ſayd: If he be a good man, there shal not ſo much as one heare of his fal vpon the ground: but if euil shal be found in him, he shal die.
\v 53 King Salomon therfore ſent, and brought him out from the altar: and going in he adored king Salomon: and Salomon ſayd to him: Goe to thy houſe.
\c 2
\cd Dauid geueth godlie preceptes to Salomon. 5. willeth him to punish certaine offenders: 10. and dieth. 12. Salomon reigneth. 13. Adonias, by interceſſion of Bethſabee, requeſteth to haue Abiſag to wife: 22. but is put to death for demanding her. 26. Abiathar the prieſt is banished, and depoſed, for conſpiring with Adonias. 28. Ioab alſo for the ſame cauſe, and former crimes is ſlaine. 36. Semei is confined in Ieruſalem, 40. & for tranſgreſſing his limites, together with old faultes is likewiſe ſlaine.
\p
\v 1 AND the dayes of Dauid approched that he should die, and he commanded his ſonne Salomon, ſaying:
\v 2 I enter into the way of al flesh: take courage, and play the man.
\v 3 And obſerue the watches of our Lord thy God, that thou walke in his waies, and keepe his ceremonies, and his preceptes, and iudgementes, and teſtimonies, as is written in the law of Moyſes: that thou mayſt vnderſtand al thinges which thou doeſt, and whitherſoeuer thou shalt turne thy ſelfe:
\v 4 that our Lord may confirme his wordes, which he hath ſpoken of me, ſaying: If thy children shal keepe their waies, and shal walke before me in truth, in al their hart, and in al their ſoul, there shal not altogether be taken from thee a man out of the throne of Iſrael.
\v 5 Thou knoweſt alſo what Ioab the ſonne of Saruia hath done to me, what he did to the two princes of the armie of Iſrael, to Abner the ſonne of Ner, and Amaſa the ſonne of Iether: whom he ſlewe, & shed the bloud of warre in peace, & put the bloud of battel in his belt, which was about his loynes, and in his shoe, which was on his feete.
\v 6 Thou shalt doe therfore accotding to thy wiſdom, and shalt not bring his hoare head peaceably vnto hel.
\v 7 But to the ſonnes alſo of Berzellai the Galaadite thou shalt render kindnes, and they shal eate on thy table: for they mette me when I fled from the face of Abſolom thy brother.
\v 8 Thou haſt alſo with thee Semei the ſonne of Gera the ſonne of Iemini of Bahurim, who curſed me with a wicked curſe, when I went to the Campe; but becauſe he came downe to meete me when I paſſed Iordan, and I ſware to him by our Lord, ſaying: I wil not kil thee with the ſword:
\v 9 doe not thou ſuffer him to be guiltles. But thou art a wiſe man, ſo that thou knoweſt what thou shalt doe to him, and thou shalt bring his hoare heare with bloud vnto hel.
\v 10 Dauid therefore ſlept with his fathers, and was buried in the citie of Dauid.
\v 11 And the daies that Dauid reigned in Iſrael, are fourtie yeares: in Hebron he reigned ſeuen yeares, in Ieruſalem thirtie three.
\v 12 \f 1 \fr 2.12 \ft The ſecond part. Of Salomon's reign, and acts good and bad.\f* And Salomon ſate vpon the throne of Dauid his father, and his kingdome was confirmed excedingly.
\v 13 And Adonias the ſonne of Haggith entered in to Bethſabee the mother of Salomon. Who ſaid to him: Is thy coming peaceable? Who anſwered: peaceable.
\v 14 And he added: I haue a word to ſpeake with thee. To whom she ſaid: Speake. And he:
\v 15 Thou knoweſt, quoth he, that the kingdom was mine, and al Iſrael had purpoſed to make me ouer them to be their king: but the kingdom is tranſpoſed, & is made my brothers: for it was appointed him of our Lord.
\v 16 Now therefore I deſire one petition of thee; Confound not my face. Who ſaid to him: Speake.
\v 17 And he ſaid: I pray ſpeake to Salomon the king (for he can not denie thee any thing) that he geue me Abiſag the Sunamite to wife.
\v 18 And Bethſabee ſaid: wel, I wil ſpeake for thee to the king.
\v 19 Bethſabee therfore came to king Salomon, to ſpeake vnto him for Adonias: and the king aroſe to meete her, and adored her, and ſate downe vpon his throne: and a throne was ſette for the kinges mother, who ſate on his right hand.
\v 20 And she ſaid to him: One litle petition I deſire of thee, confound not my face. And the king ſaid to her: My mother aske: for \f d \fr 2.20 \ft In al conuenient ſuites it behoueth the ſonne to heare his mother.\f* it behoueth not that I turne away thy face.
\v 21 Who ſaid: Let Abiſag the Sunamite be geuen to Adonias thy brother to wife.
\v 22 And king Salomon anſwered, and ſaid to his mother: Why doeſt thou aske Abiſag the Sunamite for Adonias? aske for him alſo the kingdom: for he is my brother elder then I, & hath Abiathar the prieſt, & Ioab the ſonne of Saruia.
\v 23 Therfore king Salomon ſware by our Lord, ſaying: Theſe thinges doe God to me, and theſe adde he, becauſe Adonias hath ſpoken this word againſt his life.
\v 24 And now, our Lord liueth which hath eſtablished me, and placed me vpon the throne of Dauid my father, and which hath made me a houſe, as he ſpake, this day shal Adonias be ſlaine.
\v 25 And king Salomon ſent by the hand of Banaias the ſonne of Ioiada, who ſlewe him, and he died.
\v 26 To Abiathar alſo the prieſt the king ſaid: Goe into Anathoth to thy field, and thou in deede art a man of death: but to day I wil not kil thee, becauſe thou didſt carie the arke of our Lord God before Dauid my father, & haſt ſuſteyned labour in al thinges, wherein my father laboured.
\v 27 Salomon therefore \f e \fr 2.27 \ft By ſpecial inſtinct Salomon did this extraordinarie fact as a prophet & miniſter of God, executing his ſentence geuen before againſt the houſe of Heli for the ſinnes of his children, \+it 1. Reg. 2. v. 31\+it*. and for Abiathars proper fault ioyning with Adonias againſt Salomon \+it 3. Reg. 1\+it*.\f* caſt out Abiathar, that he should not be the prieſt of our Lord, that the word of our lord might be fulfilled, which he ſpake concerning the houſe of Heli in Silo.
\v 28 And then a meſſenger came to Ioab for that Ioab had turned after Adonias, and after Salomon had not turned: Ioab therfore fled into the tabernacle of our Lord, & caught the horne of the altar.
\v 29 And it was told king Salomon, that Ioab was fled into the tabernacle of our Lord, and was beſide the altar: & Salomon ſent Banaias the ſonne of Ioiada, ſaying: Goe, kil him.
\v 30 And Banaias came to the tabernacle of our Lord, and ſaid to him: Thus ſayth the king: Come forth. Who ſaid: I wil not come forth, but here wil I die. Banaias reported his word to the king, ſaying: Thus ſpake Ioab, and thus he anſwered me.
\v 31 And the king ſaid to him: Doe as he hath ſpoken: and kil him, & burie him, and thou shalt temoue the innocent bloud, which hath beene shed of Ioab, from me, and from the houſe of my father.
\v 32 And our Lord shal render his bloud vpon his head, becauſe he murdered two iuſt men, & better then him ſelf: and ſlew them with his ſword, my father Dauid not knowing, Abner the ſonne of Ner general of the warfare of Iſrael, and Amaſa the ſonne of Iether general of the armie of Iuda:
\v 33 and their bloud shal returne vpon the head of Ioab, and vpon the head of his ſeede for euer. But to Dauid and his ſeede and his houſe, and to his throne be peace for euer from our Lord.
\v 34 Banaias therfore the ſonne of Ioiada went vp, and ſetting vpon him ſlewe him: and he was buried in his houſe in the deſert.
\v 35 And the king appoynted Banaias the ſonne of Ioiada for him ouer the armie, and Sadoc the prieſt he \f f \fr 2.35 \ft Salomon was not only a king but alſo a prophet. Moreouer ſome ſecular princes doe nominate ſpiritual ſuperiours, and inſtal them in, their ſeates yet their iuriſdiction is not depending on the prince, but the prince to be directed by them. \+it Num. 27. v.21\+it*.\f* placed for Abiathar.
\v 36 The king alſo ſent, and called Semei, and ſaid to him: Build thee a houſe in Ieruſalem, and dwel there: and thou shalt not goe out thence hither and thither.
\v 37 But what day ſoeuer thou shalt goe out, and shalt paſſe the Torrent Cedron know that thou art to be ſlaine: thy bloud shal be vpon thy head.
\v 38 And Semei ſaid to the king: The ſaying is good: as my lord the king hath ſpoken, ſo wil thy ſeruant doe. Semei therfore dwelt in Ieruſalem, manie dayes.
\v 39 And it came to paſſe after three yeares, that the ſeruantes of Semei fled to Achis the ſonne of Maacha the king of Geth: and it was told Semei that his ſeruantes were gone into Geth.
\v 40 And Semei aroſe, and ſadled his aſſe, and went to Achis into Geth to require his ſeruantes, and he brought them out of Geth.
\v 41 And it was told Salomon that Semei went into Geth out of Ieruſalem, and was returned.
\v 42 And ſending he called him, and ſaid to him: Did I not teſtifie to thee by our Lord, and told thee before: What day ſoeuer thou going out shalt paſſe hither & thither, know that thou shalt die? And thou didſt anſwere me: The ſaying is good, which I haue heard.
\v 43 Why then haſt thou not kept the oath of our Lord, and the precept that I commanded thee?
\v 44 And the king ſaid to Semei: Thou knoweſt al the euil, wherof thy hart is priuy to thy ſelfe, which thou didſt to Dauid my father: our Lord hath rendred thy malice vpon thy head:
\v 45 And king Salomon be bleſſed, and the throne of Dauid shal be ſtable before our Lord for euer.
\v 46 The king therfore commanded Banaias the ſonne of Ioiada: who going out, ſtroke him, and he died.
\c 3
\cd King Salomon marieth Pharaos ſiſter. 3. offereth Victims in high places. 5. admonished by God in his ſleepe to demand what he wil, he asketh wiſdom to gouerne his people. 10. which God granteth him, with much richeſſe alſo and glorie. 16. He decideth a controuerſie betwen two women contending about a liuing child and a dead.
\p
\v 1 THE kingdom therfore was eſtablished in the hand of Salomon, and he was ioyned in affinitie to Pharao the king of Ægypt: for he tooke his daughter, and brought her into the citie of Dauid, vntil he accomplished building his owne houſe, and the houſe of our Lord, and the wal of Ieruſalem round about.
\v 2 But yet the people immolated in the excelſes: for there was no temple built to the name of our Lord vntil that day.
\v 3 And Salomon loued our Lord, walking in the preceptes of Dauid his father, ſauing that he immolated in the excelſes, and burnt incenſe.
\v 4 He went therfore into Gabaon, to immolate there: for that was a verie great excelſe: a thouſand hoſtes for holocauſt did Salomon offer vpon that altar in Gabaon.
\v 5 And our Lord appeared to Salomon in a dreame by night, ſaying: Aske what thou wilt that I may geue it thee.
\v 6 And Salomon ſaid: Thou haſt done great mercie with thy ſeruant Dauid my father, euen as he walked in thy ſight in truth, and iuſtice, and a right hart with thee: for thou haſt kept thy great mercie, and haſt geuen him a ſonne ſitting vpon his throne, as it is this day.
\v 7 And now Lord God, thou haſt made thy ſeruant to reigne for Dauid my father: but I am a litle childe, and ignorant of my going out and coming in.
\v 8 And thy ſeruant is in the middes of the people, which thou haſt choſen, a people infinite, which can not be numbred and counted for the multitude.
\v 9 Thou shalt therfore geue to thy ſeruant a docible hart, that he may iudge the people, & diſcerne betwen good and euil. For who ſhal be able to iudge this people, this thy people great in number?
\v 10 The word therfore was liked before our Lord, that Salomon had asked ſuch a thing.
\v 11 And our Lord ſayd to Salomon: Becauſe thou haſt asked this thing, and haſt not deſired for thy ſelf manie dayes, nor riches, nor the liues of thine enemies, but haſt deſired wiſedom for thy ſelf to diſcerne iudgement:
\v 12 behold I haue done vnto thee according to thy wordes, & I haue geuen thee a wiſe hart and intelligent, inſo much that none before thee hath beene like thee, nor shal ariſe after thee.
\v 13 Yea and theſe thinges, which thou didſt not aske, I haue geuen thee: to witte, riches, and glorie, ſo that none hath beene like thee among the kinges al dayes hertofore.
\v 14 And if thou wilt walke in my wayes, and keepe my preceptes, and my commandements, as thy father walked, I wil make thy dayes long.
\v 15 Therfore Salomon awaked, and perceiued that it was a dreame: and when he was come to Ieruſalem, he ſtood before the arke of couenant of our Lord, and offered holocauſtes, and made pacifique victimes, and a great feaſt to al his ſeruantes.
\v 16 \f 1 \fr 3.16 \ft The Epiſtle on munday in the 4. weke of Lent.\f* Then came there two women harlottes to the king, and ſtood before him:
\v 17 of the which one ſaid: I beſech thee, my lord, I and this woman dwelt in one houſe, and I was deliuered of a childe beſide her in the chamber.
\v 18 And the third day, after that I was deliuered, she alſo was deliuered, and we were together, and no other perſon with vs in the houſe, except we two.
\v 19 And this womans childe died in the night. For ſleping she oppreſſed him.
\v 20 And ryſing in the dead tyme of the night, she tooke my childe from the ſide of me thy handmayd being aſlepe, and layed it in her boſome: and her childe that was dead, she put in my boſome.
\v 21 And when I was ryſen in the morning to geue my childe milke, he appeared dead: whom more diligently beholding when it was cleere day, I found that it was not mine which I bare.
\v 22 And the other woman anſwered: It is not ſo as thou ſayſt, but thy childe is dead, and mine liueth. On the contrarie part she ſayd: Thou lieſt: for my childe liueth, and thy childe is dead. And in this maner they ſtroue before the king.
\v 23 Then ſayd the king: This woman ſaith, My childe liueth, and thy childe is dead. And this hath anſwered, No, but thy childe is dead, and mine liueth.
\v 24 The king therfore ſaid: Bring me a ſword. And when they had brought a ſword before the King,
\v 25 Diuide, quoth he, the liuing child into two partes, and geue the halfe part to one, and halfe to the other.
\v 26 But the woman, whoſe childe was aliue, ſaid to the king (for her bowels were moued vpon her childe) I beſech thee my lord, geue her the childe aliue, and kil it not. On the contrarie part she ſayd: \f a \fr 3.26 \ft So heretikes not being able to proue that their ſynagogue is the true & permanent Church, would deſtroy the Catholique and ſo haue none at al.\f* be it neither mine, nor thine, but let it be diuided.
\v 27 The king anſwered, and ſaid: Geue vnto this woman the infant aliue, and let it not be killed for this is the mother therof.
\v 28 Al Iſrael therfore heard the iudgement that the king had iudged, and they feared the king, ſeing the wiſedom of God to be in him to doe iudgement.
\c 4
\cd Chiefe men of Salomons kingdom are recited by their names, and offices. 22. likewiſe the prouiſion of victuals for his houſe, 26. the number of his horſes, 29. his wiſedom excelleth al others. 32. he writte manie parables and verſes, and lernedly diſcourſed of al thinges.
\p
\v 1 AND king Salomon was reigning ouer al Iſrael:
\v 2 and theſe were the princes which he had: Azarias the ſonne of Sadoc the prieſt:
\v 3 Elihoreph, and Ahia the ſonnes of Siſa Scribes: Ioſaphat the ſonne of Ahilud, regiſter:
\v 4 Banaias the ſonne of Ioiada, ouer the armie: and Sadoc, and Abiathar prieſtes.
\v 5 Azarias the ſonne of Nathan, ouer them that aſſiſted the king: Zabud the ſonne of Nathan prieſt the kinges frend:
\v 6 and Ahizar gouernour of the houſe: and Adoniram the ſonne of Abda ouer the tributes.
\v 7 And Salomon had twelue gouernours ouer al Iſrael, which ſerued out victuals for the king and for his houſe: for euerie one miniſtred neceſſaries, eche man his moneth in the yeare.
\v 8 And theſe are their names: Benhur in mount Ephraim.
\v 9 Bendecar, in Macces, and in Salebim, and in Bethſames, and in Elon, and in Bethhanan.
\v 10 Benheſed in Aruboth: his was Socho, and al the land Epher.
\v 11 Benabinadab, whoſe was al Nepha Dor, had Tapheth the daughter of Salomon to wife.
\v 12 Bana the ſonne of Ahilud gouerned Thanach and Mageddo, and al Bethſan, which is beſide Sarthana vnder Iezrahel, from Bethſan vnto Abelmehula ouer againſt Iecmaan.
\v 13 Bengaber in Ramoth galaad: had Auothiair the ſonne of Manaſſes in Galaad, he was chiefe in al the countrie of Argob, which is in Baſan, three ſcore cities great and walled, which had braſen lockes.
\v 14 Ahinadab the ſonne of Addo was chiefe in Manaim.
\v 15 Achimaas in Nephthali: yea he alſo had Baſemath the daughter of Salomon in mariage.
\v 16 Baana the ſonne of Huſi, in Aſer, and in Baloth.
\v 17 Ioſaphat the ſonne of Pharue, in Iſacar.
\v 18 Semei the ſonne of Ela, in Beniamin.
\v 19 Gaber the ſonne of Vri, in the land of Galaad in the land of Sehon the king of the Ammorrheite, & of Og the king of Baſan, ouer al thinges that were in that land.
\v 20 Iuda and Iſrael innumereble, as the ſand of the ſea in multitude: eating, and drinking, and reioycing.
\v 21 And Salomon was in his dominion, hauing al the kingdomes with him from the riuer of the land of the Philiſthijms vnto the border of Ægypt: of them that offered him preſents, and ſerued him al the dayes of his life.
\v 22 And the prouiſion of Salomon was euerie day thirtie meaſures of floure, & three ſcore meaſures of meale,
\v 23 tenne fat oxen and twentie paſture fed, & a hundred rammes, beſide the veniſon of hartes, roes, and buffles, & fatted foule.
\v 24 For he poſſeſſed al the countrie, which was beyond the riuer, from Thapſa vnto Goza, and al the kinges of thoſe countries: and he had peace on euerie ſide round about.
\v 25 And Iuda and Iſrael dwelt without anie feare, euerie one vnder his vine, and vnder his figtree, from Dan vnto Berſabe al the dayes of Salomon.
\v 26 And Salomon had fourtie thouſand ſtalles of chariothorſes, and twelue thouſand for the ſaddle.
\v 27 And the foreſaide gouernours of the king fed them: yea and the neceſſaries of king Salomons table they gaue forth with great care in their time.
\v 28 Barley alſo and ſtrow for the horſes, and beaſtes, they brought to the place, where the king was, according as it was appointed them.
\v 29 God alſo gaue wiſdom to Salomon and prudence exceding much, and latitude of hart as the ſand that is in the ſea shore.
\v 30 And the wiſdom of Salomon paſſed the wiſdom of al them in the eaſt, and of the Ægyptians,
\v 31 and he was wiſer then al men: wiſer then Ethan the Ezralite, and Heman, and Chalcol, and Dorda the ſonnes of Mahol and he was renowmed in al nations round about.
\v 32 Salomon alſo ſpake \f a \fr 4.32 \ft Theſe bookes are not extant.\f* three thouſand parables: and his ſongues were a thouſand & fiue.
\v 33 And he diſputed of trees from the ceder, that is in Libanus, vnto the hyſſop which cometh out of the wal: and he diſcourſed of beaſtes, and foules, and creeping wormes, and fiſhes.
\v 34 And there came from al people to heare the wiſdom of Salomon, and from al the kinges of the earth, which heard his wiſdom.
\c 5
\cd Hiram king of Tyre granteth timber and workmen for building the Temple: Salomon allowing victuals, and paying wages. 13. the number of workmen and ouerſeers.
\p
\v 1 HIRAM alſo the king of Tyre ſent his ſeruantes to Salomon: for he heard that they had auointed him king for his father: becauſe Hiram had bene Dauids frend at al time.
\v 2 And Salomon ſent to Hiram, ſaying;
\v 3 Thou knoweſt the wil of Dauid my father, and that he could not build a houſe to the name of our Lord his God, becauſe of warres imminent round about, vntil our Lord put them vnder the ſole of his feete.
\v 4 But now our Lord my God hath geuen me reſt round about: and there is no ſatan, nor il rencounter.
\v 5 Wherfore I purpoſe to build a temple to the name of our Lord my God, as our Lord hath ſpoken to Dauid my father, ſaying: Thy ſonne, whom I wil geue for thee vpon thy throne, he shal build a houſe to my name.
\v 6 Command therfore that thy ſeruantes cut me downe cedres out of Libanus, and let my ſeruantes be with thy ſeruantes: and I wil geue thee the hire of thy ſeruantes whatſoeuer thou wilt aske, for thou knoweſt how there is not in my people a man that hath skil to hew wood as the Sidonians.
\v 7 When Hiram therfore had heard the wordes of Salomon, he reioyſed excedingly, and ſaid: Bleſſed be the Lord God this day, who hath geuen vnto Dauid a ſonne moſt wiſe ouer this people ſo great in number.
\v 8 And Hiram ſent to Salomon, ſaying: I haue heard whatſoeuer thou haſt willed me: I wil doe al thy wil in cedre trees, and firre trees.
\v 9 My ſeruantes shal bring them downe from Libanus to the ſea: and I wil put them in boates in the ſea, vnto the place, which thou shalt ſignifie to me; and wil land them there, and thou shalt take them: and thou shalt allow me neceſſaries, that there be meate geuen for my houſe.
\v 10 Therfore Hiram gaue Salomon cedre trees, and firre trees, according to al his wil.
\v 11 And Salomon allowed Hiram twentie thouſand cores of wheate, for prouiſion for his houſe, and twentie cores of moſt pure oile: theſe thinges did Salomon geue to Hiram euerie yeare.
\v 12 Our Lord alſo gaue wiſedom to Salomon, as he ſpake to him: & there was peace betwen Hiram & Salomon, and both made a league.
\v 13 And king Salomon choſe workmen out of al Iſrael, and the taxed number was of thirtie thouſand men.
\v 14 And he ſent them into Libanus, ten thouſand euerie moneth by courſe, ſo that two monethes they were in their houſes: and Adoniram was ouer this taxing.
\v 15 And Salomon had ſeuentie thouſand of them that caried burdens, and eightie thouſand hewers of ſtones in the mountaine:
\v 16 beſides the ouerſeers which were ouer euerie worke, in number three thouſand, and three hundred that commanded the people, and them that did the worke.
\v 17 And the king commanded, that they should take great ſtones, choſen ſtones for the foundation of the temple, and should ſquare them:
\v 18 which the maſons of Salomon, and maſons of Hiram hewed: moreouer the Giblians prepared timber and ſtones, to build the houſe.
\c 6
\cd In the yeare foure hundred and foure ſcore after the Iſraelites came from Ægypt, Salomon, the fourth yeare of his reigne, beginneth to build the Temple. 2. The principal partes with the greatnes, forme, and ornamentes thereof are deſcribed. 38. It is in building ſeuen yeares.
\p
\v 1 AND it came to paſſe in the foure hundred and foureſcore yeare of the coming forth of the children of Iſrael out of the Land of Ægypt, in the fourth yeare, the moneth Zio (that is the ſecond moneth) of the reigne of Salomon ouer Iſrael, he began to build a houſe to our Lord.
\v 2 And the houſe, which king Salomon built to our Lord, had threeſcore cubites in length, and twentie cubites in bredth, and thirtie cubites in height.
\v 3 And there was a porche before the temple of twentie cubites of length, according to the meaſure of the bredth of the temple: and it had ten cubites of bredth before the face of the temple.
\v 4 And he made in the temple \f 1 \fr 6.4 \ft \+it Narow without & broad within\+it*.\f* oblique windowes.
\v 5 And he built vpon the wal of the temple loftes round about, in the walles of the houſe round about the temple and the oracle, and he made ſides round about.
\v 6 The loft that was vnderneth, had fiue cubites of bredth, & the middle loft was of ſix cubites in bredth, and the third loft had ſeuen cubites of bredth. And he put beames in the houſe round about on the outſide, that they might not cleaue to the walles of the temple.
\v 7 And the houſe when it was built, was built of ſtones hewed and perfected: and hammer, and hachet, and al the toole of yron were \f a \fr 6.7 \ft In perfect people is neither noiſe of murmur in aduerſitie, nor of boaſting in proſperitie, but patience and humilitie with ſilence.\f* not heard in the houſe when it was built.
\v 8 The doore of the middle ſide was in the wal of the houſe on the right hand: and by wynding ſtaires they went vp into the middle rowme, and from the middle into the third.
\v 9 And he built the houſe, and finished it: he couered alſo the houſe with ſeelings of cedre trees.
\v 10 And he built a loft ouer al the houſe fiue cubites of height, and he couered the houſe with cedre timber.
\v 11 And the word of our Lord came to Salomon, ſaying:
\v 12 This houſe, which thou buildeſt, if thou wilt walke in my preceptes, and doe my iudgementes, and keepe al my commandementes, going in them, I wil eſtablish my word to thee, which I ſpake to Dauid thy father.
\v 13 And I wil dwel in the middes of the children of Iſrael, and wil not forſake my people Iſrael.
\v 14 Salomon therfore built the houſe, and finished it.
\v 15 And he built the walles of the houſe on the inſide, with cedre loftes, from the pauement of the houſe to the toppe of the walles, and to the roofes, he couered it with cedre trees on the inſide: and he couered the floore of the houſe with boordes of firre.
\v 16 And he built loftes of cedre timber of twentie cubites at the hinder part of the temple, from the pauement to the higher partes: and he made the inner houſe of the oracle to be \f b \fr 6.16 \ft \+it Holie of holies\+it* or moſt holie place.\f* Sanctum Sanctorum.
\v 17 Moreouer the temple it ſelf was fourtie cubites before the doores of the oracle.
\v 18 And al the houſe was couered within with ceder, hauing roundels, and the ioyntes therof coningly wrought and the engrauinges ſtanding out: al thinges were couered with bordes: \f c \fr 6.18 \ft Stones in buildinges, and bones in liuing bodies repreſent the ſtate of religious perſones: who being hidde in their Monaſteries and celles, ſo much the more fortifie the Church, by how much leſſe they appeare abrode. becauſe \+it their office is not to teach, but to mourne. S. Bernard. Ser. 64\+it*.\f* neither could there a ſtone appeare in the wal at al.
\v 19 And he made the oracle in the middes of the houſe, in the inner part, that he might put the arke of couenant of our Lord there.
\v 20 Moreouer the oracle had twentie cubites in length, and twentie cubites of bredth, and twentie cubites in height. And he couered and ſeeled it with moſt pure gold. and the altar alſo he decked with ceder.
\v 21 The houſe alſo before the oracle he couered with moſt pure gold, and faſtened on plates with nailes of gold.
\v 22 And there was nothing in the temple that was not couered with gold: yea and al the altar of the oracle he couered with gold.
\v 23 And he made in the oracle two cherubs of oliue trees, of ten cubites in height.
\v 24 One wing of a cherub of fiue cubites, and the other wing of a cherub fiue cubites: that is, hauing tenne cubites, from the end of one wing vnto the end of the other wing.
\v 25 Of ten cubites alſo was the ſecond cherub: in like meaſure, and the worke was one in both cherubs,
\v 26 that is to ſay, one cherub had the height of ten cubites, and in like maner the ſecond cherub.
\v 27 And he put the cherubs in the middes of the inner temple: and the cherubs extended their winges, and the one wing touched the wal, and the wing of the ſecond cherub touched the other wal: and the other winges in the middle part of the temple touched ech other.
\v 28 He couered alſo the cherubs with gold.
\v 29 And al the walles of the temple round about he graued with diuerſe engrauinges and caruing: & he made in them cherubs, and palme trees, and \f d \fr 6.29 \ft It is a clere caſe, that al carued & grauen pictures, or images were not vnlawful, but were religiouſly made & ſette in the holie Temple, for the more honour of God.\f* diuerſe pictures, as it were ſtanding out of the wal, and coming forth.
\v 30 Yea the pauement alſo of the houſe he couered, with gold within and without.
\v 31 And in the entrance of the oracle he made litle doores of the timber of oliuetrees, and fiue corner poſtes.
\v 32 And two doores of oliuetimber: and he graued in them pictures of Cherubs, and figures of Palme trees, and grauen workes ſtanding out very much; and he couered them with gold: and he couered as wel the cherubs as the palmetrees, and the other thinges with gold.
\v 33 And he made in the entrance of the temple poſtes of oliuetimber foure ſquare:
\v 34 and two doores of firre trees, one agaynſt an other: and either doore was duble, and ſo opened with folding leaues.
\v 35 And he graued cherubs, and palmetrees, and engrauinges appearing very much: and he couered al with golden plates in ſquare worke by rule.
\v 36 And he built the inner court with three rowes of ſtones polished, and one rowe of ceder timber.
\v 37 In the fourth yeare was the houſe of our Lord founded in the moneth of Zio:
\v 38 \x - \xt Mat. 22.\x* and in the eleuenth yeare in the moneth Bul (that is the eight moneth) the houſe was perfected in al the workes therof, and in al the implementes therof: and he was building it ſeuen yeares.
\c 7
\cd Salomons palace, 2. his houſe in the forreſt, 8. and the quenes houſe is built, 13. Two great braſen pillers: 23. a ſea (or lauer) 27. tenne braſen feete. 38. tenne leſſe lauatories, and other veſſels, and implementes pertaining to the Temple, adorned with images of Angels, and other creatures are further deſcribed. \f - \ft The beginning of the fifth age.\f*
\s1 THE CONTINUANCE OF THE CHURCH AND RELIGION IN THE FOURTH AGE: from the parting of Israel out of Aegypt, to the foundation of the Temple. The space of 480. years.
\ip We have seen already in the three first ages, or distinct times of the world, the beginning, \f - \ft Articles of faith, other points of religion, & state of the Church more expressed in this fourth age than before.\f* increase, and continuance of the Church and Religion of God, without interruption. Now in this fourth age, in which God gave his people a written Law, it is yet more evident, that the same faith and religion, not only continued but also was more expressed, and explicated; and the Church had more variety of Sacrifices, Sacraments, and other holy Rites, and Observances: & the states Ecclesiastical & Temporal more distinguished, and each of them, especially the Priestly and Levitical Hierarchy, more disposed in subordination: the civil government also under Dukes, Judges, and Kings, more distributed among superior and inferior officers than before.
\ip For first the principal point and ground of all religion, \it the belief in one God\it*, and his proper \f - \ft Belief in one God.\f* divine worship, is above all most strictly commanded, often repeated, diligently observed by the good; and severely punished in transgressors. To which end and purpose, after that God had singularly selected three more renowned Patriarchs, \it Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob\it*, preserving them by his special grace from idolatry, and from wicked ways of most peoples and nations, blessed their seed, not in the whole progeny of the two former, but in Jacob only, whom he otherwise named Israel, multiplying his children exceedingly, yea most of all (which was most marvelous) in hot persecution: then bringing them forth of the furnace of Aegypt, in his strong hand, as is recorded in the former age, at last his Divine Majesty delivered to them his perfect and eternal \f - \ft Divine laws.\f* Law, contained in two tables, distributed into ten precepts, teaching them their proper duties, \f - \ft Moral.\f* first towards himself their God and Lord, then towards each other. Adding moreover for the practice and execution thereof, other particular precepts of two sorts, to wit, \it Ceremonial\it* \f - \ft Ceremonial.\f* prescribing certain determinate manners and rites, in observing the commandments of the first table pertaining to God: and \it Judicial\it* laws directing in particular how to fulfill the \f - \ft Judicial.\f* commandments of the second table, concerning our duties towards our neighbours. So we see the whole law is nothing else, but to \it love God above all, and our neighbours as ourselves\it*. The manner of performing all, is \it to believe and hope in one only Lord God, honour and serve him alone\it*, who made all of nothing, conserveth all, will judge all, and render to all men as they deserve, and therefore fully to confirm this point, he beginneth his commandments with express prohibition of all false and imaginary gods, saying (Exod. 20. v. 3.) \it Thou shalt not have strange gods\it*, and after threats \f - \ft Only God to be served with divine honour.\f* to the transgressors, and recital of the other nine commandments, he concludeth (v. 23.) with repetition of the first, saying: \it You shall not make gods of silver, nor gods of gold shall you make to you\it*. The same is repeated and explained \it (Deut. 5.)\it* And in the next chapter Moyses exhorting the people saith: \it Hear Israel, the Lord our God is one Lord\it*. And God himself speaking again saith: \it (Exod. 23.) See ye that I am only, and there is no other God besides me.\it* The royal Prophet David (2. Reg. 22. and Psalm. 17.) \it who is God but our God?\it* and in sundry other places the same doctrine of one God is grounded, confirmed, and established.
\ip The Mystery of \it the B. Trinity\it*, or of three Divine Persons, is no less true and certain, than that \f - \ft The B. Trinity.\f* there is but one God, though not so manifest to reason, nor so expressly taught in the old Testament, yet believed then also, and often insinuated, where God is expressed by names of the plural number: as Elohim, Elim, Elohe, Saddai, Adonai, Tsebaoth: which import plurality of God, who is but one nature and substance. \it Distinction\it* also \it of Persons\it* in God is deduced \it (Exod. 33.)\it* God saying: \it I will call in the name of the Lord\it*. That is (as S. Augustine and other fathers expound it) \f - \ft \+it q. 154. in Exod.\+it*\f* the second Person by his grace maketh his servants to call upon God. More distinctly (Psal. 2.) \it The Lord said to me: Thou art my Son, I this day have begotten thee\it*. (Psalm. 109) \it The Lord said to my Lord\it*: that is, \it God the Father to God the Son\it*: who according to his divinity is the Lord of David, according to his humanity the son of David. The same King David maketh mention also of the \f - \ft \+it Mat. 22.\+it*\f* third Person, \it the Holy Ghost\it*, praying (Psalm. 50.) \it Thy Holy Spirit take not from me\it*. In the form of blessing the people. (Num. 6.) all three Persons seem to be understood in the name of our Lord thrice repeated; \it Our Lord\it* (the Father) \it bless thee and keep thee. Our Lord\it* (the son) \it shew his face to thee, and have mercy upon thee. Our Lord\it* (the Holy Ghost) \it turn his countenance unto thee, and give thee peace\it*.
\ip Of the \it Incarnation\it* of the Son of God, we have in this age many prophecies and figures. Moyses \f - \ft Incarnation of Christ.\f* evidently (Deut. 18.) foresheweth that after other Prophets \it Christ the Son of God\it* should come in flesh, and redeem mankind, as S. Peter teacheth (Act. 3.) Likewise in his Canticle, and Blessing of the tribes (Deut. 32. & 33.) he speaketh more expressly of \it Christ\it* and his Church, then of the Jews and their Synagogue. The star prophesied by Balaam (Num. 24.) foreshewed both to Jews and Gentiles, that Christ should subdue all nations. Josue both in name and office was a manifest figure of JESUS CHRIST. Also the Judges, and Kings, some in one thing, some in another, most especially King David & Salomon, were figures of our Lord & Saviour Jesus Christ. The brazen serpent (Num. 21.) signified \it Christ\it* to be \it crucified\it*, as himself expoundeth it (Joan. 3.) Briefly the whole Law was a Pedagogue, or conductor to bring men to Christ (Galat. 3.) and by him to know God and themselves: to wit, God omnipotent, all perfect, Creator of all, our Father, Redeemer, and Sanctifier: and man his chief earthly creature; though of himself weak and impotent, yea through sin miserable, yet in nature of free condition, endued with \it understanding\it*, to conceive, and discourse; and with \it freewill\it*, to choose or refuse what liketh or displeaseth him.
\ip For God appointing all creatures their offices, ingraffed in all other things invariable \f - \ft Freewill in Angels and men.\f* inclinations to perform the same, so that they could neither by virtue nor sin make their state better nor worse than it was created, but ordaining Angels and men to a higher end of eternal felicity, left their wills free to agree unto, or to resist his precepts, and counsels. Whereupon Angels cooperating with God's grace were confirmed in glory, and some revolting were eternally damned. Man also offending fell into damnable state, but through penance may be saved, if he cooperate with new grace of our Redeemer, which is in his choice to do, or omit. As when God gave his people meat in the desert (Exod. 16.) he so instructed them, how to receive it and use it, without force or compulsion, that he might \it prove them\it* (as himself speaketh) \it whether they would walk in his law or no\it*. And after making covenant with them (Exod. 19. Deut. 26.) required and accepted their voluntary consent: entering into formal contract or bargain between himself and them: he promising on the one party \it to make them his peculiar people, a priestly Kingdom, and a holy nation\it*: they on the other party promising loyalty, obedience and observation of his commandments, saying: \it All things that our Lord hath spoken we will do\it*. For which cause God's promises are conditional (Deut. 7.) \it if thou keep his judgements, God will keep his covenant to thee\it*.
\ip Again most plainly (Deut. 11.) \it Behold I set before your sight this day benediction and malediction\it*, and (Deut. 30.) \it I call for witnesses this day heaven and earth, that I have proposed to you life and death, blessing and cursing. Choose therefore life that thou mayest live\it*. In all which it is certain that God's promise being firm, man's will is variable, and so the event not necessary: which made Caleb hoping of victory to say (Josue. 14.) \it if perhaps our Lord be with me\it*. Neither doth God's \f - \ft Objection of \+it God's fore knowledge\+it* answered.\f* foreknowledge make the event necessary, for he seeth the effect in the cause, as it is voluntary or casual: yea God knoweth all before, and sometimes foretelleth things, which conditionally would happen, and in deed (the condition failing) come not to pass, as (1. Reg. 23.) God answered, \it that the men of Ceila would betray David\it* (meaning if he stayed there) which they did not; for he parted from thence.
\ip Yet is not man able by this his freedom, nor otherwise of himself, to do, nor so much as to \f - \ft Grace necessary.\f* think any good thing, but through God's mere mercy, and \it grace given him without\it* his \f - \ft \+it 2. Cor. 3.\+it*\f* \it deserving\it*, sufficient to all, and effectual to those that accept it. God also giveth particular grace for special functions; as (Levit. 8.) to Priests (Num. 11.) to seventy ancients, and (1. Reg. 10.) to King Saul.
\ip By which divine assistance the \it commandments of God are possible\it*, as himself avoucheth, saying: \f - \ft God's commandments possible to be kept.\f* (Deut. 30.) \it This commandment that I command thee this day is not above thee\it*. Again: \it I have set before thee life and good, death and evil, that thou mayest love God, walk in his ways, and keep his commandments\it*.
\ip \it Works\it* done by grace and freewill are good and commendable, Moyses so testifying (Deut. 14.) \it This is your wisdom and understanding before peoples\it*. Yea are \it meritorious\it*: and rewards are promised \f - \ft Good works. Meritorious.\f* for the same (Levit. 26.) and contrarywise punishments threatened to the transgressors. And Booz knowing reward to be due for well doing, prayed God to render to Ruth (ch. 2.) a full reward for her well deserving. The royal Prophet affirmeth (Psal. 18.) that \it in keeping God's precepts is much reward\it* and (Psal. 118.) professeth that he inclined his heart to keep them \it for reward\it*.
\ip Amongst other services of God, and means of men's salvation, \it external Sacrifice\it* is of the \f - \ft Diverse sorts of Sacrifices.\f* greatest. And therefore the manner of offering all sorts is at large prescribed in the Law, especially in the seven first Chapters of \it Leviticus\it*. The first and principal was \it Holocaust\it*, wherein all the \f - \ft Holocausts.\f* oblation was burned and consumed in the honour of God our Sovereign Lord. The second was \f - \ft For sin.\f* \it Sacrifice for sin\it*, according to the diversity of offences and persons, whereof part was burned, the other part remained to the Priests, except it were for the sins of Priests, or of the whole multitude (Levit. 4.) for then the Priests had no portion, but all was offered to God. The third was \it pacific\it* \f - \ft Pacific.\f* \it sacrifice\it*, either \it of thanksgiving\it* for benefits received, \it or to obtain God's favour\it* in all occurrent necessities, and good desires. And of both these sorts one part was consumed in God's honour, another part was the Priest's, the third was theirs that gave the oblation. In confirmation of these sacrifices God at first miraculously sent fire to burn them (Levit. 9.) whereof he had given \f - \ft Fire sent from God signifieth charity.\f* commandment before (Levit. 6.) that it should be conserved, and never extinguished; to teach us especially of the new Testament, that have the real Sacrifice, and very body of the former shadows and figures, to nourish and keep the fire of charity, not procured by our own power, but given by God, that it never cease, nor be extinguished in our hearts.
\ip Likewise in the same law of Moyses, besides \it Circumcision\it* instituted before (Gen. 17.) and here \f - \ft Sacraments.\f* confirmed and continued (Levit. 12. Josue. 5.) all \it hosts\it* and \it sacrifices for sin\it* (Levit. 4. 5. 6. and 7.) \it consecration of Priests\it*, (Levit. 8.) and the \it sacrifices adjoined\it* thereunto, also diverse other \it washings\it* and \it purifications\it* of legal uncleanness (Levit. 14. 15. 16. & 17.) were all \it Sacraments\it*; signifying \f - \ft \+it Alanus de Sacra. c. 9.\+it*\f* either first justification and remission of sin, or increase of grace, and purity; of which sort it is also probable that the \it Paschal lamb\it*, and \it Loaves of proposition\it* were sacraments (Exod. 12. 25.) which multitude S. Augustine comparing with ours of the new Testament, saith: \it The people\it* \f - \ft \+it li. de vera Religione c. 17.\+it*\f* \it bound with fear in the old law, was burdened with many Sacraments. For this was profitable to\it* \f - \ft Many more in the old Testament than in the new.\f* \it such men\it* (saith he) \it to make them desire the grace, foretold by the Prophets, which being come from the wisdom of God becoming Man, by whom we are called into freedom, a few most wholesome Sacraments are instituted, which hold the society of christian people under one God of a free multitude\it*. But as Christ's Sacraments are fewer in number, so they are more excellent in virtue. And to \f - \ft Christ's Sacraments more excellent.\f* most of these new, the former do answer as figures and shadows. So to our \it Baptism\it* answereth Circumcision, as S. Paul teacheth (Coloss. 1.) \it that Christians are circumcised in the circumcision of Christ, buried with him in Baptism\it*. To our holy \it Eucharist\it*, as it is a Sacrament, did answer the Paschal lamb, and Loaves of proposition, as also Manna, and blood of the Testament. It was \f - \ft Most of Christ's Sacraments prefigured in the old law but not all.\f* prophesied Psal. 18. \it Adore his foot stool\it*: as holy Fathers expound it. And as the same \it Eucharist\it* \f - \ft \+it S. Aug. in hunc Psalm ser. de verbis Domini. li. 17. civit. c. 20. li. 1. cont. advers. leg. c. 18. S. Cyril. li. 3. in Ioan. S. Leo ser. 8. de passione.\+it*\f* is a \it Sacrifice\it*, it was prefigured by all the old Sacrifices of the law of nature, and of Moyses: as S. Augustine, and S. Leo do prove; and prophesied (Psal. 19.) \it Be he mindful of all thy sacrifice &c.\it* To the sacrament of \it holy Orders\it* answered consecration of Priests. All the ablutions, purifications, cleansings, and oblations for sin, which in great part were both Sacraments and Sacrifices, answered to our Sacrament of \it Penance\it*, which was also prefigured by the second tables of the decalogue (Exod. 34.) More plainly foreshewed by example of particular confession of sins and satisfaction (Num. 5. 14. and 29.) Contrition also was no less required, as appeareth by the example of King David. 2. Reg. 24. Marriage in the old Testament, though not a sacrament yet signified the Sacrament of \it Marriage\it* among Christians. But the Sacrament of \it Confirmation\it* had not any so answerable a figure, in the old law, which brought not to perfection. Neither \it Extreme unction\it*, because the law gave not immediate entrance into the kingdom of heaven, which defects were signified by the high Priest's entering only once in the year into \it Sancta Sanctorum\it*, Levit. 16.
\ip Likewise touching practice of holy Rites; diverse uncleanness hindering participation of \f - \ft Some like impediments in use of holy Rites.\f* sacrifices, and conversation with other men (Levit. 14.) Degrees of consanguinity and affinity, hindering marriage (Levit. 18.) and sundry Irregularities excluding from the office of Priests (Levit. 21.) were \it figurative resemblances of sins and censures\it*, and of \it impediments to holy Orders\it*, and \it to Marriage\it*, in the new Testament.
\ip To the peculiar service of God pertained also \it the Tabernacle\it*, with the \it Propitiatory, Ark,\it* \f - \ft Tabernacle. Propitiatory, with appertinances.\f* \it Cherubims, Table\it* for loaves of proposition, \it Candlestick, Lamps, Altars\it* for Holocausts, & Incense, \it Vestments for Priests, a brazen laver\it*, & other vessels described Exod. 25. & seq. All which were kept & carried by the Levites, resting or marching in the midst of the camp. Nu. 2. 3. And when the Land of Chanaan was conquered, the same were fixed in Silo, Iosue 18. whither the people resorted at certain set times, and upon sundry occasions. From thence long after they took the Ark, and often upon diverse occasions removing it, made Oratories, or Chapels, wheresoever it rested, devotion increasing, and religious estimation of it in all Israel. 1. Reg. 4. 7. 10. Yea the infidel Philisthiims in Azotus seeing and feeling the virtue thereof, overthrowing their god Dagon, and themselves sore plagued, found it best for them to send the Ark home to the Israelites, not without costly and precious oblations (1. Reg. 5. & 6.) King David most specially honouring it (2. Reg. 6.) Who further considering that himself dwelt in a house of cedar, and the ark of God remained in the tabernacle covered with skins, intended to build a more excellent house for God. 2. Reg. 7. But his godly purpose was deferred by God's appointment, and his son King Salomon builded the famous Temple in Hierusalem. 3. Reg. 6.
\ip Which succeeding in place of the Tabernacle, each of them (one after the other) was the only \f - \ft The Tabernacle, and afterwards the Temple, the only place for Sacrifice.\f* ordinary place of Sacrifice. The law commanding (Levit. 17.) \it If any man of the house of Israel kill an oxe, or a sheep, or a goat\it* (to wit for Sacrifice, as S. Augustine, and other fathers expound it) \it and\it* \f - \ft \+it Quest. 56. in Levit.\+it*\f* \it offer it not at the door of the tabernacle\it*, (afterwards at the door of the Temple) \it he shall be guilty of blood, as if he had shed blood.\it* and \it so shall he perish out of the midst of his people\it*. Nevertheless \f - \ft Yet God sometimes dispensed therein.\f* upon occasions, and by special revelation sacrifice was lawfully offered in other places. For so in the time of the tabernacle, \it Samuel\it* the Prophet, \it offered Sacrifice in Masphath\it*. 1. Reg. 7. And the Prophet \it Elias offered Sacrifice without the Temple\it*, when he convinced the false prophets of Baal, 3. Reg. 18. whose fact (as S. Augustine noteth) the miracle sufficiently shewed to be done by \f - \ft \+it Ibidem.\+it*\f* God's dispensation.
\ip And as peculiar places were dedicated, so also special times were sanctified, and diverse feasts and festivities, partly ordained before (as the Sabbath Gen. 2. and Pasch Exod. 12.) were confirmed \f - \ft Feasts of the old law.\f* by the Law (Exod. 20. 23.) and others likewise instituted (Exod. 23. Levit. 23. Num. 28. 29. and Deut. 16.) with proper sacrifices for every sort. First and most general was the \it daily Sacrifice\it* of a lamb every day twice, at morning and evening (Exod. 29.) which was not properly a feast, but a sacred perpetual office in the tabernacle, and after in the temple. All the rest were festival days, in which it was not lawful ordinarily to do servile work. The first of these was \it the Sabbath\it*, that is the \f - \ft Eight sorts of feasts, besides the daily sacrifice.\f* seventh and last day of every week, which is our Saturday: Kept still solemnly by the Jews, even at this time, in all places where they dwell; but not by Christians, because the old Law is abrogated; and we keep the next day, which is \it Sunday, holy, by institution and tradition of the Church\it*. The second, \it Neomenia\it*, or new moon, in which day they always began the month; and twelve such months made a year, by the course of the moon; for by the course of the sun, the year containeth eleven days more, which in three years make above a month. And so every third year, and sometimes the second (for it happened seven times in nineteen years) had thirteen months: and was called \it Annus embolismalis\it*, being increased by means of those eleven days. The third feast \f - \ft \+it S. Beda de Embolismo. to. 1.\+it*\f* was \it Pasch\it*, or Phase, first instituted at the parting of the children of Israel out of Aegypt, in the full moon of the first month in the spring, in which the Paschal lamb was eaten, as is prescribed; Exod. 12. The fourth feast was \it Pentecost\it*, or first fruits, the fiftieth day after Pasch, when Moyses received the Law in mount Sinai. The fifth, \it the feast of Trumpets\it*, the first day of the seventh month, in grateful memory that a ram sticking by the horns, was offered in sacrifice by Abraham in place of Isaac. The sixth was \it the feast of Expiation\it*, the tenth day of the seventh month; wherein \it solemn\it* \f - \ft Prescribed fast from even to even.\f* \it fast\it* was also prescribed from evening of the ninth day to evening of the tenth, for remission of sins in general, besides particular sacrifices and satisfaction for every sin, whereof any man found himself guilty. The seventh was \it the feast of Tabernacles\it*, seven days together, beginning the fifteenth of the seventh month, in memory of God's special protection, when they remained in tabernacles, forty years in the desert. The eighth feast was of \it Assembly and Collection\it*, the next day after the foresaid seven, in commemoration of union in the people, and peaceable possession in the promised land. In this day general collection was made for necessary expenses in the public service of God.
\ip Moreover \it the seventh year\it* was as a Sabbath of rest (Levit. 25.) in which no land was plowed, no \f - \ft Seventh year of rest: and Jubilee year.\f* vines pruned, nor those fruits gathered that sprung without man's industry of the earth. Again, the fiftieth year was peculiarly made holy, and called \it the Jubilee,\it* or joyful year. In it all bondmen were set free; all inheritances amongst the Israelites, being for the time sold or otherwise alienated, returned to the former owners.
\ip Besides Sacrifices, Sacraments, holy places, holy times, and many other sacred things \f - \ft Other ceremonial observances.\f* belonging thereto; there were yet more \it Ceremonial Observances\it*, commanded by Moyses' law, as well pertaining to the service of God in that time, as signifying christian life and manners. So \f - \ft Clean and unclean.\f* certain beasts, birds, and fishes were reputed unclean (Levit. 11.) and God's people forbid to eat them; as also that they should not eat any blood at all, nor fat. Levi. 3. The reason of all which \f - \ft No blood to be eaten, nor fat.\f* was not, as though any creature were ill in nature, but partly to avoid idolatry, partly to exercise them in obedience, and temperance, and partly for that the same things signified vices and corruptions, from which Christians especially ought to refrain. Likewise Levit. 19. they were commanded not to sow their fields with two sorts of seed, nor to wear garments woven of two \f - \ft Not diverse seed in one field.\f* sorts of stuff, that they might be more distinguished from Infidels by external signs, and not \f - \ft No cloth of diverse matter.\f* only by Circumcision, but especially to teach Christians to practise simple innocency, and to avoid double and deceitful dealing.
\ip All which, and other \it precepts\it*, as well moral, as ceremonial and judicial, were most \it strictly commanded\it*; the observers blessed and \it rewarded\it*; and transgressors severely \it threatened\it* \f - \ft Strict commandment to keep all the Law.\f* \f - \ft The observers blessed and rewarded.\f* with great curses (Levit. 20. 26. Deut. 4. 27. 28.) and diverse actually \it punished\it*, Exod. 32. three \f - \ft Transgressors cursed and punished.\f* thousand slain for committing idolatry, Many swallowed up in the earth. (Num. 16.) descending quick into hell, and many more burned with fire from heaven, for making and favouring Schism. Yea by one means and other, all that were above twenty years of age, coming forth of Aegypt, except two only (Josue and Caleb) died in the desert, for the general murmur of the people. Num. 11. 14. 25. and 26. All Israel beaten in battle till one malefactor Achan was discovered and punished. Ios. 7. All the tribes were punished for suffering public idolatry in Dan: and Benjamin almost extirpate, for not punishing certain malefactors. Iudic. 20. And the whole people very often invaded and sore afflicted for their sins; as appeareth in the book of Judges. In particular also diverse were advanced and prospered for their virtue; as Josue, Caleb, Phinees, Samuel, David and others. Contrarywise Nadab and Abiu Priests were miraculously burnt for offering strange fire, Levit. 10. One stoned to death for gathering sticks on the sabbath day, Num. 15. King Saul deposed, for presuming to offer sacrifice, and not destroying Infidels (1. Reg. 13. 15.) and Oza, 2. Reg. 6. suddenly slain for touching the Ark of God, the Law forbidding under pain of death, Num. 1. v. 51. & 18. v. 7. that none should approach to holy office being not thereto orderly called.
\ip Of works also of \it Supererogation\it* (called counsels not precepts) we have examples in vows, \f - \ft Works of supererogation.\f* voluntarily made of things not commanded; the law prescribing what vows might be made, \f - \ft Vows.\f* and by whom. Num. 30. And Num. 6. \it a particular rule\it* was proposed to such as of their own accord, would embrace it, and a distinct name given them, to be called \it Nazarites\it*, that is, \it Separate or\it* \f - \ft Nazarites.\f* \it Sanctified\it*. In which state they were to remain either for a time, limited by themselves or their parents, or perpetually, if they so promised. Iudic. 13. 1. Reg. 1. For so far as their promise extended, they were strictly obliged to perform. Deut. 23. \it When thou hast vowed a vow to our Lord thy God, thou shalt not slack to pay it: because our Lord thy God will require it: and if thou delay, it shall be reputed to thee for sin. If thou wilt not promise, thou shalt be without\it* (this) \it sin. Pay thy vows unto the Highest\it*, Psal. 75. \it Vow ye, and render\it* (your vows) \it to our Lord your God\it*, Psal. 49. The \it Rechabites\it*, afterwards had a like \it rule\it* to the Nazarites; and the same perpetual (Hierem. 35.) \f - \ft Rechabites.\f* \it never to drink wine, nor to build nor dwell in houses, but in tabernacles, nor sow corn, nor plant vineyards\it*. Which rule though instituted by a man, yet the observation thereof was much commended and rewarded by God. v. 19. Such \it distinct state of religious persons, with other states of the Church\it* of Christ, were also prefigured (Levit. 11.) by the clean fishes, of three distinct \f - \ft Three sorts of Christians prefigured.\f* waters, as some holy Fathers do mystically expound that place. To wit, the clean fishes of the sea are the multitude of \it lay persons\it*, which are drawn out of the sea of this world, and happily \f - \ft Laity.\f* found good fishes in our Lord's net. Math. 13. The clean fishes of the rivers, are the good and fruitful \it Clergy men\it*, that water the whole earth, by teaching Christian doctrine, and \f - \ft Clergy.\f* ministering holy Sacraments, with other Rites, and Governing the whole Church. And the clean fishes of standing pools, are the \it Monastical persons\it*, living perpetually in Cloisters, where good \f - \ft Monks.\f* souls are always ready for our Lord's table, as S. Bernard teacheth. Much more the ancient \f - \ft \+it S. Ber. Ser. 1. de S. Andrea. S. Beda. to. 4. S. Aug. cont. Faust. S. Greg. in li. 1. Reg. et in Iob.\+it*\f* fathers, Saint Beda, Saint Gregory, Saint Augustin, and others explicate innumerable places of holy Scripture mystically; relying therein upon example of the new Testament so expounding the \f - \ft Holy scriptures explicated mystically.\f* old. Namely Saint Paul teaching (as before is noted) that the whole law was a pedagogue guiding men to Christ, and affirming that all things happened to the people of the old Testament in figure of the new.
\ip Leaving therefore to prosecute the same further, which would require a very great work, it may here suffice to give according to the literal sense, a brief view of certain other points of Religion, practised in this fourth age.
\ip Where it is clear, that as Jacob the Patriarch had foretold (Gen. 38.) that \it Abraham's, Isaac's\it*, and his own \it name\it* should be \it invocated\it*, so Moyses prayed God for his promise made to them, and for \f - \ft Invocation of Patriarchs.\f* their sake, to pardon the people, saying Exod. 32. \it Remember, o Lord, Abraham, Isaac, and Israel.\it*
\ip \it And our Lord was pacified, from doing the evil which he had spoken against his people\it*. His \f - \ft \+it S. Hiero. Ep. 12. ad Gauden.\+it*\f* divine providence so disposing, that he could be hindered, by such prayers, from that which he threatened. And whereas Moyses did not directly invocate the holy Patriarchs, as Christians \f - \ft Objections answered by holy Scriptures.\f* now call upon glorified Saints, to pray for them, the cause of difference is, for that now Saints seeing God, know in him, whatsoever pertaineth to their glory, which state none before Christ attained unto. Num. 35. v. 25. Deut. 4. v. 12. Again Protestants object, that for so much as God knoweth all our necessities, desires, dispositions, and whatsoever is in man, it is needless (say they) superfluous, and in vain, that Saints should commend our causes. To this we answer, that not only glorious Saints, but also mortal men by God's ordinance (by which nothing is done vainly) do such offices, as mediators between God and other men; for so Moyses \it told the words of the people to our Lord\it* (Exod. 19.) notwithstanding \it God's omniscience\it*, or knowledge of all things. Also God expressly commanded Job's friends to go to Job, promising to hear his prayer for them. As \f - \ft \+it Job. 42.\+it*\f* \f - \ft How Saints know men's prayers.\f* for Saints hearing or knowing our prayers made to them, though only God of himself and by his own power, seeth men's secret cogitations, and therefore is properly called \it the searcher of hearts\it* (1. Reg. 16.) yet God communicateth this power to Prophets, to see the secret thoughts of others; so Samuel knew the cogitations of Saul (1. Reg. 9. v. 20.) And Ahias, saw by revelation the coming of Jeroboam's wife to him in Silo (3. Reg. 14.) Much more God revealeth our present state and acts to \it glorified souls\it*; who \it are as Angels in heaven\it* (Math. 22.) \it and being secure of their own glory, are careful\it* (saith S. Cyprian) \it of our Salvation\it*. Neither is it derogation to God that Saints are \f - \ft \+it lib. de mortalitate.\+it*\f* \f - \ft Titles given to men in office, and to Saints.\f* honoured, and titles ascribed to them, of intercessors, mediators, and the like; for such titles are given to them not as to God, but by way of participation only. So \it Judges\it* are called \it gods\it* and \it saviours\it* (Exod. 21. Iudic. 3.) and \it Priests\it* called \it gods\it* (Exod. 21.) Praise given \it to God and Gedeon\it*.
\ip Iudic. 7. \it Protection\it* and \it adoration of Angels\it* is very frequent, Exod. 23. 31. Num. 22. Iosue. 5. \f - \ft Angels adored.\f*
\ip Iudic. 2. 6. 13. \it The names of the twelve sons of Israel\it* were graven in the two chief ornaments of the high Priest, in the \it Ephod and Rationale\it* (Exod. 28.) \it Manna\it* was not only reserved as a memory of God's singular benefit, but also honourably reposed as a \it Relic in a golden vessel\it*, and kept in the \f - \ft Relics.\f* Ark of God (Exod. 16. Heb. 9.) \it Joseph's bones\it* reserved and removed (Iosue. 24.) \it Images\it* of holy \f - \ft Images.\f* \it Cherubims\it* were made and set up together with the Ark and Propitiatory in the chief place of the Tabernacle, called \it Sancta Sanctorum\it* (Exod. 25.) An \it image also of a serpent\it* was made in brass for the health of those that were striken by serpents (Num. 21.) \it Images\it* also \it of lions and oxen\it* were made, and set under the foot of the laver (called a sea) in the Temple (3. Reg. 7.) The \it honour\it* done to any holy thing, namely \it to the Ark\it* (2. Reg. 6.) redounded to God's more honour, and all this so far from idolatry, that quite contrary, in presence of the Ark the idol Dagon fell to the ground, and broke in pieces. 1. Reg. 5.
\ip \it Exequies\it* for the dead with \it weeping and fasting\it* were then practised in the Church, as \f - \ft Exequies for the dead.\f* appeareth by the people's mourning for Aaron thirty days. Nu. 20. Also for Moyses (Deut. 34.) By the Gabaonites fasting \it seven days for Saul and his sons lately slain\it*. 1. Reg. 31. Likewise King David with all his court \it mourning weeping and fasting\it* for them. 2. Reg. 1. All which were to no purpose, if souls departed could not be relieved by such means. It moreover appeareth that the same royal Prophet believed diverse places to be in hell, when he said (Psal. 85.) \it Thou hast delivered my soul from the lower hell\it*, signifying plainly that there is \it a lower and a higher hell\it*: which higher the Church calleth \it Purgatory\it*: where souls suffer that pain in satisfaction for their sins, which \f - \ft Purgatory.\f* remaineth not satisfied before death, and is due after the guilt of sin is remitted, the law prescribing that besides restitution of damage, sacrifice should also be offered (Levit. 5. 6. 16.) And David was punished by the death of his child, 2. Reg. 12. and by the plague sent amongst his people, 2. Reg. 24. after his sins were remitted. He feared also punishment in the other world, yea two sorts, and therefore prayed to be delivered from both, saying: (Psal. 6.) \it Lord rebuke me not in thy fury, nor chastise me in thy wrath\it*. That is (saith S. Gregory) \it Strike me not with the\it* \f - \ft \+it To. 2. in sept. Ps. paenitent.\+it*\f* \it reprobate, nor afflict me with those, that are purged by the punishing flames\it*. And most expressly signifieth also a higher place called hell, saying (Psal. 15.) in the person of Christ to his Father: \it Thou shalt not leave my soul in hell\it*. From whence Christ delivered the holy Patriarchs, \f - \ft Limbus Patrum.\f* Prophets and, other perfect souls, resting without sensible pain, and brought them into heaven, whither before him none could enter. Which was also signified by the cities of refuge, whence \f - \ft No entrance into heaven before Christ.\f* none might depart to their proper country, \it till the death of the high Priest\it* (Num. 35.) and by \it Moyses\it* dying in the desert, and \it not entering into the promised land\it* over Jordan, Deut. 4. 31. & 34.
\ip Presupposing the \it general Resurrection\it* of all men (as a truth known by former traditions) King \f - \ft Resurrection.\f* David sheweth the difference of the wicked, and godly in that time, saying (Psal. 1.) \it The impious shall not rise again in judgement: nor sinners in the Councel of the just\it*. That is, the wicked shall not rise to joy and glory, as the just and godly shall do.
\ip \it Of general judgement\it* is more plainly prophesied, 1. Reg. 2. \it That our Lord shall judge the ends of\it* \f - \ft Judgement.\f* \it the earth\it*, not that David, nor Salomon, but Christ should reign in his militant Church, even \it to the ends of the earth\it*, and in fine judge the whole world. The same is confirmed Psal. 49. \it God will come manifestly, our God, and he will not keep silence. Fire shall burn forth in his sight\it*. Psal. 95. \it He shall judge the round world in equity, and the peoples in his truth\it*. Psal 96. \it Fire shall go before him, and shall inflame his enemies round about\it*. Again, the same royal Prophet (Psal. 48.) describeth the future and eternal state of the \it damned\it* saying: as \it sheep\it* (creatures unable to help themselves) \it they are put in hell, death shall feed upon them\it*. Of the blessed he addeth: \it And the just shall rule over\it* \f - \ft Eternal pain of the damned and glory of the blessed.\f* \it them in the morning\it*, that is, in the Resurrection, and Psal. 149. \it The Saints shall rejoice in glory, they shall be joyful in their beds\it* (in eternal rest.) \it The exaltations\it* (praises) \it of God in their throat, and two edged swords in their hands: to do revenge in the nations, punishments among the peoples. To bind their Kings in fetters, and their nobles in iron manacles. That they may do in them the judgement that is written: This glory is to all his Saints\it*. And much greater glory belongeth to \it Saints\it*: for this is but accidental, uttered according to vulgar capacity. The essential and perfect glory, which no eye \f - \ft \+it 1. Cor. 2.\+it*\f* hath seen, nor ear hath heard, nor heart can conceive, consisteth in seeing God. Among accidental glorious gifts, the four dowries of glorified bodies are especially prefigured: \it Impassibility\it* \f - \ft \+it 1. Cor. 15.\+it*\f* \f - \ft Four dowries of glorified bodies prefigured.\f* by \it the wood Setim\it*, whereof the Ark was made (Exod. 25.) \it Agility\it* and \it Penetrability\it* in some sort by David's quickness against Goliath, and his conveying of himself into Saul's camp and forth again (1. Reg. 17. & 26.) but a more plain figure of \it Clarity\it* was in Moyses' face (Exod. 34.) which by his conversation with God, became more glorious than mortal eyes were able to behold, glistering and shining as most splendent light through crystal, described as if his skin had been a clear \it horn\it*, appearing and spreading beams like the sun, proceeding from the beauty of his soul, so that none of all the people could look directly upon him, except he covered his face. \f - \ft \+it Catechis. Rom. p. 1. c. 12. q. 9.\+it*\f*
\ip Thus much concerning particular points of faith and religion. And it is no less evident, that the universal \it Church\it* and City of God still continued: yea was \it more visible\it* and conspicuous to the \f - \ft The Church more known to other nations than before.\f* whole world than before. First, by God's marvelous protection thereof in the desert, and famous victories and conquests of the land of Chanaan. And by the excellent laws given to this people, which all nations admired, and none had the like. Deut. 4. For in this fourth age, besides other laws and precepts, the spiritual and temporal States were more distinguished, and the \it Ecclesiastical\it* \f - \ft The Ecclesiastical and temporal states more distinguished.\f* \it Hierarchy\it* escpecially disposed in subordination of one supreme head, with inferior governers, each in their place and office, for edification of the whole body. For Moyses being chief ruler and conductor of the Israelites out of Aegypt, received and delivered to them the written Law (Exod.
\ip 20.) And for observation and conservation thereof by God's express appointment (Levit. 8.) consecrated \it Aaron the ordinary High Priest\it*, himself remaining still extraordinary Superior, also above Aaron. And after Aaron he consecrated in like manner his son \it Eleazar\it* high Priest, and \f - \ft Succession of High Priests.\f* successor to his father (Num. 20.) To whom succeeded others in this order (1. Paralip. 6.) \it Phinees, Abisue, Bocci, Ozi, Zacharias\it*, (otherwise 1. Reg. 1. called Heli) \it Meraioth, Amarias\it* (otherwise Achimelec, whom Saul slew, 1. Reg. 22.) \it Achitob\it* (otherwise Abiathar, who was deposed, 3. Reg. 2.) and \it Sadoc\it*, in whose time the Temple was founded.
\ip To these were adjoined other \it Priests\it*, also consecrated in a praescript form (Levit. 8.) and \it Levites\it* \f - \ft Distinction of offices in Priests & Levites.\f* ordained to assist in lower and distinct offices (Num. 3. & 4.) In the first degree \it the Caathites\it*, whose office was to carry the Sanctuary, and vessel thereof wrapped up by the Priests, but were forbid in pain of death, to touch them, or to see them. In the second degree \it the Gersonites\it*; who carried the curtains and covers of the Tabernacle, and vessel of the Altar. In the third degree the \it Merarites\it*; who carried the boards, bars, and pillars, with their feet, pins, cords, and other implements of the tabernacle; \it every one according to their office and burdens, Num. 4. v. ult.\it*
\ip But \it in the temporal state\it* and government \it Iosue\it* of the tribe of Ephraim \it succeeded to Moyses\it* \f - \ft Succession of temporal Princes interrupted.\f* (Num. 27. Deut. 3. & 34.) And after Iosue were diverse \it interruptions of succession\it*, with governers of diverse tribes, and \it change of government\it*, from \it Dukes\it* to \it Judges\it*, and from Judges to \it Kings\it*. For \f - \ft Dukes.\f* after Iosue's death the people being sore afflicted by invasions of Infidels, God raised certain special men, with title of \it Judges\it* to deliver and save them. First \it Othoniel\it* of the tribe of Juda; \f - \ft Judges.\f* then \it Aod\it* of Benjamin; after him \it Samgar\it* (the Scripture not signifying of what tribe) then \it Barach\it* with \it Debora\it* of Ephraim; \it Gedeon\it* of Manasses; \it Abimelech\it*, his base son, \it an usurper\it*; \it Thola\it* of Issachar; \it Jair, and Jephte\it* of Manasses; \it A besan\it* of Juda; \it Aialon\it* of Zebulon; \it Abdon\it* of Ephraim; \it Sampson\it* of Dan; and \it Heli\it*, who was also high Priest of Aaron's stock, otherwise called Zaraias (1. Paralip. 6.) and \it Samuel\it* also of the tribe of Levi a \it Prophet\it*. In his time the people demanding and urging to have \it a King, Saul\it* of the tribe \it of Benjamin\it* was anointed. 1. Reg. 10. But for \f - \ft Kings.\f* transgressing God's commandments, especially for exercising spiritual function without warrant (1. Reg. 13.) and not destroying idolaters (1. Reg. 15.) was deposed, and \it David\it* of the tribe \it of Juda\it* was anointed King, who after many great troubles possessed the whole Kingdom, and died in peace, leaving his son \it Salomon\it* invested and anointed King in his throne.
\ip The Church being thus established in distinct states and orders, albeit there were many \f - \ft Many sins & difficulties in the Church.\f* imperfections in all sorts of persons, and great sins committed, yet God so punished offenders, and chastised the whole people, that he still conserved, the greatest, or chief part, in true faith and religion. For whiles they were in the desert, they \it murmured\it* very often against God, and his \f - \ft Murmur.\f* ministers their Superiors. (Exod. 17. Num. 11. 14. 20. 21.) Many \it fell to idolatry\it* (Exod. 32.) \it Aaron\it* \f - \ft Idolatry.\f* \it not free from cooperating\it* in the people's sin. \it Nadab\it* and \it Abiu\it* Aaron's sons, and consecrated Priests, \it offered strange fire\it* (Levit. 10.) \it Core, Dathan\it* and \it Abiron\it*, with their complices \it made a great\it* \f - \ft Schism.\f* \it schism\it* (Num 16.) Many committed \it carnal fornication\it* with Infidels; and were thereby drawn to \f - \ft Carnal fornication cause of Idolatry.\f* \it spiritual\it* (Num. 25.) Of which and other like sins the Psalmist speaketh (Psal. 94.) exhorting his people \it not to harden their hearts, as in the desert their fathers had tempted God. Forty years was I offended\it* (saith God) \it with that generation and said: They always err in heart\it*. And therefore he sware in his wrath: that same generation should not enter into the promised land of Chanaan: but their children entered and possessed it. Num. 14. Iosue 3.
\ip Again, the people falling to idolatry and other sins, were afflicted and sore pressed by foreign \f - \ft The Church afflicted for sins, yet was still conserved.\f* enemies, but repenting were delivered and saved by certain captains called \it Judges\it* and \it Saviours\it* \f - \ft \+it Iudic. 3.\+it*\f*: as appeareth in the book of Judges. They had also tribulations by some of their own nation, for among the Judges one (called Abimelec) was a \it tyrannical usurper\it* (Iudic. 9.) \it Saul\it* their first King falling from God unjustly \it persecuted David\it* (1. Reg. 18. &c.) Ambitious \it Absalom\it* rebelled against the King his father (2. Reg. 15.) and \it Saba\it* of the tribe of Benjamin raised another rebellion (2. Reg. 20.) Likewise \it Adonias\it*, assisted by \it Abiathar\it* the high Priest, and by \it Joab\it* general of the army, pretended to reign his father David yet living, to prevent Salomon of the Kingdom (3. Reg. 1.) So God both shewed his justice, in suffering such afflictions to happen, for punishment of sin: and his mercy, in saving his Church from ruin.
\ip Moreover, for preservation of the Church, there were diverse divine Ordinances provided by \f - \ft Ordinary means of conserving the Church.\f* the law. For first all were strictly commanded, not to communicate with Infidels in their \f - \ft No participation with Infidels.\f* idolatry (Ex. 23.) nor with Schismatics in their schism (Nu. 16.) but \it to destroy all Idolaters\it* (Num. 33.) and \it shun all novelties\it* in religion, as a sure mark of idolatry, or false doctrine (Deut. 13.) \f - \ft No novelty to be admitted.\f* Further to conserve unity there was but \it one Tabernacle\it*, and \it one Altar for Sacrifice\it*, in \f - \ft \+it S. Chris. orat. 1. advers. Iudeos.\+it*\f* \f - \ft But one Tabernacle.\f* \f - \ft One Altar for sacrifice.\f* the whole people of Israel. Whereupon when the two tribes and half, on the other side Iordan, had made a several altar, all the tribes that dwelt in Chanaan, suspecting it was for sacrifice, sent presently to admonish them, and prepared to make war against them, except they destroyed their new altar, but being advertised that it was only \it an altar of monument\it*, and not for sacrifice, were therewith satisfied (Iosue. 22.) Afterwards the tribe of \it Dan, setting up idolatry\it*, and the other tribes \it not correcting\it* it, they were all punished. Which happened by occasion of another enormous sin, committed and not corrected in the tribe of Benjamin. For the other eleven tribes making war against them for this just cause, yea \it by God's direction\it*, and warrant, yet \it had the worse\it*, sustaining great slaughter of men in two conflicts; and in the third Benjamin was almost destroyed. Iudic. 20.
\ip Finally, for \it decision of all controversies\it* and ending of strife, the \it High Priest\it* was expressly \f - \ft The Church of Christ preserved from erring in Religion.\f* \it ordained\it* supreme Judge (Deut. 17.) And all were commanded in pain of death to submit their opinions, and obey his sentence: with promise of God's assistance, whereby his \it definitions\it* \f - \ft \+it Math. 16. 28. Luc. 22. Ioan. 14. 16. Eph. 4. 1. Tim. 3.\+it*\f* were \it certain and infallible\it*. For in consultations of doubts and difficult cases, God inspired him \f - \ft Not any temporal but Christ's Kingdom is in all nations and perpetual.\f* with \it doctrine of verity\it* (Exod. 28. 29. Levit. 8. Num. 3. 7. 9. 1. Reg. 23. 30.) Which judgement Seat Christ admonished the Jews to repair unto and follow (Math. 23.) though the Judges themselves did not the things which they taught. In so much that Caiphas, through this assistance of God's spirit, being otherwise a wicked man, yet pronounced the truth, \it That one must die for the people\it*. Which therefore S. John the Evangelist ascribeth to his Chair and office, \it because he was High Priest that year\it*. Ioan. 11.
\ip Seeing then God's providence and continual assistance was so clear, and assured in the Church of the old Testament, much more is the \it Church of Christ builded upon a sure rock, assured of\it* \f - \ft One supreme Judge of controversies.\f* \f - \ft All bound to obey him.\f* \it his perpetual assistance, & always preserved from erring in Faith, or in general practice of Religion\it*.
\ip And that by God's like assured ordinance of \it one supreme head and Judge, S. Peter, and his Successor\it*: for whom our Saviour prayed, \it that his faith should not fail\it*. Further commanding him, that \it he should confirm his brethren\it*. All which we see is performed in the Successors of S. Peter, whereas the successors of the other Apostles are all failed long since. The same most assured stability of the Church of Christ, is further confirmed by the whole Law and Prophets. Namely, Deut. 32. and 33. where Moyses foretelleth more power and grace in \it the Church, to be collected in the Gentiles of all nations\it*, than ever was in that of the Israelites or Jews. Likewise, 1. Reg. 2. The same was both prefigured and prophesied by holy Anna: \it The hungry\it* (those that desire God's grace and glory) \it are filled: until the barren woman\it* (the Church of the Gentiles) \it bare very many: and she that had many children was weakened\it*. Shewing that the Church of the Jews had many, until the plentitude of Gentiles much more abounded. Wherefore the Psalmist inviteth all nations to praise God, saying: Psal. 116. \it Praise our Lord all ye Gentiles: praise him all ye peoples\it*. Also 2. Reg. 7. God promised David, saying: \it Thy Kingdom for ever before thy face, and thy throne shall be firm continually.\it* \f - \ft His sentence infallible.\f*
\ip which was not verified in David's temporal Kingdom. For it was quickly divided, after \f - \ft \+it S. Aug. li. 17. c. 8. de civit. S. Epiph: heres. 29.\+it*\f* Salomon's death, and a small part left to his son Roboam. And after the captivity in Babylon, his seed had only title and right without possession of royal throne. Again 2. Reg. 22. The same royal Prophet in his Canticle of thanksgiving, and last prophetical words (chap. 23.) much preferreth the spiritual Kingdom of Christ, before the earthly Kingdom of the Jews. But most specially and plainly in the Psalms: Psal. 2. \it Why did the Gentiles rage, and peoples meditate vain things?\it* Signifying that the fury of all adversaries rageth in vain, against Christ and his Church. \it For I am appointed, by him\it* (saith Christ of his Father) \it King over Sion, his holy hill. I will give thee\it* (saith God to his Son) \it the\it* \f - \ft The Church of Christ universal.\f* \it Gentiles for thine inheritance, and thy possession the ends of the earth\it*. Psal. 17. \it A people which I\it* \f - \ft \+it Act. 4.\+it*\f* \it knew not, hath served me\it*. Psal. 44. \it The Queen\it* (the Church) \it stood on thy right hand in golden raiment, compassed with variety\it*, of virtues, and diverse sorts of holy professions. Psal. 47. \it Mount Sion is founded with the exultation of the whole earth. For ever and ever he\it* (Christ) \it shall rule us evermore\it*. Psal. 86. \it Glorious things are said of thee, o city of God\it*. But omitting innumerable other such texts, the 88. Psalm containeth a large prophecy of Christ and his Church, where S. Augustin giveth us this brief admonition: \it Christiani estis, Christum agnoscite. You are Christians, agnise\it* \f - \ft \+it in hunc. Psalm.\+it*\f* \it Christ. I will put\it* (saith God) \it his hand in the sea\it*, Christ's dominion in the Gentiles, and \it his right hand in the rivers\it*; all sorts shall serve him. \it He shall be high above the Kings of the earth\it*. Of the Church he addeth: \it I will put his seed for ever and ever, and his throne as the days of heaven\it*. Neither do sins frustrate this promise of God, therefore it followeth: \it But if his children shall forsake my law: and will not walk in my judgements: If they shall profane my justices, and not keep my commandments\it*; What then, will Christ for all this abandon his Church, as he did the old \f - \ft The Jews will not see Christ: \+it 2. Cor. 3.\+it* And Heretics will not see the Church which yet is always visible. \+it S. Aug. in Psal. 30. Conc. 2. Collat. Carthag. et Cont. Donatist.\+it*\f* Synagogue, of which God saith: Deut. 32. \it They have provoked me in that which was no God: and I will provoke them, in that which is no people\it*? Not so. How then? \it I will visit\it*, saith our Lord, \it their iniquities with a rod, and their sins with stripes. But my mercy I will not take away from him\it*. This is a strong Firmament (saith S. Augustin) \it God promiseth\it*, yea \it sweareth\it*, and will \it not lie to David,\it* \f - \ft \+it Ibidem.\+it*\f* \it that his seed shall continue for ever. His throne as the Sun in God's sight, and the Moon perfected for ever\it*. So this great Doctor sheweth by holy Scriptures against the Donatists, and in them against Protestants, that the militant Church of Christ hath been still, and shall be visible, during this transitory world.
\p
\v 1 AND his owne houſe Salomon built in thirtene yeares, and brough it to perfection.
\v 2 He built alſo the houſe of the foreſt of Libanus of an hundred cubites in length, and fiftie cubites in bredth, and thirtie cubites in height: and foure ſcore galleries betwen pillers of ceder: for he had cut ceder trees into pillers.
\v 3 And he decked the whole vaut with bordes of ceder, which was held vp with fiue and fourtie pillers. And one order had fiften pillers,
\v 4 ſet one againſt an other,
\v 5 and looked one ouer againſt an other, with equal ſpace betwen the pillers, and ouer the pillers ſquare beames in al equal.
\v 6 And the porche of the pillers he made of fiftie cubites in length, and thirtie cubites in bredth: and an other porche before the greater porche: and pillers, and toppes vpon the pillers.
\v 7 He made alſo the porche of the throne, wherein the ſeate of iudgement is; and couered it with ceder wood from the pauement vnto the toppe.
\v 8 And the litle houſe, where they ſate in iudgement, was in the middes of the porche of like worke. He made alſo a houſe for the daughter of Pharao (which Salomon had taken to wife) of ſuch worke, as alſo this porche.
\v 9 Al of choſen ſtones, which were ſawed by a certain rule & meaſure both within & without: from the fundation to the toppe of the walles, & without vnto the greater courte.
\v 10 And the fundations of choſen ſtones, great ſtones of ten or eight cubites.
\v 11 And aboue there were hewed choſen ſtones of equal meaſure, and in like maner of ceder.
\v 12 And the greater court round with three rewes of hewed ſtones, and one rew of planed ceder, moreouer alſo in the inner court of the houſe of our Lord, and in the porche of the houſe.
\v 13 King Salomon alſo ſent, and tooke Hiram from Tyre,
\v 14 the ſonne of a widow woman of the tribe of Nepthali, his father a Tyrian, an artificer in braſſe, and ful of wiſdom, and intelligence, and skil to make al worke of braſſe. Who when he was come to king Salomon, made al his worke.
\v 15 And he caſt two braſen pillers, of eightene cubites in height one piller: and a line of twelue cubites compaſſed both pillers.
\v 16 He made alſo two litle heades, which should be put vpon the heades of the pillers, caſt of braſſe: fiue cubites high one litle head, and fiue cubites the other litle head:
\v 17 and as it were in maner of a nette, and of cheynes knitte one to the other with maruelous worke. Both litle heades of the pillers were caſt: ſeuen rewes of litle nettes in one litle head, & ſeuen litle nettes in the other litle head.
\v 18 And finiſhed the pillers, and two rewes round about euerie nette, that they might couer the litle heades, which were ouer the toppe of the pomegranates: in like maner did he alſo to the ſecond litle head.
\v 19 And the litle heades, that were vpon the heades of the pillers, were made as it were with lilie worke, in the porche, of foure cubites.
\v 20 And againe other litle heades in the toppe of the pillers about, according to the meaſure of the piller againſt the litle nettes: and of the pomegranates were two hundred rewes round about the ſecond litle head.
\v 21 And he ſette two pillers in the porche of the temple: and when he had erected the piller on the right hand, he called the name therof, \f 1 \fr 7.21 \ft \+it * Firmnes.\+it*\f* Iachin: in like maner he erected the ſecond piller, and called the name therof \f 2 \fr 7.21 \ft \+it * in ſtrength.\+it*\f* Booz.
\v 22 And vpon the heades of the pillers he put a worke in maner of a lilie: and the worke of the pyllers was perfected.
\v 23 He made alſo \f a \fr 7.23 \ft A veſſel ſo called for the bigneſſe being a great lauatorie.\f* a ſea of founders worke of ten cubites from brimme to brimme, round in cõpaſſe, the height therof was of fiue cubites, and a corde of thirtie cubites did compaſſe it, round about.
\v 24 And the grauing vnder the brĩme compaſſed it, ten cubites going about the lauatorie: there were two rewes of chamfered forowed grauinges caſt.
\v 25 And it ſtood vpon twelue oxen, of which three looked to the North, and three to the Weſt, and three to the South, and three to the Eaſt, and the ſea was ouer them: whoſe hinder partes were al hid inward.
\v 26 And the thickenes of the lauatorie was of three ounces; and the brimme therof as it were the brimme of a chalice, and the leafe of criſped lilie: it contayned two thouſand \f b \fr 7.26 \ft Batus conteyneth about fiue galons: ſo this veſſel conteyned tenne thouſand galons.\f* bates.
\v 27 And he made ten braſen feete, of foure cubites in length euerie foote, and foure cubites in bredth, and three cubites in height.
\v 28 And the verie worke it ſelfe of the feete, was entergrauen: and entergrauinges betwen the ioyntures.
\v 29 And betwen the litle crownes and the playtes, lions, and oxen, and cherubs: and in the ioyntures likewiſe aboue: and vnder the lions, and oxen as it were bandes of braſſe hanging downe.
\v 30 And foure wheeles at euerie foote, and axeltrees of braſſe: and at foure ſides as it were litle ſhoulders vnder the lauatorie caſt, looking one againſt an other.
\v 31 The mouth alſo of the lauatorie was inward in the toppe of the head: and that which appeared outward, was of one cubite al round, and together it had one cubite & a halfe: and in the corners of the pillers were diuers engrauinges: and the middle enterpillers ſquare not round.
\v 32 The foure wheeles alſo, which were at the foure corners of a foote, ioyned one to an other vnder the foote: one wheele had in height a cubite and a halfe.
\v 33 And they were ſuch wheeles as are accuſtomed to be made in a chariote: and their axeltrees and ſpokes, and ſtrakes, and naues, al caſt.
\v 34 For thoſe foure litle ſhoulders alſo at euerie corner of one foote, were caſt out of the foote and ioyned together.
\v 35 And in the toppe of the foote was a certayne roundnes of halfe a cubite, ſo wrought, that the lauatorie might be put thereon, hauing the engrauinges therof, and diuerſe caruinges of it ſelf.
\v 36 He graued alſo in thoſe ſielinges, which were of braſſe, and in the corners, cherubs, and lions, and palmetrees, as it were in the ſimilitude of a man ſtanding, that they ſemed not to be engrauen, but put to round about.
\v 37 After this maner made he ten feete, of one caſting and meaſure, & like grauing.
\v 38 He made alſo ten lauatories of braſſe: one lauatorie conteyned fourtie bates, and it was of foure cubites: alſo at euerie foote, that is ten, he put ſo manie lauatories.
\v 39 And he ſette the ten feete, fiue on the right ſide of the temple, and fiue on the leaft: and the ſea he put on the right ſide of the temple againſt the Eaſt toward the South.
\v 40 Hiram therfore made cauldrons, and shoueles, and litle pottes, and perfected al the worke of king Salomon in the temple of our Lord.
\v 41 Two pyllers, and two cordes of the litle heades, vpon the litle heades of the pyllers: and two litle nettes, to couer the two cordes, that were ouer the heades of the pyllers.
\v 42 And foure hundred pomegranates in the two nettes: two rewes of pomegramates in euerie nette, to couer the cordes of the litle heades.
\v 43 And tenne feete, and tenne lauatories vpon the feete.
\v 44 And one ſea, and twelue oxen vnder the ſea.
\v 45 And cauldrons, and shouels, and litle pottes. Al the veſſels that Hiram made to king Salomon in the houſe of our Lord, were of bright latten.
\v 46 In the champayne countrie of Iordan did the king caſt thoſe thinges in clay ground, betwen Sacoth and Sarthan.
\v 47 And Salomon placed al the veſſel: but for the exceding great multitude the braſſe could not be weyed.
\v 48 And Salomon made al the veſſels in the houſe of our Lord: an altar of gold, and a table, whervpon the loaues of propoſition should be put, of gold:
\v 49 and candleſtickes of gold, fiue on the right hand, and fiue on the leaft againſt the oracle, of pure gold: and as it were lilie floures, and lampes about of gold: and golden ſnuffers,
\v 50 and water pottes, and fleshehookes, and phiales, and morters, and cenſars, of moſt pure gold: and the hindges of doores of the inner Sanctum ſanctorum, and of the doores of the houſe of the temple, were of gold.
\v 51 And Salomon perfected al the worke that he did in the houſe of our Lord, and brought in the thinges that Dauid his father \f c \fr 7.51 \ft Had deſigned and dedicated to holie vſes.\f* had ſanctified, ſiluer and gold, and the veſſel, and layed them in the treaſures of the houſe of our Lord.
\c 8
\cd The arke is brought in, and the temple dedicated. 10. a glorious clowde replenisheth it, 14. Salomon prayeth long to God, 55. bleſſeth the people. 62. and manie victimes are offered in this ſolemne feſtiuitie.
\p
\v 1 THEN were gathered together al the ancientes of Iſrael with the princes of the tribes, and the heades of the families of the children of Iſrael to king Salomon into Ieruſalem: that they might carrie the Arke of the couenant of our Lord out of the citie of Dauid, that is, out of Sion.
\v 2 And al Iſrael aſſembled to king Salomon in the moneth of Ethanim, on a ſolemne day, that is the ſeuenth moneth.
\v 3 And al the ancientes of Iſrael came, and the prieſtes tooke the arke,
\v 4 and caried the arke of our Lord, and the tabernacle of couenant, and al the veſſels of the Sanctuarie, that were in the tabernacle: and the Prieſtes and the Leuites caried them.
\v 5 And king Salomon, and al the multitude of Iſrael, which was aſſembled vnto him, went with him before the arke, and they immolated sheepe and oxen without eſtimation & number.
\v 6 And the prieſtes brought in the arke of the couenant of our Lord into his place, into the oracle of the temple, into Sanctum ſanctorum vnder the winges of the cherubs.
\v 7 For the cherubs ſpred their winges ouer the place of the arke, and couered the arke, and the barres therof aboue.
\v 8 And wheras the barres ſtood out, and the endes of them appeared without in the Sanctuarie before the oracle, they appeared no farder outward, which alſo were there vntil this preſent day.
\v 9 And in the arke there was \f a \fr 8.9 \ft There was no more within the arke, \+it Deut. 10\+it*. but on the outſide was the rodde of Aaron. \+it Nu. 17. Heb. 9\+it*. the golden potte with Manna. \+it Exod. 16. Heb. 9\+it*. and the booke of the law repeted by Moyſes. \+it Deut. 31\+it*.\f* nothing els but two tables of ſtone, which Moyſes put in it in Horeb, when our Lord made the couenant with the children of Iſrael, when they came out of the Land of Ægypt.
\v 10 And it came to paſſe, when the prieſtes were gone out of the Sanctuarie, a clowde filled the houſe of our Lord,
\v 11 and the prieſtes could not ſtand and miniſter for the clowde: for the glorie of our Lord had filled the houſe of our Lord.
\v 12 Then ſayd Salomon: Our Lord ſayd that he would dwel in a clowde.
\v 13 Building I haue built a houſe for thy habitation, thy moſt firme throne for euer.
\v 14 And the king turned his face, and \f b \fr 8.14 \ft Princes bleſſe their people & parentes their children.\f* bleſſed al the church of Iſrael: for al the church if Iſrael ſtood.
\v 15 And Salomon ſayd: Bleſſed be our Lord the God of Iſrael, who ſpake by his mouth to Dauid my father, & in his owne handes hath perfected it, ſaying:
\v 16 Since the day that I brought my people Iſrael out of Ægypt, I choſe no citie of al the tribes of Iſrael, that a houſe might be built, and my name might be there: but I choſe Dauid to be ouer my people Iſrael.
\v 17 And Dauid my father would haue built a houſe to the name of our Lord the God of Iſrael:
\v 18 and our Lord ſayd to Dauid my father: In that thou haſt thought in thy hart to build a houſe to my name, thou haſt done wel, caſting this ſame thing in thy mynd.
\v 19 Neuertheleſſe thou shalt not build me a houſe, but thy ſonne, that shal come forth of thy reynes, he shal build a houſe to my name.
\v 20 Our Lord hath confirmed his word, which he ſpake: and I ſtand for Dauid my father, and ſitte vpon the throne of Iſrael, as our Lord hath ſpoken: and I haue built a houſe to the name of our Lord the God of Iſrael.
\v 21 And I haue appoynted there a place for the arke, wherin the couenant of our Lord is, which he made with our fathers, when they came out of the Land of Ægypt.
\v 22 And Salomon ſtood before the altar of our Lord in the ſight of the aſſemblie of Iſrael, and extended his handes toward heauen,
\v 23 and ſayd: Lord God of Iſrael, there is no God like to thee in heauen aboue, and vpon the earth beneth: which keepeſt couenant and mercie with thy ſeruantes, that walke before thee in al their hart.
\v 24 Which haſt kept to thy ſeruant Dauid my father, the thinges that thou haſt ſpoken to him: by mouth thou didſt ſpeake, and with thy handes thou haſt accomplished, as this day proueth.
\v 25 Now therfore Lord God of Iſrael, keepe vnto thy ſeruant Dauid my father the thinges which thou haſt ſpoken to him, ſaying: There shal not be taken away of thee a man before me, which ſitteth vpon the throne of Iſrael: \f c \fr 8.25 \ft Salomon knew wel Gods conditional promiſe, but perſeuered not in keping his commandments, and therfore a great part of the kingdom was taken from his children, yet the right of the kingdom of Iuda remayned to his ſeede euen to Chriſt our Sauiour.\f* yet ſo if thy children shal keepe their way, that they walke before me as thou haſt walked in my ſight.
\v 26 And now Lord God of Iſrael, let thy wordes be eſtablished, which thou haſt ſpoken to thy ſeruant Dauid my father.
\v 27 Is it then to be thought that in deede God dwelleth vpon the earth? for if heauen, and the heauens of heauens can not conteyne thee, how much more this houſe, which I haue built?
\v 28 But looke toward the prayer of thy ſeruant, and to his petitions ô Lord my God: heare the hymne and the prayer, which thy ſeruant prayeth before thee this day:
\v 29 that thy eies be opened vpon this houſe night and day: vpon the houſe, wherof thou ſayeſt: My name ſhal be there: that thou heare the prayer, which thy ſeruant prayeth in this place to thee.
\v 30 That thou heare the requeſt of thy ſeruant and of thy people Iſrael, whatſoeuer they shal pray for in this place, and thou shalt heare in the place of thy habitation in heauen: and when thou haſt heard, thou shalt be merciful.
\v 31 If a man shal ſinne agaynſt his neighbour, and shal haue any oath, wherwith he is held faſt bound: and shal come becauſe of the oath before thine altar into thy houſe,
\v 32 thou shalt heare in heauen: and shalt doe, and iudge thy ſeruantes, condemning the impious, and rendring his way vpon his head, and iuſtifying the iuſt, and \f d \fr 8.32 \ft Reward of good workes.\f* rewarding him according to his iuſtice.
\v 33 If thy people Iſrael shal flee their enemies (becauſe they wil ſinne agaynſt thee) and doing penance, and confeſſing to thy name, shal come, and pray, and beſech thee in this houſe;
\v 34 heare in heauen, and forgeue the ſinne of thy people Iſrael, and thou shalt reduce them vnto the land, which thou gaueſt to their fathers.
\v 35 If the heauen shal be shut, and it rayne not, becauſe of their ſinnes, and praying in this place, they doe penance to thy name, and shal be conuerted from their ſinnes through their affliction:
\v 36 heare them in heauen, and forgeue the ſinnes of thy ſeruantes, and of thy people Iſrael: and shew them a good way wherin they may walke, and geue rayne vpon thy land, which thou haſt geuen to thy people in poſſeſſion.
\v 37 If famine aryſe in the land, or peſtilence, or corrupt ayre, or blaſting, or locuſt, or ruſt, and their enemie afflict them beſieging the gates, al plague, al infirmity,
\v 38 al curſing, and banning, that shal chance to any man of thy people Iſrael: if any man shal know the wound of his hart, and shal ſpred forth his handes in this houſe,
\v 39 thou shalt heare in heauen, in the place of thy habitation, and shalt be merciful agayne, and shalt ſo doe that thou geue to euerie one according to his wayes, as thou shalt ſee his hart (for thou onlie knoweſt the hart of al the children of men)
\v 40 that they feare thee al the dayes, which they liue vpon the face of the land, which thou haſt geuen our fathers.
\v 41 Moreouer alſo the ſtranger, which is not of thy people Iſrael, when he shal come from a farre countrie for thy name (for thy great name shal be heard of, and thy ſtrong hand,
\v 42 and thy ſtretched out arme euerie where) when therfore he shal come, and shal pray in this place,
\v 43 thou shalt heare in heauen, in the firmament of thy habitation, & thou shalt doe al thinges, for the which the ſtranger shal inuocate thee: that al the peoples of the earth may lerne to feare thy name, as thy people Iſrael, and may proue that thy name is inuocated vpon this houſe, which I haue built.
\v 44 If thy people shal goe forth to warre agaynſt their enemies, by the way, whitherſoeuer thou shalt ſend them, they shal pray to thee agaynſt the way of the citie, which thou haſt choſen, and agaynſt the houſe, which I haue built to thy name,
\v 45 and thou shalt heare in heauen their prayers, and their petitions, and shalt doe iudgement for them.
\v 46 But if they shal ſinne to thee (for there is no man which ſinneth not) and thou being wrath shalt deliuer them to their enemies, and they shal be led captiue into the land of their enemies farre or neere,
\v 47 and shal doe penance in their \f e \fr 8.47 \ft External workes of penance, except they proceede from the hart, ſuffice not for remiſſion of ſinne.\f* hart in the place of captiuitie, and conuerted shal beſech thee in their captiuitie, ſaying: We haue ſinned, we haue done wickedly, we haue dealt impiouſly:
\v 48 and shal returne to thee in al their hart, and al their ſoule, in the land of their enemies, to the which they shal be led captiue: & shal pray to thee agaynſt the way of their land, which thou haſt geuen to their fathers, and of the citie which thou haſt choſen, & of the temple which I haue built to thy name:
\v 49 thou shalt heare in heauen, in the firmament of thy throne their prayers, and their petitions, and shalt doe their iudgement for them:
\v 50 and shalt be merciful to thy people, which ſinned to thee, and to al their iniquities, wherwith they haue tranſgreſſed agaynſt thee: and shalt geue mercie before them, that shal haue them captiues, that they may haue compaſſion on them.
\v 51 For they are thy people, and thine inheritance, whom thou haſt brought out of the Land of Ægypt, from the middes of the yron fornace.
\v 52 That thy eies be open to the petition of thy ſeruant, and of thy people Iſrael, & thou heare them in al thinges for which they shal inuocate thee.
\v 53 For thou haſt ſeparated them to thee for an inheritance from al the peoples of the earth, as thou haſt ſpoken by Moyſes thy ſeruant, when thou didſt bring our fathers out of Ægypt, Lord God.
\v 54 And it came to paſſe, when Salomon had accomplished praying to our Lord al this prayer and petition, he roſe from the ſight of the altar of our Lord: for he had faſtened both knees on the ground, and had ſpred his handes toward heauen.
\v 55 He ſtood therfore and bleſſed al the aſſemblie of Iſrael with a lowde voyce, ſaying:
\v 56 Bleſſed be our Lord, which hath geuen reſt to his people Iſrael, according to al thinges that he hath ſpoken: there hath not fayled ſo much as one word of al the good thinges, that he ſpake by Moyſes this ſeruant.
\v 57 Be our Lord God with vs, as he hath beene with our fathers, not forſaking, nor reiecting vs.
\v 58 But incline he our hartes to him, that we may walke in al his waies, and keepe his commandementes, and his ceremonies, and iudgementes whatſoeuer he commanded our fathers.
\v 59 And be theſe my wordes, wherewith I haue prayed before our Lord, approching to our Lord God day and night, that he may doe iudgement for his ſeruant, and for his people Iſrael day by day:
\v 60 that al the peoples of the earth may knowe, that our Lord he is God, and there is none other beſides him.
\v 61 Let our hart alſo be perfect with our Lord God, that we walke in his decrees, and keepe his cõmandementes, as alſo this day.
\v 62 Therfore the king, and al Iſrael with him, did immolate victimes before our Lord.
\v 63 And Salomon killed pacifique hoſtes, which he immolated to our Lord, of oxen two and twentie thouſand, and of sheepe an hundred twentie thouſand: and they dedicated the temple of our Lord, the king, and the children of Iſrael.
\v 64 In that day the king ſanctified the middes of the court, that was before the houſe of our Lord: for he made the holocauſt there, and ſacrifice, and fatte of the pacifiques: becauſe the braſen altar, that was before our Lord, was too litle, and could not take the holocauſt, and ſacrifice, and fatte of the pacifiques.
\v 65 Salomon therfore made in that time a ſolemne feſtiuitie, and al Iſrael with him, a great multitude from the entrance of Emath vnto the Ryuer of Ægypt, before our Lord God, ſeuen daies and ſeuen daies, that is, fourtene daies.
\v 66 And in the eight day he diſmiſſed the people: Who bleſſing the king, went into their tabernacles reioyſing, and with a ioyful hart for al the good thinges, that our Lord had done to Dauid his ſeruant, and to Iſrael his people.
\c 9
\cd Our Lord appearing againe to Salomon, 4. admonisheth him and his people to keepe the precepts, 6. threatening punishment if they do not. 10. The king of Tyre receiueth twentie cities of Salomon, but liketh them not. 14. Salomon buildeth more cities and townes. 20. Maketh diuers nations tributarie. 24. The Quene repayreth to her houſe. 25. The king offereth victimes thriſe euerie yeare. 26. and fetcheth gold from Ophir.
\p
\v 1 AND it came to paſſe when Salomon had perfected the building of the houſe, of our Lord, & the kinges houſe, and al that he wiſhed and would haue done,
\v 2 our Lord appeared to him the ſecond time, as he had appeared to him in Gabaon.
\v 3 And our Lord ſaid to him: I haue heard thy prayer & thy petition, which thou haſt prayed before me: I haue ſanctified this houſe, which thou haſt built, that I might put my name there for euer, and myne eies and my hart shal be there alwaies.
\v 4 Thou alſo if thou wilt walke before me, as thy father walked, \f a \fr 9.4 \ft External worship is not acceptable to God, except it procede from internal ſinceritie and deuotion. VVherfore S. Auguſtin ſayth: \+it God is worshipped in faith, hope, and charitie. Enchirid. c. 3\+it*.\f* in ſimplicitie of hart, and in equitie: and wilt doe al thinges, which I haue commanded thee, and wilt keepe my ordinances and my iudgementes,
\v 5 I wil ſette the throne of thy kingdom ouer Iſrael for euer, as I haue ſpoken to Dauid thy father, ſaying: There shal not be taken away a man of thy ſtocke from the throne of Iſrael.
\v 6 But if by reuolting you and your children shal turne away, not folowing me, nor keeping my commandementes, and my ceremonies, which I haue propoſed to you, but shal goe and worshippe ſtrange goddes, and adore them:
\v 7 I wil take away Iſrael from the face of the land, which I haue geuen them, and the temple which I haue ſanctified to my name, I wil caſt away from my ſight, and Iſrael shal be for a prouerbe, and for a fable to al peoples.
\v 8 And this houſe shal be for an example: euerie one that shal paſſe by it, shal wonder, and hiſſe, and ſay: Why hath the Lord done thus to this land, and to this houſe?
\v 9 And they shal anſwere: Becauſe they haue forſaken the Lord their God, which brought their fathers out of the land of Ægypt, and haue folowed ſtrange goddes, & adored them, and worshipped them: therfore hath the Lord brought vpon them al this euil.
\v 10 And twentie yeares being complete, after that Salomon had built the two houſes, that is, the houſe of our Lord, and the houſe of the king
\v 11 (Hiram the king of Tyre miniſtring to Salomon ceder trees & firre trees, and gold according to al that he had neede) then Salomon \f b \fr 9.11 \ft Salomon did not ſel theſe cities, for he could not alienate them, but let the king of Tyre haue the vſe and reuenewes in payment for timber, & for the gold which he ſent.\f* gaue to Hiram twentie townes in the Land of Galilee.
\v 12 And Hiram went from Tyre to ſee the townes which Salomon had geuen him, and they pleaſed him not,
\v 13 and he ſayd: Are theſe the cities, which thou haſt geuen me, brother? And he called them the land \f 1 \fr 9.13 \ft \+it dirtie, or diſpleaſing\+it*.\f* Chabul, vntil this day.
\v 14 Hiram alſo ſent to king Salomon an hundred and twentie talentes of gold.
\v 15 This is the ſumme of the expences, which king Salomon offered to build the houſe of our Lord, and his owne houſe, and Mello, and the wal of Ieruſalem, and Heſer, and Mageddo, and Gazer
\v 16 Pharao the king of Ægypt came vp and tooke Gazer, and burnt it with fire: And the Chananite, that dwelt in the citie, he ſlewe, and gaue it for a dowrie to his daughter the wife of Salomon.
\v 17 Salomon therfore built Gazer, and Bethhoron the lower,
\v 18 and Baalath, and Palmira in the Land of the wildernes.
\v 19 And al the villages, that perteyned to him, and were without wal, he fenſed, and the cities of the chariotes, and the cities of the horſemen, and whatſoeuer pleaſed him to build in Ieruſalem, and in Libanus, and in al the land of his dominion.
\v 20 Al the people, that was remayning of the Amorrheites, and Hetheites, and Pherezeites, and Heueites, and Iebuſeites, that are not of the children of Iſrael:
\v 21 their children, that were remayning in the land, to witte, thoſe whom the children of Iſrael could not abolish: Salomon made tributaries, vntil this day.
\v 22 But the children of Iſrael Salomon appoynted not any man to ſerue, but they were men of warre, and his ſeruantes, and princes, and captaynes, and ouerſeers of the chariotes and horſes.
\v 23 And there were princes ouer al the workes of Salomon, made ouerſeers, fiue hundred fiftie, which had the people ſubiect, and commanded ouer their appoynted workes.
\v 24 And the daughter of Pharao went vp out of the citie of Dauid into her houſe, which Salomon had built her: then did he build Mello.
\v 25 Salomon alſo offered three tymes euerie yeare holocauſtes, and pacifique victimes vpon the altar, which he had built to our Lord, and he burnt incenſe before our Lord: and the temple was perfected.
\v 26 King Salomon alſo made \f c \fr 9.26 \ft a monument.\f* a name in Aſiongaber, which is beſide Ailath in the shore of the Readſea in the Land of Idumea.
\v 27 And Hiram ſent in that nauie his men, that were mariners and skilful of the ſea, with the ſeruantes of Salomon.
\v 28 Who when they were come into Ophir, the gold taken thence of foure hundred and twentie talentes, they brought to king Salomon.
\c 10
\cd The quene of Saba coming to king Salomon, admireth his wiſdom, magnificence, and order of gouernment 10. She geueth and receiueth giftes. 14. Salomon receiueth much gold diuers wayes: 16. maketh golden targettes. 18. a magnificent throne. 21. and much golden veſſel. 25. Manie bring him preſentes. 26. He hath manie chariottes, horſemen. 27. abundance of ſiluer 28. Marchantes of diuers kingdomes ſel him horſes.
\p
\v 1 BVT \f 1 \fr 10.1 \ft A wonderful thing, that a Quene vpon fame of a mans wiſdom traueled ſo farre to heare him ſpeake, and to ſee his gouernment. but it was Gods inſpiration, to ſignifie by this figure, that the Church of Chriſt should be gathered of the Gentiles in al nations. Kinges, Quenes & moſt potent Princes alſo ſubmitting themſelues to Chriſt. \+it Iſaie. 49\+it*.\f* the Queene of \f a \fr 10.1 \ft Part of Arabia is called Saba, nere to Iurie. but this Saba is beyond Arabia, as S. Hierom teſtifieth \+it in Eſaia. 60. li. 17\+it*. it ſemeth to be in Æthiopia. for our Sauiour ſaith \+it Mat 12. The quene of the South came from the endes of the earth, to heare the wiſdom of Salomon\+it*.\f* Saba alſo hauing heard the fame of Salomon, in the name of our Lord came to proue him, in hard propoſitions.
\v 2 And entring into Ieruſalem with a great trayne, and riches, and camels carying ſpices, and gold exceding infinite, and pretious ſtones, she came to king Salomon, and ſpake to him al thinges that she had in her hart.
\v 3 And Salomon interpreted to her al the wordes, that she propoſed: there was not a word, that the king could be ignorant of, and could not anſwer her.
\v 4 And the queene of Saba ſeing al the wiſedom of Salomon, and the houſe, which he had built,
\v 5 and the meates of his table, and the habitations of his ſeruantes, and the orders of them that ſerued, and their garmentes, cupbearers, and the holocauſtes which he offered in the houſe of our Lord \f b \fr 10.5 \ft As this quene had no ſpirite, when she ſaw Salomons wiſdom, ſo the Church gathered of gentiles knowing Chriſts grace, & finding the maſters of Euangelical doctrin, caſting away the ſpirite of pride, and laying of al hautineſſe of mind, lerned to diſtruſt in her ſelf, and to truſt in the great mercie of her king. \+it S. Greg in Pſal. 7. pæniten. to. 2\+it*.\f* she had no longer ſpirit,
\v 6 and she ſaid to the king: The report is true, which I haue heard in my countrie,
\v 7 concerning thy wordes, and concerning thy wiſedom. and I did not beleue them that told me, til my ſelfe came, and ſawe with myne eies, and haue proued that the half not been told me: greater is thy wiſdom, and thy workes, then the rumour, which I haue heard.
\v 8 Bleſſed are thy men, and bleſſed are thy ſeruantes, which ſtand before thee alwaies, and heare thy wiſedom.
\v 9 Be the Lord thy God bleſſed, whom thou haſt pleaſed, and that hath ſette thee vpon the throne of Iſrael, for that the Lord hath loued Iſrael for euer, and hath appointed thee king, to do iudgement and iuſtice.
\v 10 She therfore gaue to the king an hundred and twentie talentes of gold, and ſpices exceding much, and pretious ſtones: There was no more brought ſo much ſpice, as that which the Quene of Saba gaue to king Salomon.
\v 11 (But the naue alſo of Hiram, which caried gold out of Ophir, brought from Ophir thyine trees exceding manie, and pretious ſtones.
\v 12 And the king made of the thyine trees the porches of the houſe of our Lord, and of the kinges houſe and harpes & vials for the ſingers: there wete not ſuch thyine trees broughs, nor ſeen vntil this preſent daye.)
\v 13 And king Salomon gaue to the queene of Saba al that she would, and asked of him: beſide thoſe thinges, which of him ſelfe he offered her for a royal gift. Who returned, and went into her countrie with her ſeruantes.
\v 14 And the weight of the gold, that was brought to Salomon euerie yeare, was of ſix hundred ſixtie ſix talentes of gold:
\v 15 beſide that, which the men brought, that were ouer the tributes, and merchantes, and al that ſold light wares, and al the kinges of Arabia, and the dukes of the land.
\v 16 Salomon alſo made two hundred ſhieldes of moſt pure gold, ſix hundred ſicles of gold did he allow for the plates of one shield.
\v 17 And three hundred tergattes of tried gold: and three hundred poundes of gold garnished one terget: and the king put them in the houſe of the foreſt of Libanus.
\v 18 King Salomon alſo made a great throne of yuorie: and couered it with gold exceding yellow,
\v 19 which had ſixe ſteppes: and the toppe of the throne was round in the hinder part: and the two handes on either ſide holding the ſeate: and two lyons ſtood at euery hand.
\v 20 And twelue litle lyons ſtanding vpon the ſixe ſteppes on either ſide: there was not ſuch a worke made in al kingdomes.
\v 21 Yea and al the veſſels, out of the which the king Salomon drunke, were of gold: and al the furniture of the houſe of the foreſt of Libanus of moſt pure gold: there was no ſiluer, neither was it thought of any price in the daies of Salomon,
\v 22 becauſe rhe kinges nauie, once in three yeares, went with the nauie of Hiram on the ſea into Tharſis, bringing thence gold, and ſiluer, and the teeth of elephantes, and apes, and pecockes.
\v 23 King Salomon therfore was magnified aboue al the kinges of the earth in riches, and wiſedom.
\v 24 And al the earth deſired to ſee Salomons face, that they might heare his wiſedom, which God had geuen in his hart.
\v 25 And euerie one preſented him giftes, veſſel of ſiluer and gold, garmentes and inſtrumentes for warre, ſpices alſo, and horſes and mules euerie yeare.
\v 26 And Salomon gathered together the chariotes and horſemen, and there amounted to him a thouſand foure hundred chariotes, and twelue thouſand horſemen: and he diſpoſed them in fenſed cities, and with the king in Ieruſalem.
\v 27 And he made that there was as great abundance of ſiluer In Ieruſalem, as of ſtones: and of ceder trees he cauſed ſuch a multitude, as if it were ſycomore trees, which grow in the playnes.
\v 28 And there were horſes brought for Salomon out of Ægypt, and Coa, for the kinges merchantes brought them out of Coa, and brought them at a ſette price.
\v 29 And a chariote of foure horſes came out of Ægypt, for ſixe hundred ſicles of ſiluer, and one horſe for an hundred and fiftie. And after this maner did al the kinges of the Hetheites, and of Syria ſel horſes.
\c 11
\cd Salomon louing and marying manie wemen of diuers nations, is drawen by them to idolatrie. 9. God therfore offended ſuffereth Adad an Idumean, 23. Razon king of Damaſcus, 26. and Hieroboam his owne ſeruant to make warre against him. 29. Ahias the prophet fortelleth Hieroboam, that he shal reigne ouer tenne tribes, leauing but two to Salomons heyres, 38. with promiſe to proſper, if he ſerue God. 42. Salomon dieth.
\p
\v 1 AND king Salomon loued manie wemen ſtrangers, the daughter alſo of Pharao, and Moabites, and Ammonites, Idumeians, and Sidonians, and Hetheians:
\v 2 of the nations, wherof our Lord ſayd to the children of Iſrael: You ſhal not goe in vnto them, neither shal anie of them come in vnto yours: for they wil moſt certainly turne away your hartes to folow their goddes. To theſe therfore was Salomon copled in moſt feruent loue.
\v 3 And he had wiues as it were queenes \f a \fr 11.3 \ft Though pluralitie of wiues was then alowed, yet it was forbid to multiplie manie. \+it Deut. 17\+it*.\f* ſeuen hundred, and concubines three hundred: and the wemen turned away his hart.
\v 4 And when he was now old, his hart was depraued by wemen, that he folowed ſtrange goddes: neither was his hart perfect with our Lord his God, as the hart of Dauid his father.
\v 5 But Salomon worshipped Aſtarthee the goddeſſe of the Sidonians, and Moloch the idol of the Ammonites.
\v 6 And Salomon did that which was not liked before our Lord, and he accomplished not to folow our Lord, as Dauid his father.
\v 7 Then built Salomon a temple to Camos the idol of Moab, in the mount that is agaynſt Ieruſalem, and to Moloch the idol of the children of Ammon.
\v 8 And in this maner did he to al his wiues that were ſtrangers, which burnt frankencenſe, and immolated to their goddes.
\v 9 Therfore our Lord was wrath with Salomon, becauſe his minde was turned away from our Lord the God of Iſrael, who had appeared vnto him the ſecond tyme,
\v 10 and had commanded him concerning this word, that he should not folow ſtrange goddes, & he kept not the thinges which our Lord commanded him.
\v 11 Our Lord therfore ſayd to Salomon: Becauſe thou haſt done this, and haſt not kept my couenant, and my preceptes, which I haue commanded thee, breaking I wil rent aſunder thy kingdom, and wil geue it to thy ſeruant.
\v 12 Neuertheleſſe in thy dayes I wil not doe it, becauſe of Dauid thy father: out of the hand of thy ſonne I wil rent it,
\v 13 neither wil I take away the whole kingdom, but \f b \fr 11.13 \ft The tribe of Iuda.\f* one tribe I wil geue to thy ſonne for Dauid my ſeruant, and \f c \fr 11.13 \ft By Ieruſalem is vnderſtood the tribe of Beniamin, wherin it ſtood. ſo there remained two tribes to Salomons heynes.\f* Ieruſalem. Which I haue choſen.
\v 14 And our Lord rayſed vp an aduerſarie to Salomon, Adad an Idumeite of the kinges ſeede, who was in Edom.
\v 15 For when Dauid was in Idumea, and Ioab the general of the warfare was gone vp to burie them that were ſlayne, and had ſlayne al malekind in Idumea, \x - \xt 2. Reg. 8.\x*
\v 16 (for Ioab taried there ſix monethes and al Iſrael, til he ſlew al malekind in Idumea,)
\v 17 Adad him ſelf fled, and men of Idumea of his fathers ſeruantes with him, to goe into Ægypt: and Adad was a litle boy.
\v 18 And when they roſe out of Madian, they came into Pharan, and they tooke with them men of Pharan, and entered into Ægypt to Pharao the king of Ægypt: who gaue him a houſe, and appoynted him meates, and aſſigued him land.
\v 19 And Adad found grace before Pharao excedingly, in ſo much that he gaue him to wife, the germane ſiſter of his wife Taphnes the queene.
\v 20 And the ſiſter of Taphnes bare him a ſonne Genubath, and Taphnes brought him vp in the houſe of Pharao: and Genubath was dwelling at Pharaoes houſe with his children.
\v 21 And when Adad in Ægypt had heard, that Dauid ſlept with his fathers, and that Ioab the general of the warefare was dead, he ſayd to Pharao: Diſmiſſe me, that I may goe into my countrie.
\v 22 And Pharao ſayd to him: For what lackeſt thou with me, that thou ſeekeſt to goe into thyne owne countrie? But he anſwered: Nothing: yet I beſech thee that thou diſmiſſe me.
\v 23 God alſo rayſed vp to him an aduerſarie, Razon the ſonne of Eliada, who had fled Adarezer the king of Soba his lord: \x - \xt 2. Reg. 10.\x*
\v 24 and he gathered men agaynſt him, and he became the captayne of theues, when Dauid killed them: and they went to Damaſcus, and dwelt there, and they made him king in Damaſcus,
\v 25 and he was an aduerſarie to Iſrael \f d \fr 11.25 \ft From the time that Salomon fel to idolatrie, he was more impugned by three perpetual aduerſaries, Adad, Razon, and Hieroboam; myſtically ſignifying the flesh, the world, and the diuel.\f* al the dayes of Salomon: and this is the euil of Adad, and hatred agaynſt Iſrael, and he reigned in Syria.
\v 26 Ieroboam alſo the ſonne of Nabath, an Ephratheite of Sareda, the ſeruant of Salomon, whoſe mother was called Serua, a woman widow lifted vp his hand agaynſt the king.
\v 27 And this is the cauſe of his rebellion agaynſt him, becauſe Salomon built Mello, and filled vp the breache of the citie of Dauid his father.
\v 28 And Ieroboam was a ſtrong man and mightie: and Salomon ſeing the youngman of a good witte & induſtrious, had made him chief ouer the tributes of al the houſe of Ioſeph.
\v 29 It came to paſſe therfore at that tyme, that Ieroboam went out of Ieruſalem, and the prophete Ahias the Silonite found him in the way, couered with a new cloke: and they two onlie were in the field.
\v 30 And Ahias taking his new cloke, wherwith he was couered, \f e \fr 11.30 \ft This fact confirmed his wordes, that he ſpoke ſeriouſly & fained not.\f* cut it into twelue partes.
\v 31 And he ſayd to Ieroboam: Take vnto thee ten pieces: for thus ſayth our Lord the God of Iſrael: Behold I wil rent the kingdom out of the hand of Salomon, and wil geue thee ten tribes.
\v 32 But one tribe shal remayne to him for my ſeruant Dauid, and Ieruſalem the citie, which I haue choſen of al the tribes of Iſrael:
\v 33 becauſe he hath forſaken me, and hath adored Aſtarthee the goddeſſe of the Sidonians, & Chamos the god of Moab, and Moloch the god of the children of Ammon: and hath not walked in my waies, to doe iuſtice before me, and my preceptes, and iudgementes as Dauid his father.
\v 34 Neither wil I take away al the kingdom out of his hand, but I wil make him prince al the daies of his life, for Dauid my ſeruant, whom I choſe, who kept my commandmentes and my preceptes.
\v 35 But I wil take away the kingdom out of his ſonnes hand, and wil geue thee ten tribes:
\v 36 and to his ſonne I wil geue one tribe, that there may remayne a lampe to Dauid my ſeruant at al times before me in Ieruſalem, the citie which I haue choſen, that my name might be there.
\v 37 And thee wil I take, and thou shalt reigne ouer al thinges, that thy ſoule deſireth, and thou shalt be king ouer Iſrael.
\v 38 If therfore thou wilt heare al thinges, that I shal command thee, and wilt walke in my waies, and doe that which is right before me, keeping my commandmentes and my preeceptes, as Dauid my ſeruant did: I wil be with thee, and wil build thee a faythful houſe, as I built a houſe to Dauid, and I wil deliuer Iſrael to thee:
\v 39 and I wil afflict the ſeede of Dauid vpon this, but yet not alwaies.
\v 40 Salomon therfore would haue killed Ieroboam: who aroſe, and fled into Ægypt to Seſac the king of Ægypt, and was in Ægypt vntil the death of Salomon.
\v 41 And the reſt of the wordes of Salomon, and al that he did, and his wiſedom: behold they are al written in the Booke of the wordes of the daies of Salomon.
\v 42 And the daies, that Salomon reigned in Ieruſalem ouer al Iſrael, are fourtie yeares.
\v 43 And Salomon \f f \fr 11.43 \ft Whether he repented and was ſaued or no, is vncertaine.\f* ſlept with his fathers, and was buried in the citie of Dauid his father, and Roboam his ſonne reigned for him.
\c 12
\cd Roboam folowing youngmens counſel, 16. Ieroboam poſſeſſeth ten tribes of his Kingdom. 21. VVhich he endouoring to recouer by warre, is admonished by a prophet to ceaſe. 26. Hieroboam ſetteth vp golden calues to be adored, making temples, altares, and prieſtes fitte for his purpoſe.
\p
\v 1 AND \f 1 \fr 12.1 \ft The third part. The diviſion of the Kingdom. Several reigns of certain kings: and preaching of ſpecial prophets.\f* Roboam came into Sichem: for thither was al Iſrael gathered together to make him king.
\v 2 But Ieroboam the ſonne of Nabat, when he was yet in Ægypt fugitiue from the face of king Salomon, hearing of his death, returned out of Ægypt.
\v 3 And they ſent and called him: Ieroboam therfore came, and al the multitude of Iſrael, and they ſpake to Roboam, ſaying.
\v 4 Thy father layd a moſt hard yoke vpon vs: thou therfore diminish now a litle of thy fathers moſt hard empire, and of the moſt heauie yoke, that he layd vpon vs, and we wil ſerue thee.
\v 5 Who ſayd to them: Goe vntil the third day, and returne to me. And when the people was gone,
\v 6 king Roboam tooke counſel with the ancièntes, that aſſiſted before Salomon his father, whiles he yet liued, and he ſayd: What counſel doe you geue me, that I may anſwer this people?
\v 7 Who ſayd to him: If this day thou wilt yeld to this people, and condeſcend to them, and graunt to their petition, and wilt ſpeake to them gentle wordes, they wil be thy ſeruantes alwaies.
\v 8 Who leaft the counſel of the ancientes, which they had geuen him, and admitted yongmen, that had bene brought vp with him, and wayted on him,
\v 9 and he ſayd to them: What counſel geue you me, that I may anſwer this people, which haue ſayd to me: Make the yoke lighter which thy father hath put vpon vs?
\v 10 And the yongmen, that had bene brought vp with him, ſayd: Thus ſpeake to this people, which haue ſpoken to thee, ſaying: Thy father aggrauated our yoke, doe thou eaſe it. Thus shalt thou ſpeake to them: My leaſt finger is groſſer then the backe of my father.
\v 11 And now my father layd vpon you a heauie yoke, but I wil adde vpon your yoke: my father bette you with ſcourges, but I wil beate you with ſcorpions.
\v 12 Ieroboam therfore came, and al the people to Roboam the third day, as the king had ſpoken, ſaying: Returne to me the third day.
\v 13 And the king anſwered the people rough wordes, leauing the counſel of the ancientes, which they had geuen him,
\v 14 and he ſpake to them according to the counſel of the youngmen, ſaying: My father made your yoke heauie, but I wil adde to your yoke: my father bette you with whippes, but I wil beate you with ſcorpions.
\v 15 And the king condeſcended not to the people: becauſe our Lord was turned away from him, \f a \fr 12.15 \ft This phraiſe noteth the ſequel, not the final cauſe. As \+it chap. 14. v. 9\+it*.\f* that he might rayſe vp his word, which he had ſpoken in the hand of Ahias the Silonite, to Ieroboam the ſonne of Nabat.
\v 16 The people therfore ſeing that the king would not heare them, anſwered him, ſaying: What part haue we in Dauid? or what inheritance in the ſonne of Iſai? Goe into thy tabernacles Iſrael, now ſee to thy houſe Dauid. And Iſrael went into their tabernacles.
\v 17 But ouer the children of Iſrael, whoſoeuer dwelt in the cities of Iuda, Roboam reigned.
\v 18 King Roboam therfore ſent Aduram, who was ouer the tributes: and al Iſrael ſtoned him, and he died, moreouer King Roboam in haſt went vp into his chariote, and fled into Ieruſalem:
\v 19 and Iſrael reuolted from the houſe of Dauid, vntil this preſent day.
\v 20 And it came to paſſe when al Iſrael had heard, that Ieroboam was returned, they ſent, and called him, an aſſemblie being gathered, and they made him king ouer al Iſrael, neither did any man folow the houſe of Dauid beſide the tribe of Iuda onlie.
\v 21 And Roboam came to Ieruſalem, and gathered together al the houſe of Iuda, and the tribe of Benjamin, an hundred foureſcore thouſand choſen men warriers, to fight agaynſt the houſe of Iſrael, and to reduce the kingdom to Roboam the ſonne of Salomon.
\v 22 But the word of our Lord came to Semeias the man of God, ſaying:
\v 23 Speake to Roboam the ſonne of Salomon, the king of Iuda, and to al the houſe of Iuda, and Beniamin, and the reſt of the people, ſaying:
\v 24 Thus ſayth our Lord: You shal not goe vp, neither ſhal you fight agaynſt your brethren the children of Iſrael: let euerie man returne into his houſe, for this word is done by me. They heard the word of our Lord, and returned from their iourney as our Lord had commanded them.
\v 25 And Ieroboam built Sichem in mount Ephraim, and dwelt there: and departing thence he built Phanuel.
\v 26 And Ieroboam ſayd in his hart: Now wil the kingdom returne to the houſe of Dauid,
\v 27 if this people shal goe vp to make ſacrifices in the houſe of our Lord into Ieruſalem: and the hart of this people wil be turned to their lord Roboam the king of Iuda, and they wil kil me, and returne to him.
\v 28 And finding out \f b \fr 12.28 \ft A diuelish policie to make a religion conformable to the temperal ſtate.\f* a deuiſe he made two golden calues, and ſayd to them: Goe vp no more into Ieruſalem: Behold thy goddes Iſrael, which brought thee out of the Land of Ægypt.
\v 29 And he put one in Bethel, and the other in Dan:
\v 30 and this thing was an occaſion of ſinne: for the people went to adore the calfe, as farre as Dan.
\v 31 And he made temples in the excelſes, and prieſtes \f c \fr 12.31 \ft For ſuch a religion ſuch prieſtes were fitteſt.\f* of the abiectes of the people, which were not of the children of Leui.
\v 32 And he appoynted a ſolemne day in the eight moneth, the fiftenth day of the moneth, after the ſimilitude of the ſolemnitie, that was celebrated in Iuda. And going vp he made in like maner an altar in Bethel, to immolate to the calues, which he had framed: and he ordayned in Bethel prieſtes of the \f d \fr 12.32 \ft Places on hilles, where they ſacrificed calues, and other thinges to the images of calues.\f* excelſes, which he had made.
\v 33 And he went vpon the altar, which he had built in Bethel, the fiftenth day of the eight moneth, which he had forged out of his owne hart: and he made a ſolemnitie to the children of Iſrael, and went vp vpon the altar, to burne incenſe.
\c 13
\cd A prophet ſent from Iuda to Bethel fortelleth the birth of Ioſias, and deſtruction of Ieroboams altar, 4. whoſe hand being ſudenly withered, 6. is reſtored by the prophets prayer. 11. The ſame prophet is deceiued by an other prophet, and ſlaine by a lion. 33. Hieroboam proceedeth in impietie.
\p
\v 1 AND behold a man of God came out of Iuda, in the word of our Lord into Bethel, Ieroboam \f a \fr 13.1 \ft This fore-ſhewing long before the name of a childe that ſhould be borne, importeth that he should do great thinges. \+it See. 4. Reg. 22\+it*.\f* ſtanding vpon the altar, and cenſing.
\v 2 And he cried out againſt the altar in the word of our Lord, and ſayd: Altar, altar, thus ſaith our Lord: Behold a child shal be borne to the houſe of Dauid, named Ioſias, and he shal immolate vpon thee prieſtes of the excelſes, which now doe burne frankencenſe on thee, and he shal burne mens bones vpon thee.
\v 3 And he gaue a ſigne in that day, ſaying: This ſhal be the ſigne, that our Lord hath ſpoken: Behold the altar shal be clouen, and the ashes shal be powred out in it.
\v 4 And when the king had heard the word of the man of God, which he cried out againſt the altar in Bethel, he ſtretched forth his hand from the altar, ſaying: Take him. And his hand withered, which he ſtretched forth agaynſt him: neither was he able to draw it backe vnto him.
\v 5 The altar alſo was clouen, and the ashes were powred out of the altar, according to the ſigne which the man of God had told before in the word of our Lord.
\v 6 And the king ſaid to the man of God: Beſech the face of our Lord thy God, and pray for me, that my hand may be reſtored me. And the man of God beſought the face of our Lord, and the kinges hand was reſtored to him, and it became as it was before.
\v 7 And the king ſpake to the man of God: Come home with me, that thou mayſt dyne, and I wil geue the giftes.
\v 8 And the man of God anſwered the king: If thou wouldeſt geue me the halfe part of thy houſe, I wil not come with thee, nor eate bread, nor drinke water in this place:
\v 9 for ſo was it enioyned in the word of our Lord cõmanding: Thou ſhalt not eate bread nor drinke water, nor returne by the way that thou cameſt.
\v 10 He departed therfore by an other way, and returned not by the way, that the came into Bethel.
\v 11 And a certaine prophete being old dwelt in Bethel, to whom his ſonnes came and told him al the workes, that the man of God had done that day in Bethel: and the wordes which he had ſpoken to the king, they told their father.
\v 12 And their father ſayd to them: What way went he? His ſonnes shewed him the way, by which the man of God was gone, which came out of Iuda.
\v 13 And he ſaid to his ſonnes: Sadle me and aſſe. Who when they had ſadled it, he got vp,
\v 14 and went after the man of God, and found him ſitting vnder a terebinth: and he ſaid to him: Art thou the man of God that cameſt out of Iuda? He anſwered: I am he.
\v 15 And he ſayd to him: Come home with me, that thou mayſt eate bread.
\v 16 Who ſayd: I can not returue, nor come with thee, neither wil I eate bread, nor drinke water in this place:
\v 17 becauſe our Lord ſpake to me in the word of our Lord, ſaying: Thou shalt not eate bread, and thou shalt not drinke water there, nor returne by the way thou wenteſt.
\v 18 Who ſayd to him: I alſo am a prophet like to thee: and \f b \fr 13.18 \ft This man of Bethel was in deede a prophet of God, but in this lied wickedly, and ſo deceiuing the other prophet, made him to breake Gods commandment, for which he was ſlaine. VVhervpon Hieroboam (whom the wiked prophet ſought to pleaſe) was leſſe afeard to procede in idolatrie.\f* an Angel hath ſpoken to me in the word of our Lord, ſaying: Bring him backe wirh thee into thy houſe, that he may eate bread, and drinke water. He deceiued him,
\v 19 and brought him backe with him: he did eate therfore bread in his houſe, and drunke water.
\v 20 And when they ſate at the table, the word, of our Lord came to the prophete, that brought him backe.
\v 21 And he cried out to the man of God, which came out of Iuda, ſaying: Thus ſayth our Lord: Becauſe thou haſt not beene obedient to the mouth of our Lord, and haſt not kept the commandment, which our Lord thy God commanded thee,
\v 22 and haſt returned, and eaten bread, & drunke water in the place wherin he commanded thee that thou shouldeſt not eate bread, nor drinke water, thy dead bodie ſhal not be brought into the ſepulchre of thy fathers.
\v 23 And when he had eaten & drunke, he ſadled his aſſe for the prophet, whom he brought backe.
\v 24 Who when he was gone, a lion found him in the way, and \f c \fr 13.24 \ft Not only the deceiuer, but alſo he that is deceiued, is guiltie and punishable for breaking Gods commandment.\f* killed him, and his bodie was caſt forth in the way: and the aſſe ſtood by him, and the lion ſtood by the dead bodie.
\v 25 And behold, men paſſing by ſaw the dead bodie caſt in the way, and the lion ſtanding beſide the bodie. And they came and diuulged it in the citie, wherein that old prophet dwelt,
\v 26 Which when that prophet heard, which had brought him backe out of the way, he ſayd: It is the man of God, that was diſobedient to the mouth of our Lord, and our Lord hath deliuered him to the lion, & he hath torne him, and killed him according to the word of our Lord, that he ſpake to him.
\v 27 And he ſayd to his ſonnes: Sadle me an aſſe. Who when they had ſadled,
\v 28 and he was gone, he found his dead bodie caſt forth in the way, and the aſſe and the lion ſtanding by the corſe: the lion \f d \fr 13.28 \ft By this it appeareth to be Gods worke and punishment.\f* did not eate of the dead bodie, nor hurt the aſſe.
\v 29 The prophet therfore tooke the corſe of the man of God, and layd it vpon the aſſe, and returning brought it into the citie of the old prophete, that they might mourne for him.
\v 30 And he layd his corſe in his owne ſepulchre: and they mourned for him: Alas, alas my brother.
\v 31 And when they had mourned for him, he ſayd to his ſonnes: When I shal be dead, burie me in the ſepulchre, wherein the man of Gcd is buried: beſide his bones lay my bones.
\v 32 For aſſuredly the word shal come to paſſe, which he hath foretold in the word of our Lord agaynſt the altar that is in Bethel: and agaynſt al the temples of the excelſes, that are in the cities of Samaria.
\v 33 After theſe wordes Ieroboam returned not from his wicked way: but on the contrarie part he made of the moſt abiect of the people prieſtes of the excelſes: Whoſoeuer would, he filled his hand, and he was made a prieſt of the excelſes.
\v 34 And for this cauſe did the houſe of Ieroboam ſinne, and was ouerthrowen, and deſtroyed from the face of the earth.
\c 14
\cd Abias the prophet forsheweth the ruine of Hieroboams familie: 12. namely the death of his ſonne, for whom being ſick, the mother conſulteth the prophet. 20. Hieroboam dieth, and his ſonne Nadab reigneth. 21. Some alſo of the people of Iuda, committing idolatrie and other ſinnes, 25. the King of Ægypt inuadeth and ſacketh Hieruſalem. 31. Roboam dieth and his ſonne Abias reigneth.
\p
\v 1 AT that time Abia the ſonne of Ieroboam was ſicke.
\v 2 And Ieroboam ſayd to his wife: Ariſe, and change thy habite, that thou be not knowen to be the wife of Ieroboam, and goe into Silo, where Ahias the prophete is, which ſpake to me, that I should reigne ouer this people.
\v 3 Take alſo in thy hand ten loaues, and crackneles, and a veſſel of honie, and goe to him: for he wil shew thee what shal happen to this childe.
\v 4 The wife of Ieroboam did as he had ſpoken: and riſing vp went into Silo, and came into the houſe of Ahias: but he could not ſee, becauſe his eies were dimme for age.
\v 5 And our Lord ſayd to Ahias: Behold the wife of Ieroboam cometh in, to conſult thee concerning her ſonne that is ſicke: thus and thus shalt thou ſpeake to her. When ſhe therfore entered in, and diſſembled to be that she was,
\v 6 Ahias heard the ſound of her feete entring in at the doore, and ſayd: Come in Ieroboams wife: Why doeſt thou fayne thy ſelf to be an other woman? But I am ſent to thee a heauie meſſenger.
\v 7 Goe, and tel Ieroboam: Thus ſayth our Lord the God of Iſrael: Becauſe I haue exalted thee out of the middes of the people, and made thee prince ouer my people Iſrael:
\v 8 and haue rent the kingdom of the houſe of Dauid, and geuen it to thee, and thou haſt not beene as my ſeruant Dauid, who kept my commandementes, and folowed me in al his hart, doing that which was wel liked in my ſight:
\v 9 but haſt wrought euil aboue al, that haue beene before thee, and haſt made thee ſtrange and molten goddes, \f a \fr 14.9 \ft Ieroboam did not wittingly and of purpoſe ſet vp falſe goddes, to the end he might prouoke God to anger: for his intention only was to kepe the people from going to Ieruſalem, leſt by that occaſion \+it they should returne to Roboam their Lord, king of Iuda. ch. 12. v. 27\+it*. But by ſetting vp idols he did prouoke God conſequently to anger. So here and in other places this phraſe: \+it that he might prouoke: that it might be fulfilled\+it*, and the like, ſignifieth not the final cauſe, but the ſequele of other factes, without direct intention.\f* that thou mighteſt prouoke me to anger, and haſt reiected me behind thy backe:
\v 10 therfore behold I wil bring in euils vpon the houſe of Ieroboam, and wil ſtrike of Ieroboam him that pyſſeth to the wal, and the incloſed, and the vileſt in Iſrael: and I wil cleanſe the remaynes of the houſe of Ieroboam, as dung is wont to be cleanſed til al be pure.
\v 11 They that shal die of Ieroboam in the citie, them the dogges ſhal eate: and they that shal die in the field, them the foules of the ayre shal deuoure: becauſe our Lord hath ſpoken.
\v 12 Thou therfore ariſe, and goe into thy houſe: and in the verie entrance of thy feete into the citie, the childe shal die,
\v 13 and al Iſrael shal mourne for him, and shal burie him: for this onlie of Ieroboam shal be brought into the ſepulchre, becauſe vpon him hath beene found a good word from our Lord the God of Iſrael, in the houſe of Ieroboam.
\v 14 And our Lord wil appoynt to him ſelf a king ouer Iſrael, that shal ſtrike the houſe of Ieroboam in this day, and in this time:
\v 15 and our Lord the God of Iſrael shal ſtrike it, as a reede is wont to be moued in the water: and he shal plucke out Iſrael from this good countrie, which he gaue to their fathers, and shal ſcatter them ouer the Riuer: becauſe they haue made to them ſelues groues, to prouoke our Lord.
\v 16 And our Lord shal deliuer Iſrael for the ſinnes of Ieroboam, who hath ſinned, & made Iſrael to ſinne.
\v 17 The wife therfore of Ieroboam aroſe, and departed, and came into Therſa: and when she entered the threshold of the houſe, the childe died,
\v 18 and they buried him. And al Iſrael mourned for him according to the word of our Lord, which he ſpake in the hand of his ſeruant Ahias the prophete.
\v 19 But the reſt of the wordes of Ieroboam, how he fought, and how he reigned, behold they are written in the Booke of the wordes of the daies of the kinges of Iſrael.
\v 20 And the daies, that Ieroboam reigned, are two and twentie yeares: and he ſlept with his fathers: and Nadab his ſonne reigned for him.
\v 21 Moreouer Roboam the ſonne of Salomon reigned in Iuda: one and fourtie yeares old was Roboam when he began to reigne: ſeuentene yeares reigned he in Ieruſalem the citie, which our Lord choſe to put his name there, of al the tribes of Iſrael. And his mothers name was Naama an Ammonite.
\v 22 And Iudas did euil before our Lord, and prouoked him aboue al thinges, that their fathers had done, in their ſinnes which they ſinned.
\v 23 For they alſo built them altars, and ſtatues, and groues vpon euerie high hil, and vnder euerie tree ful of grene leaues:
\v 24 yea and effeminates were in the land, and they did al the abominations of the gentiles, which our Lord deſtroyed before the face of the children of Iſrael.
\v 25 And in the fifth yeare of the reigne of Roboam, Seſac the king of Ægypt came vp into Ieruſalem,
\v 26 and tooke the treaſures of the houſe of our Lord, and the kinges treaſures, and al thinges he ſpoyled: the shieldes alſo of gold, which Salomon had made:
\v 27 for the which Roboam made braſen shieldes, & deliuered them into the hand of the captaynes of shield bearers, and of them that kept watch before the doore of the kinges houſe.
\v 28 And when the king went into the houſe of our Lord, they that had the office to goe before, caried them: and afterward they recaried them to the armourie of the shieldbearers.
\v 29 And the reſt of the wordes of Roboam, & al that he did, behold they are written in the Booke of the wordes of the daies of the kinges of Iuda.
\v 30 And there was warre betwen Roboam and Ieroboam alwaies.
\v 31 And Roboam ſlept with his fathers, and was buried with them in the citie of Dauid: and his mothers name was Naama an Ammonite: and Abias his ſonne reigned for him.
\c 15
\cd Abias reigneth wickedly in Iuda three yeares. 8. After him his ſonne Aſa ſucceding deſtroyeth idolatrie, reigning fourtie one yeares. 16. VVho hauing warres with the king of Iſrael, maketh league with the king of Syria, 24. Aſa dying Ioſaphat ſuccedeth. 25. Nadab reigneth wickedly two yeares in Iſrael, is then ſlaine by Baaſa of the tribe of Iſſachar, 29. and his whole familie is deſtroyed. 33. Baaſa alſo reigneth wickedly twentie foure yeares.
\p
\v 1 THERFORE in the eightenth yeare of king Ieroboam the ſonne of Nabat, Abias reigned ouer Iuda.
\v 2 Three yeares reigned he in Ieruſalem: the name of his mother was Maacha the daughter of Abeſſalom.
\v 3 And he walked in al the ſinnes of his father, which he had done before him: neither was his hart perfect with our Lord his God, as the hart of Dauid his father.
\v 4 But for Dauids ſake our Lord his God gaue him a lampe in Ieruſalem, that he might rayſe vp his ſonne after him, and eſtablish Ieruſalem:
\v 5 \f a \fr 15.5 \ft Dauids poſteritie conſerued for his ſake.\f* becauſe Dauid had done right in the eies of our Lord, and had not declined from al thinges, which he commanded him, al the daies of his life, except the matter of Vrias the Hetheite.
\v 6 But there was warre betwen Roboam and Ieroboam al the time of his life.
\v 7 And the reſt of the wordes of Abias, and al that he did, are they not written in the Booke of the wordes of the kinges of Iuda? And there was warre betwen Abias and Ieroboam.
\v 8 And Abias ſlept with his fathers, and they buried him in the citie of Dauid: and Aſa his ſonne reigned for him.
\v 9 In the twenteth yeare therfore of Ieroboam the king of Iſrael reigned Aſa the king of Iuda.
\v 10 And he reigned one & fourtie yeares in Ieruſalem. His mothers name was Maaca, the daughter of Abeſſalom.
\v 11 And Aſa did right before the ſight of our Lord, as Dauid his father:
\v 12 and he tooke away the effemmate out of the land, and he purged al the filth of the idols, which his fathers had made.
\v 13 Moreouer he remoued alſo Maaca his mother, that she ſhould not be princeſſe in the ſacrifices of Priapus, and in the groue which she had conſecrated: and he deſtroyed her denne, and brake the moſt filthie idol, and burnt it in the torrent cedron:
\v 14 but \f b \fr 15.14 \ft Thoſe altares which Salomon had made for his wiues, that were idolaters Aſa deſtroyed not. but al which Roboam and Abias had made, or ſuffered to be made for their owne people he pulled downe. Ioſias afterward deſtroyed alſo thoſe which Salomon had made. \+it 2. Paral\+it*, 34.\f* the excelſes he did not take away. Otherwiſe the hart of Aſa was parfect with our Lord al his daies:
\v 15 and he caried in thoſe thinges, which his father had ſanctified, and vowed into the houſe of our Lord, ſiluer and gold, and veſſels
\v 16 And there was warre betwen Aſa, and Baaſa the king of Iſrael al their daies.
\v 17 Baaſa alſo the king of Iſrael went vp into Iuda, and built Rama, that no man might go out or come in of Aſaes ſide the king of Iuda.
\v 18 Aſa therfore taking al the ſiluer, and gold that remained in the treaſures of the houſe of our Lord, and in the treaſures of the kinges houſe, gaue it into the handes of his ſeruantes: and he ſent to Benadad the ſonne of Tabremon the ſonne of Hezion, the king of Syria, which dwelt in Damaſcus, ſaying:
\v 19 There is a league betwen me and thee, & betwixt my father and thy father: therfore I haue ſent thee giftes, ſiluer and gold: and I deſire thee that thou come, and make void the league, that thou haſt with Baaſa the king of Iſrael, and he may retire from me.
\v 20 Benadad agreing to king Aſa, ſent the princes of his armie into the citiees of Iſrael, and they ſtroke Ahion, and Dan, and Abeldomum of Maacha, and al Cenneroth, to witte, al the Land of Nephthali.
\v 21 Which when Baaſa had heard, he intermitted to build Rama, and returned into Therſa.
\v 22 But king Aſa ſent word into al Iuda, ſaying: Let no man be excuſed. and they tooke ſtones from Rama, and the timber therof, wherwith Baaſa had built, and Aſa of it built GabaaBeniamin, and Maſpha.
\v 23 But the reſt of al the wordes of Aſa, and al his forces, and al that he did, & the cities that he built, are not theſe written in the Booke of the words of the dayes of the kinges of Iuda? How beit in the time of his old age he was diſeaſed in his feete.
\v 24 And he ſlept with his fathers, & was buried with them in the citie of Dauid his father. And Ioſaphat his ſonne reigned for him.
\v 25 But Nadab the ſonne of Ieroboam reigned ouer Iſrael the ſecond yeare of Aſa the king of Iuda: and he reigned ouer Iſrael two yeares.
\v 26 And he did that which is euil in the ſight of our Lord, and walked in the waies of his father, and in his ſinnes, wherwith he made Iſrael to ſinne.
\v 27 And Baaſa the ſonne of Ahias of the houſe of Iſſachar, lay in wayte againſt him, and ſtroke him in Gebbethon, which is a citie of the Philiſtimes: for Nadab and al Iſrael beſieged Gebbethon.
\v 28 Baaſa therfore ſlew him in the third yeare of Aſa the king of Iuda, and reigned for him.
\v 29 And when he reigned, he ſtroke \f c \fr 15.29 \ft The anthour of ſchiſme punished in his poſteritie.\f* al the houſe of Ieroboam: he leaft not ſo much as one ſoule of his ſeede, til he deſtroied him according to the word of our Lord, which he had ſpoken in the hand of Ahias the Silonite;
\v 30 for the ſinnes of Ieroboam, which he had ſinned, and wherwith he had cauſed Iſrael to ſinne, and for the offence, wherwith he prouoked our Lord the God of Iſrael.
\v 31 But the reſt of the wordes of Nadab, and al that he wrought, are not theſe thinges written in the Booke of the wordes of the daies of the kinges of Iſrael?
\v 32 And there was warre betwen Aſa and Baaſa the king of Iſrael al their daies.
\v 33 In the third yeare of Aſa the king of Iuda, reigned Baaſa the ſonne of Ahias, ouer al Iſrael in Therſa foure and twentie yeares.
\v 34 And he did euil before our Lord, & walked in the waies of Ieroboam, and in his ſinnes, wherwith he made Iſrael to ſinne.
\c 16
\cd Iehu for prophecying the deſtruction of Baaſa and his houſe, 7. is ſlaine. 8. yet his ſonne Ela reigneth two yeares. 9. Then Zambri rebelleth, killeth Ela, and reigneth. 16. Part of the people chooſing Amri (prince of the armie) their king, 18. Zambri deſperatly burneth himſelfe and the kings palace, 21. an other part folow Thebni as king til his death. 23. Amri reigneth tvvelue yeares wickedly. 29. His ſonne Achab ſuccedeth, marieth Iezabel, and ſerueth Baal. 34. In the meane time Hiel repairerh Iericho.
\p
\v 1 AND the word of our Lord came to Iehu the ſonne of Hanani agaynſt Baaſa, ſaying:
\v 2 For ſo much as I haue exalted thee out of the duſt, & ſette thee duke ouer my people Iſrael, but thou haſt walked in the way of Ieroboam, and haſt made my people Iſrael to ſinne, that thou mighteſt anger me with their ſinnes:
\v 3 behold, I wil cut downe the poſteritie of Baaſa, and the poſteritie of his houſe, and I wil make thy houſe as the houſe of Ieroboam the ſonne of Nabat.
\v 4 Whoſoeuer of Baaſa shal die in the citie, him shal the dogges eate: and whoſoeuer of his shal die in the countrie, him shal the fowles of the ayre deuoure.
\v 5 But the reſt of the wordes of Baaſa, and whatſoeuer he did, and his battels, are not theſe things written in the Booke of the wordes of the daies of the kinges of Iſrael?
\v 6 Baaſa therfore ſlept with his fathers, and was buried in Therſa: and Ela his ſonne reigned for him.
\v 7 And when the word of our Lord came in the hand of Iehu the ſonne of Hanani the prophete agaynſt Baaſa, and agaynſt his houſe, and agaynſt al the euil, that he had done before our Lord, to anger him in the workes of his handes, that it should be made as the houſe of Ieroboam: for this cauſe he ſlew him, that is to ſay, Iehu the ſonne of Hanani, the prophete.
\v 8 In the ſixe and twenteth yeare of Aſa the king of Iuda, reigned Ela the ſonne of Baaſa ouer Iſrael in Therſa two yeares.
\v 9 And his ſeruant Zambri rebelled agaynſt him, the captayne of the halfe part of the horſemen: and Ela was in Therſa drinking, and dronken in the houſe of Arſa the gouernour of Therſa.
\v 10 Zambri therfore rushing in, ſtroke and ſlew him in the ſeuen and twenteth yeare of Aſa the king of Iuda, & he reigned for him.
\v 11 And when he teigned, and ſate vpon his throne, he ſtroke al the houſe of Baaſa, and he leaft not of it one that could pyſſe agaynſt a wal, & his kinsfolke and frendes.
\v 12 And Zambri deſtroyed al the houſe of Baaſa, according to the word of our Lord, that he had ſpoken to Baaſa in the hand of Iehu the prophet,
\v 13 for al the ſinnes of Baaſa, and the ſinnes of Ela his ſonne, who ſinned, and made Iſrael to ſinne, prouoking our Lord the God of Iſrael in their vanities.
\v 14 But the reſt of the wordes of Ela, and al that he did, are not theſe writen in the Booke of the wordes of the daies of the kinges of Iſrael?
\v 15 In the ſeuen and twenteth yeare of Aſa the king of Iuda, reigned Zambri ſeuen daies in Therſa: moreouer the armie beſieged Gebbethon a citie of the Philiſthines.
\v 16 And when they heard that Zambri had rebelled, and ſlayne the king, \f a \fr 16.16 \ft Al thoſe that were in the campe choſe their general to be their king and preuailed therin: though an other half of Iſrael choſe and folowed an other for a time.\f* al Iſrael made Amri their king, who was General of the warfare ouer Iſrael that day, in the campe.
\v 17 Amri therfore went vp, and al Iſrael with him from Gebbethon, and they beſieged Therſa.
\v 18 And Zambri ſeing that the citie should be taken, he went into the palace, and burnt him ſelf with the kinges houſe: and he died
\v 19 in his ſinnes, which he had ſinned doing euil before our Lord, and walking in the way of Ieroboam, and in his ſinne; wherwith he made Iſrael to ſinne.
\v 20 But the reſt of the wordes of Zambri, and of his treaſon, and tyrannie, are not theſe thinges writen in the Booke of the wordes of the dayes of the kinges of Iſrael?
\v 21 Then was the people of Iſrael diuided into two partes: the halfe part of the people folowed Thebni the ſonne of Gineth, to make him king: and the halfe part Amri.
\v 22 But the people that was with Amri, preuayled ouer the people that folowed Thebni the ſonne of Gineth: and Thebni died, and Amri reigned.
\v 23 In \f b \fr 16.23 \ft Thebni being then dead he reigned peaceably for he began his reigne the 27. yeare of Aſa. \+it v. 15. & 16\+it*. and reigned in al 12. yeares.\f* the one and thirteth yeare of Aſa the king of Iuda Amri reigned ouer Iſrael, twelue yeares: in Therſa he reigned ſix yeares.
\v 24 And he bought the mount of Samaria of Somer for two talentes of ſiluer: and he built it, and he called the citie which he had built, by the name of Semer the lord of the mount of Samaria.
\v 25 And Amri did euil in the ſight of our Lord, and wrought wickedly aboue al, that were before him.
\v 26 And he walked in al the way of Ieroboam the ſonne of Nabat, and in his ſinnes wherwith he made Iſrael to ſinne: that they might anger our Lord the God of Iſrael in theit vanities.
\v 27 Bur the reſt of the wordes of Amri, and the battels he made, are not theſe thinges writen in the Booke, of the battels that he made are not theſe thinges written in the Booke of the wordes of the daies of the kinges of Iſrael?
\v 28 And Amri ſlept with his fathers, and was buried in Samaria and Achab his ſonne reigned for him.
\v 29 But Achab the ſonne of Amri reigned ouer Iſrael the eight and thirteth yeare of Aſa the king of Iuda. And Achab the ſonne of Amri reigned ouer Iſrael in Samiaria two and twentie yeares.
\v 30 And Achab the ſonne of Amri did euil in the ſight of our Lord aboue al, that were before him.
\v 31 Neither did it ſuffice him that he walked in the ſinnes of Ieroboam the ſonne of Nabat: beſides he tooke to wife Iezabel the daughter of Ethbaul the king of the Sidonians. And he went, and ſerued Baal, and adored him.
\v 32 And he ſette an altar to Baal in the temple of Baal, which he had built in Samaria,
\v 33 and he planted a groue: and Achab added in his worke, prouoking our Lord the God of Iſrael aboue al the kinges of Iſrael, that were before him.
\v 34 In his daies Hiel of Bethel built Iericho: \f c \fr 16.34 \ft VVhen Hiel began to build Iericho, his eldeſt ſonne died, ſo the reſt ſucceſſiuely, that the laſt died when he finiſhed the building: becauſe God by the mouth of Ioſue had forbid the building therof.\f* in Abiram his firſt borne he founded it, and in Segub his laſt he ſette vp the gates therof: according to the word of our Lord, which he ſpake in the hand of Ioſue the ſonne of Nun. \x - \xt Ioſue. 6.\x*
\c 17
\cd Elias by his prayer shutteth the heauen from raining. 2. Is fed by a crovv. 8 and by a vvidovv of Sareptha. 13. vvhoſe potte of meale, and barrel of oyle diminisheth not. 17. Her ſonne dieth, and is rayſed to life.
\p
\v 1 AND Elias the Theſbite of the inhabiters of Galaad ſaid to Achab: Our Lord liueth the God of Iſrael, in whoſe ſight I ſtand, if there shal be theſe yearer dew and rayne, but according to the wordes of my mouth.
\v 2 And the word of our Lord came to him, ſaying:
\v 3 Depart from hence, and goe agaynſt the Eaſt, and be hidde in the Torrent carith, which is agaynſt Iordan,
\v 4 and there thou shalt drinke of the torrent: and I haue commanded the rauens that they feede thee there
\v 5 He therfore went, and did according to the word of our Lord: and when he was gone, he ſate in the Torrent carith, which is againſt Iordan.
\v 6 The rauens alſo brought him bread and flesh in the morning, in like maner bread and flesh in the euening, and he dranke of the torrent.
\v 7 \f 1 \fr 17.7 \ft The Epiſtle on Teuſday in the 2. weke of Lent.\f* But after certayne daies the torrent was dried: for it bad not rayned vpon the earth.
\v 8 Therfore the word of our Lord came to him, ſaying:
\v 9 Ariſe, and goe into Sareptha of the Sidonians, and thou shalt tarie there: for I haue commanded a wydow woman there to feede thee.
\v 10 He aroſe, and went into Sareptha. And when he was come to the gate of the citie, the wydow woman appeared to him gathering ſtickes, and he called her, and ſayd to her: Geue me a litle water in a veſſel, that I may drinke.
\v 11 And when she went to fetch it, he cried after her ſaying: Bring me alſo, I beſeche thee, a morſel of bread in thy hand.
\v 12 Who anſwered: Our Lord thy God liueth, I haue no bread, but ſo much meale in a potte as a hand can hold, and a litle oile in a veſſel: \f 1 \fr 17.12 \ft The epiſtle on Friday in the 4. weke of Lent.\f* behold I gather two ſtickes, that I may goe in, and dreſſe it for me and my ſonne, that we may eate, and die.
\v 13 To whom Elias ſayd: feare not, but goe, and doe as thou haſt ſayd: but firſt make for me of the ſame meale a litle harth cake, and bring it to me: and for thy ſelf and thy ſonne thou shalt make afterward.
\v 14 For thus ſayth our Lord the God of Iſrael: The potte of meale shal not fayle, nor the veſſel of oile be diminished vntil the day, wherin our Lord wil geue rayne vpon the face of the earth.
\v 15 Who went and did according to the word of Elias: and he did eate, and she, and her houſe: and from that day
\v 16 the pot of meale fayled not, and the veſſel of oyle was not diminished, according to the word of our Lord, which he ſpake in the hand of Elias.
\v 17 And it came to paſſe after theſe thinges, the ſonne of the woman, the goodwife of the houſe, fel ſicke, and the ſickeneſſe was verie vehement ſo that there remayned no breath in him.
\v 18 She therfore ſayd to Elias: What is to me and thee thou man of God? comeſt thou vnto me, that myne iniquities might be remembred, and thou mighteſt kil my ſonne?
\v 19 And Elias ſayd to her: Geue me thy ſonne. And he tooke him from her boſome, and caried him into the vpper chamber where him ſelf abode, and layd him vpon his bed.
\v 20 And he cried to our Lord, and ſayd: O Lord my God, \f a \fr 17.20 \ft To this queſtion (ſayth S. Auguſtin) the prophet anſwered in ſpirite: No. For God killed not this childe to afflict ſo good a mother, but to confirme her in true religion & comforte her by rayſing him from death. So Lazarus died not to remaine dead, but to be rayſed to life, for Gods more glorie. \+it Ioan. 11. v. 4. S. Aug. li. 2. q. 5. ad Simplician\+it*.\f* what, the widow alſo with whom I am after a ſort ſuſteyned, haſt thou afflicted, that thou wouldeſt kil her ſonne?
\v 21 And he ſtretched forth, & meaſured him ſelfe vpon the childe three tymes, & he cried to our Lord, and ſayd: O Lord my God, let the ſoule of this childe, I beſeche thee, returne into his bodie.
\v 22 And our Lord heard the voice of Elias: and the ſoule of the child returned into him, and he reuiued.
\v 23 And Elias tooke the childe, and brought him downe from the vpper chamber into the lower houſe, and deliuered him to his mother, and ſayd to her: Behold thy ſonne liueth.
\v 24 And the woman ſayd to Elias: Now, in this I haue knowen that thou art a man of God, and the word of our Lord in the mouth iſ true.
\c 18
\cd The third yeare of famine, Elias meeting the chief gouernour of Achabs houſe, hardly perſwadeth him to tel Achab, that he is preſent. 17. Achab blameth Elias; but Elias freely auoucheth that not he, but Achab trubleth Iſrael. 19. By a miracle foure hundred and fiftie falſprophetes are conuinced, 40. and are ſlaine. 41. Elias prayeth and it rayneth.
\p
\v 1 AFTER manie daies the word of our Lord came to Elias, the third yeare, ſaying: Goe, and shew thy ſelf to Achab, that I may geue rayne vpon the face of the earth.
\v 2 Elias therfore went to shew himſelf to Achab: and there was ſore famine in Samaria.
\v 3 And Achab called Abdias the gouernour of his houſe: and Abdias did feare our Lord very much.
\v 4 For when Iezabel killed the prophetes of our Lord, he tooke an hundred prophetes, and hid them by fiftie and fiftie in caues, and fed them with bread and water.
\v 5 Achab therfore ſayd to Abdias: Goe into the land to al the fountaynes of waters, and into al valleys, if perhaps we may finde graſſe, and ſaue the horſes and mules, and the beaſtes may not vtterly perish.
\v 6 And they diuided the countries betwen them, that they might goe circuite about them: Achab went one way, and Abdias an other way ſeuerally.
\v 7 And when Abdias was in the way, Elias mette him: who when he knew him, \f a \fr 18.7 \ft Abdias adored Elias as the prophete of God, and a holie man not with ciuil honour, for in the world Abdias was the greater perſon nor with diuine honour, for that had benne idolatrie. It was therfore religious honour, called \+it dulia\+it*, due to ſpiritual excellencie of Gods ſeruantes.\f* fel on his face, and ſayd: My lord, art not thou Elias?
\v 8 To whom he anſwered: I am. Goe, and tel thy lord: Elias is here.
\v 9 And he ſayd: What haue I ſinned, that thou deliuereſt me thy ſeruant into the hand of Achab, that he may kil me?
\v 10 Our Lord thy God liueth, there is no nation or kingdom, whither my lord hath not ſent to ſeeke thee: and al anſwering: He is not here: he adiured al kingdomes and nations, for that thou waſt not found.
\v 11 And now thou ſayeſt to me: Goe, and tel thy lord: Elias is here.
\v 12 And when I am departed from thee, the Spirit of our Lord wil carie thee into a place, that I know not: and I entering in shal tel Achab, and not finding thee, he wil kil me: and thy ſeruant feareth our Lord from his infancie.
\v 13 Hath it not beene told thee my lord, what I did when Iezabel killed the prophetes of our Lord, that I hid of the prophetes of our Lord an hundred men, by fiftie and fiftie in caues, and fed them with bread and water?
\v 14 And now thou ſayſt: Goe, and tel thy lord: Elias is here: that he may kil me?
\v 15 And Elias ſayd: The Lord of hoſtes liueth, before whoſe face I ſtand, this day wil I appeare to him.
\v 16 Abdias therfore went to meete Achab, and told him: and Achab came to meete Elias.
\v 17 And when he had ſeene him, he ſayd: Art thou he that doeſt truble Iſrael?
\v 18 And he ſayd: not I haue trubled Iſrael, but thou, and the houſe of thy father, who haue forſaken the commandmentes of our Lord, and haue folowed Baalim.
\v 19 Neuertheleſſe ſend now, and gather vnto me al Iſrael in the mount of Carmel, and the prophetes of Baal foure hundred fiftie, and the prophetes of the groues foure hundred, which eate of Iezabels table.
\v 20 Achab ſent to al the children of Iſrael, and gathered together the prophetes in the mount of Carmel.
\v 21 And Elias coming to al the people, ſayd: \f b \fr 18.21 \ft Such zelous expoſtulation is neceſſarie to al Neutrals in religion who are \+it neither hote nor cold, but luke warme\+it*. ſuch as Angels deteſt \+it Apoc. 3\+it*.\f* How long halt you on two ſides? If our Lord be God, folow him: but if Baal, folow him. And the people did not anſwer him a word.
\v 22 And Elias ſayd agayne to the people: I onlie remayne a prophete of our Lord: and the prophetes of Baal are foure hundred and fiftie men.
\v 23 Let two oxen be geuen vs, and let them chooſe to them ſelues one oxe, and cutting it into peeces let them lay it vpon wood, but put no fyre vnder: and I wil dreſſe the other oxe, and wil lay it vpon wood, and put no fire vnder.
\v 24 Inuocate ye the names of your goddes, & I wil inuocate the name of my Lord: and the God that shal heare by fyre, let the ſame be God. And al the people anſwering ſayd: A very good propoſition.
\v 25 Elias therfore ſayd to the prophetes of Baal: Chooſe you one oxe, and make it firſt, becauſe you are manie: & inuocate the names of your goddes, and put no fire vnder.
\v 26 Who when they had taken the oxe, which he gaue them, they dreſſed it: and they inuocated the name of Baal from morning vntil midday, ſaying: Baal heare vs. And there was no voice, nor any that anſwered: and they leaped ouer the altar, that they had made.
\v 27 And when it was now midday, Elias ieſted at them, ſaying: Crie with a lowder voice: for he is God, and perhaps he ſpeaketh, or is in his inne, or in the way or at the leaſt he ſlepeth, that he muſt be waked.
\v 28 They cried therfore with a lowd voice, and cut them ſelues after their rite with kniues and lancers, til they were al embrewed with bloud
\v 29 And after the midday was paſt, and whiles they prophecied, the time was come, when they vſed to offer ſacrifice, neither voice was heard, nor any did anſwer, nor attend them praying:
\v 30 Elias ſayd to al the people: Come vnto me. And the people coming to him, he repayred the altar of our Lord, that was deſtroyed.
\v 31 And he tooke twelue ſtones according to the number of the tribes of the children of Iacob, to whom the word of our Lord came, ſaying: Iſrael shal be thy name.
\v 32 And he built of the ſtones an altar in the name of our Lord: and he made a water gutter, as it were by two furrowes round about the altar,
\v 33 and he layed the wood in order, and diuided the oxe in iointes, and layd it vpon the wood,
\v 34 and ſaid: Fil foure buckettes with water, and powre vpon the holocauſte, and vpon the wood. And againe he ſayd: Doe it alſo the ſecond time. Who hauing donne it the ſecond time, he ſaid: The third time alſo doe the ſame. And they did ſo the third time,
\v 35 and the warers ranne about the altar, and the trough of the conduite was filled.
\v 36 And when it was now time that the holocauſte should be offered, Elias the prophete coming ſaid: Lord God of Abraham, and Iſaac, & Iſrael, shew this day that thou art the God of Iſrael, and I thy ſeruant, & that according to thy commandment I haue done al theſe thinges.
\v 37 Heare me Lord, heare me: \f c \fr 18.37 \ft When miracles are attempted for trial of the truth, the diuels powre is reſtrayned, & only the truth is teſtified: \+it Our Lord vvorking withal, & confirming the doctrin vvith ſignes folovving. Marc. vlt\+it*.\f* that this people may learne, that thou art our Lord God, & thou haſt conuerted their hart againe.
\v 38 And the fire of our Lord fel, & deuoured the holocauſte, & the wood, and the ſtones, licking alſo the duſt, and the water, that was in the water gutter.
\v 39 Which when al the people had ſeene, they fel on their face, and ſaid: Our Lord he is God, our Lord he is God.
\v 40 And Elias ſaid to them: Apprehend the prophetes of Baal, and let not one eſcape of them. Whom when they had taken, Elias brought them to the Torrent ciſon, and killed them there.
\v 41 And Elias ſayd to Achab: Goe vp, eate, and drinke: becauſe there is ſound of much raine.
\v 42 Achab went vp to eate and drinke: and Elias went vp into the toppe of Carmel, and flatte on the earth put his face betwen his knees,
\v 43 and he ſaid to his ſeruant: Goe vp, and looke toward the ſea. Who when he was gone vp, and had looked, he ſaid: There is nothing. And againe he ſaid to him: Returne ſeuen tymes.
\v 44 And in the ſeuenth time: Behold a litle cloud as it were a mans foote, came vp from the ſea. Who ſaid: Goe vp and ſay to Achab: Yoke thy chariote and goe downe, leſt the raine preuent thee.
\v 45 And when he turned him ſelfe hither and thither, behold the heauens were darkened, and cloudes, and winde, and there fel great raine. Achab therfore going vp went into Iezrahel:
\v 46 and the hand of our Lord was made vpon Elias, and his loynes girded he ranne before Achab, vntil he came into Iezrahel.
\c 19
\cd Elias fleing Iezabel, in the deſert eateth a harth cake and drinketh water, brought by an Angel, and ſo goeth fourtie dayes and nightes vnto mount Horeb. 9. Lamenting that he alone is leift of the prophetes of God. 15. God commandeth him to returne to Damaſcus, and anoint Hazael king of Syria, Iehu king of Iſrael, and Eliſeus a prophet: 18. and telleth him, there remaine ſeuen thouſand in Iſrael, which haue not bowed to Baal.
\p
\v 1 AND Achab told Iezabel al thinges that Elias had done, and how he had killed al the prophetes with the ſword.
\v 2 And Iezabel ſent a meſſenger to Elias, ſaying: Theſe thinges do the goddes to me, and theſe adde they, if this houre to morow I make not thy ſoule as the ſoule of one of them.
\v 3 Elias therfore was afraid, & ryſing he went whither ſoeuer his wil caried him: and he came into Berſabee of Iuda, and leaft his ſeruant there,
\v 4 and went forward into the deſert, one dayes iourney. And when he was come, and ſate vnder a iuniper tree, he deſired for his ſoule to die, and ſayd: It ſufficeth me Lord, take my ſoul: for I am not better then my fathers.
\v 5 And he caſt him ſelf downe, and ſlept in the shadow of the iuniper tree: and behold an Angel of our Lord touched him, and ſayd to him: Ariſe, and eate.
\v 6 He looked, and behold at his head harthbaked bread, and a veſſel of water: he therfore did eate, and drinke, and he ſlept againe.
\v 7 And the Angel of our Lord returned the ſecond time, and touched him, and ſaid to him: Ariſe, eate: for thou haſt yet a great way to goe.
\v 8 Who when he was riſen: did eate and drinke, & walked in the ſtrength of that meate fourtie daies, and fourtie nightes, vnto the mount of God, Horeb.
\v 9 And when he was come thither, he taried in a caue: and behold the word of our Lord vnto him, & he ſaid to him: What doeſt thou here Elias?
\v 10 But he anſwered: With zele haue I beene zelous for our Lord the God of hoſtes. becauſe the children of Iſrael haue forſaken thy couenant: thy altars haue they deſtroyed, & thy prophetes they haue ſlaine with the ſword, & \f a \fr 19.10 \ft Elias knew no other at that inſtant in the tenne tribes, that were not partakers in idolatrie; but there were in deede ſeuen thouſand \+it (v. 18\+it*.) that bowed not their knees to Baal. And the whole kingdom of Iuda obſerued true religion, where Aſa then reigned, and after him Ioſaphat: both good kinges, \+it chap. 15. v 11. 22. v. 43. 2. Par. 15. &. 17\+it*.\f* I alone am leaft, & they ſeeke my life to take it away.
\v 11 And he ſaid to him: Come forth, and ſtand in the mount before our Lord: and behold our Lord paſſeth, and a great winde, and ſtrong ouerthrowing mountaynes, and breaking rockes before our Lord: not in the winde is our Lord, and after the winde an earth quake: not in the earth quake is our Lord,
\v 12 and afrer the earth quake fire: not in the fire is our Lord, and after the fire a wiſtling of a gentle winde.
\v 13 Which when Elias had heard, he couered his face with his mantel, and coming forth ſtood in the doore of the caue, and behold a voyce vnto him, ſaying: What doeſt thou here Elias?
\v 14 And he anſwered: With zele haue I beene zelous for our Lord the God of hoſtes: becauſe the children of Iſrael haue forſaken thy couenant: thyn altars they haue deſtroyed, and thy prophetes they haue ſlaine with the ſword, and I alone am leaft, and they ſeeke my life to take it away.
\v 15 And our Lord ſayd to him: Goe, and returne into thy way by the deſert of Damiaſcus: and when thou art come thither thou \f b \fr 19.15 \ft Elias prophecied that theſe two should be kinges, and caſt his cloke ouer Elizeus. \+it v. 19\+it* Elizeus declared to Hazael. that he should be king. \+it 4. Reg 8\+it*. an other prophet annointed Iehu. \+it 4. Reg. 9\+it*.\f* shalt anoint Hazael king ouer Syria,
\v 16 and Iehu the ſonne of Namſi thou ſhalt anoynt king ouer Iſrael: and Eliſeus the ſonne of Saphat, which is of Abelmeula, thou shalt anoynt prophet for thee.
\v 17 And it shal be, whoſoeuer shal eſcape the ſword of Hazael, him Iehu shal kil: and whoſouer shal eſcape the ſword of Iehu, him shal Eliſeus kil.
\v 18 And I wil leaue me in Iſrael ſeuen thouſand men, whoſe knees haue not beene bowed before Baal, and euerie mouth, that hath not adored him kyſſing his handes.
\v 19 Elias therfore departing thence, found Eliſeus the ſonne of Saphat, ploughing with twelue yoke of oxen, and he was one of them that ploughed with twelue yoke of oxen: and when Elias came to him, he caſt his mantel vpon him.
\v 20 Who forth with leauing the oxen ranne after Elias, and ſaid: Let me kiſſe, I pray thee, my father, and my mother, and ſo I wil folow thee. And he ſayd to him: Goe, and returne: for that which was my part, I haue donne to thee.
\v 21 And returning from him. he tooke a yoke of oxen, and killed them, and ſod the flesh with the plough of the oxen, and gaue to the people, and they did eate: and ryſing vp he departed, and folowed Elias, & miniſtred to him.
\c 20
\cd The Syrians threatning and beſieging Samaria, 13. God ſignifieth by a prophet to Achab, that he shal haue the victorie. 20. Which he obtaineth. 23. Alſo the ſecond yeare fighting in the champaine. 31. But ſauing the king of Syrians life, and making league with him, 35. one of the children of the prophetes being ſlaine, for not ſtryking when he was ſo commanded, 37. an other denounceth reuenge to Achab, for not killing the Syrian King.
\p
\v 1 MOREOVER Benadad the king of Syria, gathered together al his hoſt, & two & thirtie kinges with him, & horſes, and chariotes: and going vp fought agaynſt Samaria, and beſieged it.
\v 2 And ſending meſſengers to Achab the king of Iſraal into the citie,
\v 3 he ſaid: Thus ſaith Benadad: Thy ſiluer, and thy gold is mine: and thy wiues, and thy principal children be mine.
\v 4 And the king of Iſrael anſwered: According to thy word my lord king, I am thine, and al that is mine.
\v 5 And the meſſengers returning, ſaid: Thus ſaith Benadad, which ſent vs vnto thee: Thy ſiluer, and thy gold, and thy wiues, and thy children thou shalt geue me.
\v 6 To morow therfore this verie houre I wil ſend my ſeruantes to thee, and they shal ſearch thy houſe, and the houſe of thy ſeruantes: and al that pleaſeth them, they shal put in their handes, and take away.
\v 7 And the king of Iſrael called al the ancientes of the land, and ſaid: Marke, and ſee that he ſeeketh to intrappe vs. for he ſent to me for my wiues, and children, and for the ſiluer and gold: and I ſaid not nay.
\v 8 And al the ancientes, and al the people ſaid to him: Heare not, neither agree vnto him.
\v 9 He therfore anſwered the meſſengers of Benadad: Tel my Lord the king: Al thinges for the which thou didſt ſend to me thy ſeruant in the beginning I wil doe: but this thing I can not doe.
\v 10 And the meſſengers returning made report vnto him, who ſent againe, and ſayd: Theſe thinges doe the goddes to me, and theſe adde they, if the duſt of Samaria shal ſuffice for the hanfulles of al the people, that foloweth me.
\v 11 And the king of Iſrael anſwering, ſayd: Tel him \f a \fr 20.11 \ft Not at the putting on of armour, is time to boaſt of victorie, but rather at putting it of. For thoſe that are girded, and ſtrongly armed do vn wiſely in truſting their owne ſtrength, and contemning their aduerſaries: for by ſo doing they are often ouerthrowne. And thoſe that diſtruſting them ſelues truſt in God, one way or other alwaies preuaile A neceſſarie leſſon, both in temporal and ſpiritual warfayre.\f* Let not the girded glorie as the vngirded.
\v 12 And it came to paſſe, when Benaded had heard this word, himſelf and the kinges dranke in pauilions, and he ſayd to his ſeruantes: Beſette the citie, and they did beſette it.
\v 13 And behold a prophete coming to Achab the king of Iſrael, ſaid to him: Thus ſayth our Lord; Haſt thou in dede ſene al this exceding great multitude? behold, I wil deliuer them into thy hand this day: that thou mayſt know, that I am the Lord.
\v 14 And Achab ſaid: By whom? And he ſaid to him: Thus ſaith our Lord: By the ſeruantes of the princes of the prouinces. And he ſaid: Who shal begin to fight? And he ſaid: Thou.
\v 15 He therfore muſtered the ſeruantes of the princes of the prouinces, and he found the number of two hundred thirtie two: and he muſtered after them the people, al the children of Iſrael, ſeuen thouſand.
\v 16 And they went forth at noone. But Benadad dranke al dronken in his tent, and two and thirtie kinges with him, which were come to ayde him.
\v 17 The ſeruantes therfore of the princes of the prouinces iſſued forth in the forefront. Benadad therfore ſent. Who told him, ſaying: Men are come forth out of Samaria.
\v 18 And he ſaid: Whether they come for peace, take them aliue: or els to fight, aliue take ye them.
\v 19 The ſeruantes therfore of the princes of the prouinces iſſued forth, and the reſt of the armie folowed:
\v 20 and euerie one ſtroke the man, that came againſt him: and the Syrians fled, and Iſrael purſued them. Benadad alſo the king of Syria fled on horſebacke with his horſemen.
\v 21 Moreouer the king of Iſrael iſſuing forth ſtroke the horſes and chariotes, and he ſtroke Syria with a great ſlaughter.
\v 22 And a prophet coming to the king of Iſrael, ſaid to him: Goe, and take courage, and know, and ſee what thou doeſt: for the yeare folowing the king of Syria wil come vp againſt thee. )
\v 23 But the ſeruantes of the king of Syria ſaid to him: The goddes of the mountaynes be their goddes, therfore haue they ouercome vs: but it is better that we fight againſt them in the champaine, & we shal ouercome them.
\v 24 Thou therfore do this word: Remoue al the kinges from thine armie, and put captaines for them:
\v 25 and repaire the number of ſouldiars, that are ſlaine of thine, and horſes according to the old horſes, & chariotes according to the chariotes, which thou hadſt before: and we wil fight againſt them in the champaine, and thou shalt ſee that we shal ouercome them. He beleued their counſel, and did ſo.
\v 26 Therfore after a yeare was paſſed, Benadad muſtered the Syrians, and went vp into Aphec, to fight againſt Iſrael.
\v 27 Moreouer the children of Iſrael were muſtered, and taking victuals they went forth, on the contrarie ſide, and camped againſt them, as it were two litle flockes of goates: but the Syrians filled the land.
\v 28 ( And a man of God coming, ſaid to the king of Iſrael: Thus ſaith our Lord: Becauſe the Syrians haue ſaid: The Lord is God of the mountaines, and is not God of the Valleis: I wil geue al this great multitude into thy hand, and \f b \fr 20.28 \ft Manie victories and other benefites were beſtowed vpon Achab, to make him knowe God: but he contemning them al remained in his impietie, and finally was ſlaine. \+it ch. 22. v. 38\+it*.\f* you ſhal know that I am the Lord. )
\v 29 And ſeuen dayes did theſe, and they direct their armies one againſt the other, and in the ſeuenth day was the battel fought: and the children of Iſrael ſtroke of the Syrians an hundred thouſand footemen in one day.
\v 30 And they that remained in Aphec, fled into the citie: and the wal fel vpon ſeuen and twentie thouſand men, that were leaft. Moreouer Benadad fleing entered the citie, into a chamber that was within a chamber,
\v 31 and his ſeruantes ſaid to him: Behold, we haue heard that the kinges of the houſe of Iſrael are merciful: Let vs therfore put ſackeclothes on our loynes, and cordes on our heades, and goe forth to the king of Iſrael: perhaps he wil ſaue our liues.
\v 32 They girded their loines with ſackclothes, and put cordes on their heades, and came to the king of Iſrael, and ſaid to him: Thy ſeruant ſaith: Let my ſoule liue, I beſech thee. And he ſaid: If he be yet aliue he is my brother.
\v 33 Which the men tooke for good lucke: and in haſt caught the word of his mouth, and ſaid: Thy brother Benadad. And he ſaid to them: Goe and bring him to me. Benadad therfore came out to him, and he lifted him vp into his chariote.
\v 34 Who ſaid to him: The cities which my father tooke from thy father, I wil render: doe thou make thee ſtretes in Damaſcus, as my father made in Samaria, and I confederate wil depart from thee. He therfore made a league, and dimiſſed him.
\v 35 Then a certaine man of the children of the prophetes ſayd to his felow in the word of our Lord: Strike me. But he would not ſtrike.
\v 36 To whom he ſaid: Becauſe thou wouldeſt not heare the word of our Lord, behold thou shalt depart from me, and a lion shal ſtrike thee. And when he was departed a litle from him, a lyon found him, and ſlew him.
\v 37 But finding alſo an other man, he ſaid to him: Strike me. Who ſtroke him, and wounded him.
\v 38 The prophete therfore went, and mette the king in the way, and with ſprinkling of duſt changed his face and his eies.
\v 39 And when the king paſſed by, he cried to the king, and ſaid: Thy ſeruant went forth to fight hand ſtrokes: and when a certaine man was fled, one brought him to me, and ſaid: Keepe this man: who if he shal ſlippe away, thy life shal be for his life, or thou shalt pay a talent of ſiluer.
\v 40 And whiles I being trubled turned hither and thither, ſodenly he appeared not. And the king of Iſrael ſayd to him: This is thy iudgement, which thy ſelf haſt decreed.
\v 41 But he forthwith wyped of the duſt from his face, and the king of Iſrael knew him, that he was of the prophetes.
\v 42 Who ſayd to him: Thus ſayth our Lord: \f c \fr 20.42 \ft Fooliſh pittie in ſparing a dangerous & common enimie is offenſiffe to God, & ſeuerely punished by his iuſtice.\f* Becauſe thou haſt let goe out of thy hand a man worthie to die, thy life shal be for his life, and thy people for his people.
\v 43 The king of Iſrael therfore returned into his houſe, contemning to heare, & raging came into Samaria.
\c 21
\cd Naboth for denying his vineyard to King Achab, is by quene Iezabels commandment, falſly accuſed, and ſtoned to death. 10. Achab haſtening to poſſeſſe the vineyard, Elias the prophet threatneth him manie euiles. 23. and no leſſe to Iezabel: both being obſtinate in ſinne. 27. Yet Achab for feare of pnnishment, doth external workes of penance, and therby eſcapeth part of the temporal plagues.
\p
\v 1 AND after theſe wordes, at that tyme Naboth the Iezrahelite had a vineyard, which was in Iezrahel, beſide the palace of Achab the king of Samaria.
\v 2 Achab therfore ſpake to Naboth, ſaying: Geue me thy vineyard, that I may make me a garden of herbes, becauſe it is nigh, and ioyning to my houſe, and \f a \fr 21.2 \ft If no ſubiect were lord of anie land, but only at the kings pleaſure & kinges were proper lordes of al the landes in their kingdomes, then Achab might haue taken Naboths vinyard; eſpecially geuing him a better, or money for it. Neither was it a vaine ſcruple in Naboth, to conſerue his anceſters inheritance. For both his denial is here iuſtified, and Achabs extortion condemned. \+it S. Ambros li. 3. offic. c. 9\+it*. counteth Naboth a Martyr, \+it See Annot. 1. Reg. 8\+it*.\f* I wil geue thee for it a better vineyard: or if thou thinke it more commodious for thee, the price of ſiluer, ſo much as it is worth.
\v 3 To whom Naboth anſwered: Our Lord be merciful to me, that I geue not the inheritance of my fathers to thee.
\v 4 Achab therfore came into his houſe with indignation, and fretting vpon the word, that Naboth the Iezrahelite had ſpoken to him, ſaying: I wil not geue thee the inheritance of my fathers. And caſting him ſelf vpon his bed, he turned away his face to the wal, and did not eate bread.
\v 5 And Iezabel his wife went in vnto him, and ſayd to him: What is this matter, whereupon thy ſoule is greued? and why eateſt thou not bread?
\v 6 Who anſwered her: I ſpake to Naboth the Iezrahelite, and ſayd to him: Geue me thy vineyard, taking money for it: or if it pleaſe thee, I wil geue thee a better vineyard for it. And he ſayd: I wil not geue thee my vineyard.
\v 7 Iezabel therfore his wife ſayd to him: Thou art of great authoritie, and doeſt wel gouerne the kingdome of Iſrael: Ariſe, and eate bread, and be of good cheere, I wil geue thee the vineyard of Naboth the Iezrahelite.
\v 8 She therfore wrote letters in the name of Achab, and ſigned them with his ring, and ſent to the ancientes, and the chiefe men that were in his citie, and dwelt with Naboth.
\v 9 And this was the tenor of the letters: Proclame a faſt, and make Naboth ſitte among the chiefe of the people,
\v 10 and ſuborne two men the children of Belial agaynſt him, and let them beare falſe teſtimony: that he hath \f b \fr 21.10 \ft To auoide horrore of blaſphemie holie ſcripture often vſeth the terme \+it bleſſing\+it* for \+it curſing\+it*.\f* bleſſed God and the king: and bring him forth, and ſtone him, and ſo let him die.
\v 11 His citizens therfore the ancientes and chiefe men, that dwelt with him in the citie, did as Iezabel had commanded them, and as it was written in the letters which she ſent to them:
\v 12 they proclamed a faſt, and made Naboth ſitte among the chiefe of the people.
\v 13 And two men the children of the diuel being brought forth, they made them ſitte agaynſt him: but they, as deuelish men, gaue teſtimonie agaynſt him before the multitude: Naboth hath bleſſed God and the king: for the which thing they brought him forth without the citie, and killed him with ſtones.
\v 14 And they ſent to Iezabel, ſaying: Naboth is ſtoned, and is dead.
\v 15 And it came to paſſe, when Iezabel had heard that Naboth was ſtoned, and dead, she ſpake to Achab: Ariſe, and poſſeſſe the vineyard of Naboth the Iezrahelite, who would not agree vnto thee, and geue it taking money: for Naboth liueth not, but is dead.
\v 16 Which when Achab had heard, to witte, that Naboth was dead, he aroſe, & went downe into the vineyard of Naboth the Iezrahelite, to poſſeſſe it.
\v 17 The word of our Lord therfore came to Elias the Theſbite, ſaying:
\v 18 Ariſe, and goe downe to meete Achab the king of Iſrael, who is in Samaria: behold he goeth downe to the vineyard of Naboth, to poſſeſſe it:
\v 19 and thou shalt ſpeake to him, ſaying: Thus ſayth our Lord: Thou haſt ſlayne, moreouer alſo thou haſt poſſeſt. And after theſe wordes thou shalt adde: Thus ſayth our Lord: In this place, wherein the dogges haue licked the bloud of Naboth, they \f 1 \fr 21.19 \ft \+it chap. 22. v. 38\+it*.\f* shal licke thy bloud alſo.
\v 20 And Achab ſayd to Elias: Haſt thou found me thyne enemie? Who ſayd: I haue found thee, for that thou art ſold, to doe euil in the ſight of our Lord.
\v 21 Behold I wil bring euil vpon thee, and wil cut downe thy poſteritie, and wil kil of Achab him that piſſeth agaynſt the wal, and the incloſed, and the laſt in Iſrael.
\v 22 And I wil make thy houſe, as the houſe of Ieroboam the ſonne of Nabat, and as the houſe of Baaſa the ſonne of Ahias: becauſe thou haſt done, to prouoke me to anger, and haſt made Iſrael to ſinne.
\v 23 But of Iezabel alſo our Lord ſpake, ſaying: The dogges shal eate Iezabel in the field of Iezrahel.
\v 24 If Achab die in the citie, the dogges shal eate him: but if he die in the field, the foules of the ayre shal eate him.
\v 25 Therfore there was not ſuch an other as Achab, who \f c \fr 21.25 \ft So addicted to wickednes, as if he had ſolde him ſelfe so the diuel. For ſome temporal profite, or pleaſure. \+it In your iniquities you are ſolde. Iſaiæ. 50. S. Aug. q. 102. ex vtro. teſt: S. Greg. ho. 10. in Ezech\+it*.\f* was ſold to doe euil in the ſight of our Lord: for his wife Iezabel ſette him on,
\v 26 and he became abominable, in ſo much that he folowed the idols, which the Amorrheites had made, whom our Lord conſumed before the face of the children of Iſrael.
\v 27 Therfore when Achab had heard theſe wordes, he rent his garmentes, and couered his flesh with haercloth, and faſted and ſlept in ſackcloth, and walked caſting downe his head.
\v 28 And the word of our Lord came to Elias the Theſbite, ſaying:
\v 29 Haſt thou not ſeene Achab humbled before me? therfore, becauſe he hath humbled him ſelf for my ſake, I wil not bring in the euil in his dayes, but in his ſonnes daies wil I bring the euil vpon his houſe.
\c 22
\cd Achab King of Iſrael conſulting and beleuing falſe prophetes rather then Micheas a true prophet, accompanied with Ioſaphat king of Iuda, reſolueth to fight againſt the Syrians for Ramoth Galaad. 26. Committeth Micheas to priſon, 29. procedeth to the warre, 34. is ſlaine, and Ochozias ſuccedeth. 41. Ioſaphat refuſeth to traffike longer with Ochozias; dieth, and his ſonne Ioram reigneth. 52. Ochozias foloweth the euil ſteppes of his parents.
\p
\v 1 THERE paſſed therfore three yeares without warre betwen Syria aud Iſrael.
\v 2 And in the third yeare, Ioſaphat the king of Iuda went downe to the king of Iſrael.
\v 3 (And the king of Iſrael ſayd to his ſeruantes: are you ignorant that Ramoth-Galaad is ours, and we neglect to take it out of the hand of the king of Syria?)
\v 4 And he ſayd to Ioſaphat: Wilt thou come with me to fight into Ramoth Galaad?
\v 5 And Ioſaphat ſayd to the king of Iſrael: As I am, ſo thou alſo: my people and thy people are one: and my horſemen thy horſmen. And Ioſaphat ſayd to the king of Iſraal: Aske, I beſech thee, this day the word of our Lord.
\v 6 The king of Iſrael therfore aſſembled the prophetes, about foure hundred men, and he ſayd to them: Shal I goe into Ramoth Galaad to fight, or ſitte ſtil? Who anſwered: Goe vp, and our Lord wil geue it into the kinges hand.
\v 7 And Ioſaphat ſayd: \f a \fr 22.7 \ft The godlie King Ioſaphat, iuſtly ſuſpecting the ſchiſmatieal falſe prophetes, aduiſed the other King to conſult a true prophet of God.\f* Is there not here ſome prophete of our Lord, that we may aske by him?
\v 8 And the king of Iſrael ſayd to Ioſaphat: There is one man leaft, by whom we may aske our Lord: but I hate him, becauſe he doeth not prophecie vnto me good, but euil, Micheas the ſonne of Iemla. To whom Ioſaphat ſayd: Speake not ſo ô king.
\v 9 The king of Iſrael therfore called a certaine eunuch, and ſayd to him: Make haſt, and bring hither Micheas the ſonne of Iemla.
\v 10 And the king of Iſrael, and Ioſaphat the king of Iuda ſate eche in his throne clothed with royal attyre, in a court beſide the doore of the gate of Samaria, and al the prophetes prophecied before them.
\v 11 Sedecias the ſonne of Chanaana made him \f b \fr 22.11 \ft Falſprophetes imitate true prophetes in ſome exterior thinges, to make their prophecies ſeme more authentical. Such hornes appeared in a prophetical viſion to Zacharie the prophet. \+it Zach. 1. v. 18\+it*.\f* ſelf hornes of yron, and ſayd: Thus ſayth our Lord: With theſe shalt thou ſtrike Syria, til thou deſtroy it.
\v 12 And al the prophetes in like maner prophecied, ſaying: Aſcend into Ramoth Galaad, and goe proſperouſely, & our Lord wil deliuer it into the Kinges handes.
\v 13 But the meſſenger, that went to cal Micheas, ſpake to him, ſaying: Behold the wordes of the prophetes with one mouth preach good thinges to the king: let thy word therfore be like to theirs, and ſpeake good thinges.
\v 14 To whom Micheas ſayd: Our Lord liueth, whatſoeuer our Lord shal tel me, that wil I ſpeake.
\v 15 He therfore came to the king, and the king ſayd to him: Micheas, shal we goe into Ramoth Galaad to fight, or ſitte ſtil? To whom he anſwered: \f c \fr 22.15 \ft The prophet geueth not this for a reſolute anſwer, but ſeing the king wil goe, he prayeth he may go proſperouſly. And the king conceiueth no otherwiſe of his anſwer, and therfore vrgeth him to anſwer reſolutly in the next wordes. \+it I adiure thee &c\+it*.\f* Aſcend, and goe proſperouſely, and our Lord wil deliuer it into the kinges handes.
\v 16 But the king ſayd to him: I adiure thee agayne and agayne, that thou ſpeake not to me but that which is true in the name of our Lord.
\v 17 And he ſayd: I ſaw al Iſrael diſperſed in the mountaynes, as sheepe not hauing a ſhepeheard, and our Lord ſayd: Theſe haue no maiſter: let euerie man returne into his houſe in peace.
\v 18 (Therfore the king of Iſrael ſayd to Ioſaphat: Did I not tel thee, that he doeth not prophecie me good, but alwaies euil?)
\v 19 But he adding, ſayd: Therfore heare the word of our Lord: I ſaw our Lord ſitting vpon his throne, and al the hoſte of heauen aſſiſting him on the right hand and on the left:
\v 20 and our Lord ſayd: Who shal deceiue Achab the king of Iſrael, that he may goe vp, and fal in Ramoth Galaad? And one ſayd theſe maner of wordes, and an other otherwiſe.
\v 21 And there came forth a ſpirit, and ſtood before our Lord, and ſayd: I wil deceiue him. To whom our Lord ſpake: Wherein?
\v 22 And he ſayd: I wil goe forth, and be a lying ſpirit in the mouth of al his prophetes. And our Lord ſayd: Thou shalt deceiue, and shalt preuayle: \f d \fr 22.22 \ft The coherence of the text sheweth that God only permitted, but commanded not the diuel to deeeiue Achab. So S. Auguſtin. \+it li. 2. q. vlt. ad Simplic\+it*. S. Greg. \+it li. 2. c. 21. Moral\+it*. and other fathers explicate this & like places.\f* goe forth, and doe ſo.
\v 23 Now therfore behold our Lord hath geuen the ſpirite of lying in the mouth of al thy prophetes, that are here, and our Lord hath ſpoken euil agaynſt thee.
\v 24 And Sedecias the ſonne of Chanaana came, and ſmote Micheas on the cheeke, and ſayd: Hath the Spirit of our Lord leaft me, and hath it ſpoken to thee?
\v 25 And Micheas ſayd: Thou shalt ſee in that day, when thou shalt enter into thy chamber, \f e \fr 22.25 \ft VVhen this falſe prophet heard that the king was ſlaine he hidde him ſelfe, fearing the kings ſonnes, that they would kil him. \+it Ioſephus li. 8. c. 14. Antiq\+it*.\f* within the chamber to be hid.
\v 26 And the king of Iſrael ſayd: Take Micheas, and let him tarie with Amon the gouernour of the citie, and with Ioab the ſonne of Amelech,
\v 27 and tel them: Thus ſayth the king: Caſt this man into priſon, and feede him with bread of tribulation, and water of diſtreſſe, til I returne in peace.
\v 28 And Micheas ſayd: If thou returne in peace, our Lord hath not ſpoken in me. And he ſayd: Heare al ye peoples.
\v 29 Therfore the king of Iſrael went vp, and Ioſaphat the king of Iuda into Ramoth Galaad.
\v 30 The king of Iſrael therfore ſayd to Ioſaphat: Take armour, and goe into the battel, and put on thyne owne garmentes. Moreouer the king of Iſrael changed his habite, and went into the battel.
\v 31 And the king of Syria had commanded the princes of his chariotes thirtie and two, ſaying: You shal not fight agaynſt any leſſer, or greater, but agaynſt the king of Iſrael onlie.
\v 32 When therfore the princes of the chariotes had ſeene Ioſaphat, they ſuſpected that he was the king of Iſrael, and making a violent aſſault they fought agaynſt him: & Ioſaphat cried out.
\v 33 And the princes of the chariotes perceiued that he was not the king of Iſrael, and they ceaſed from him.
\v 34 And a certaine man bent his bow, directing the arrow at al aduenture, and \f f \fr 22.34 \ft It happened by chance in reſpect of the archers intention, but otherwiſe by Gods prouidence directing his hand. So Achabs craftie perſwading Ioſaphat to put on his kinglie attyre, \+it (v. 30\+it*.) him ſelf fighting in vulgar armour, ſaued him not from iuſt reuenge,\f* by chance he ſtroke the king of Iſrael betwen the lunges and the ſtomacke. But he ſayd to his cochere: Turne thy hand, and carrie me out of the armie, becauſe I am greuouſely wounded.
\v 35 The battel therfore was fought that day, and the king of Iſrael ſtood in his chariote agaynſt the Syrians, and he died in the euening: & the bloud of the wound ranne into the middes of the chariote,
\v 36 and the herauld ſounded in al the armie before the ſunne ſette, ſaying: Euerie man returne into his citie, and into his countrie.
\v 37 And the king died, and was carried into Samaria: and they buried the king in Samaria,
\v 38 and washed his chariote in the poole of Samaria, and the dogges licked his bloud, and they washed the raynes of the bridle, according to the word of our Lord which he had ſpoken.
\v 39 But the reſt of the wordes of Achab, and al that he did, and the houſe of yuorie, that he built, and of al the cities that he built, are not theſe thinges written in the Booke of the wordes of the daies of the kinges of Iſrael?
\v 40 Achab therfore ſlept with his fathers, and Ochozias his ſonne reigned for him.
\v 41 But Ioſaphat the ſonne of Aſa began to reigne ouer Iuda the fourth yeare of Achab the king of Iſrael.
\v 42 Fiue and thirtie yeares old was he when he began to reigne, and fiue and twentie yeares he reigned in Ieruſalem: the name of his mother was Azuba the daughter of Salai.
\v 43 And he walked in al the way of Aſa his father, and he declined not from it: and he did that which was right in the ſight of our Lord.
\v 44 But yet he tooke not away the excelſes: for as yet the people did ſacrifice, and burnt incenſe in the excelſes.
\v 45 And Ioſaphat had peace with the king of Iſrael.
\v 46 But the reſt of the wordes of Ioſaphat, and his workes, which he did, and his battels, are not theſe thinges written in the Booke of the wordes of the daies of the kinges of Iuda?
\v 47 Yea and the remnant of the effeminate, which remayned in the daies of Aſa his father, he tooke out of the land.
\v 48 Neither was there then a king appoynted in Edom.
\v 49 But king Ioſaphat had made nauies on the ſea, which should ſaile into Ophir for gold: and they could not goe, becauſe they were broken in Aſiongaber.
\v 50 Then ſayd Ochozias the ſonne of Achab to Ioſaphat: Let my ſeruantes goe with thy ſeruantes in the shippes. And Ioſaphat would not.
\v 51 And Ioſaphat ſlept with his fathers, and was buried with them in the Citie of Dauid his father: and Ioram his ſonne reigned for him.
\v 52 And Ochozias the ſonne of Achab began to reigne ouer Iſrael in Samaria, in the ſeuententh yeare of Ioſaphat the king of Iuda, and he reigned ouer Iſrael two yeares.
\v 53 And he did euil in the ſight of our Lord, and walked in the way of his father and his mother, and in the way of Ieroboam the ſonne of Nabat, who made Iſrael to ſinne.
\v 54 He ſerued alſo Baal, and adored him, and prouoked our Lord the God of Iſrael, according to al thinges which his father had done.
